var tafsirData_2 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Mĩm.\"\nThese opening letters belong to the unique group of disconnected letters that appear at the beginning of twenty-nine chapters of the Quran. When recited, they are not blended into a single word; rather, each individual letter is pronounced distinctly by its name.\n\nThe profound wisdom behind starting chapters with these mysterious letters serves as a divine challenge and proof of the Quran's miraculous nature. The Quran was revealed in the clear speech of the people, using the very same alphabet, letters, and vocal sounds that the poets, orators, and literary masters of Arabia used every day. Yet despite their unparalleled eloquence and fierce opposition to the Prophet's message, they were utterly unable to produce even a single short chapter comparable to the Quran in its beauty, depth, moral perfection, and wisdom, as confirmed in the Quran: \"Say, 'If mankind and the jinn gathered in order to produce the like of this Quran, they could not produce the like of it, even if they were to each other assistants'\" (Surah Al-Isra, 17:88).\n\nThese letters arrest the attention of the listener, demonstrating that although the Quran is composed of familiar linguistic elements, its origin, composition, and message are entirely divine. They remind human beings of their intellectual limits before the infinite knowledge of God. While scholars explore their rhetorical impact, they also acknowledge with humility that the definitive, absolute knowledge of their precise purpose remains with God alone, setting a tone of deep humility and active attentiveness before divine revelation.",
  "2": "\"This is the Book! There is no doubt about it\"\nThis opening statement makes an immediate and powerful declaration regarding the absolute authenticity and divine perfection of the Quran. Human authors typically open their writings with modest disclaimers, asking for pardon regarding potential errors, oversights, or limitations in their work. In sharp contrast, the Quran opens with the bold declaration that it contains no flaws, contradictions, uncertainties, or defects whatsoever.\n\nThe assurance that there is no doubt about it guarantees the seeker of truth that every historical narrative, theological reality, moral command, and divine promise contained within these pages is completely certain and trustworthy. The Quran leaves no valid basis for skepticism because its proofs are crystal clear, internally harmonious, and deeply aligned with sound human reason and natural conscience, as God states: \"Had it been from other than Allah, they would surely have found within it much contradiction\" (Surah An-Nisa, 4:82).\n\n\"a guide for those mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nThis explains the primary purpose of the scripture: to guide those who cultivate mindfulness and reverence for God. Mindfulness means constructing a protective shield between oneself and the displeasure of God through eager obedience to His commands and sincere avoidance of His prohibitions.\n\nWhile the Quran offers universal guidance to all humanity, only those who possess an honest heart, an active conscience, and a sincere desire to do what is right will truly benefit from its wisdom, absorb its lessons, and allow it to transform their daily lives. Sincerity and an open heart are the essential keys that unlock the transformative light of divine guidance.",
  "3": "\"who believe in the unseen\"\nThe first core quality highlighted is faith in the unseen. This encompasses all true realities that lie beyond direct sensory observation and physical measurement, yet are known with certainty through authentic divine revelation. This includes faith in God, His angels, the Day of Judgment, Paradise, Hell, the soul, and divine destiny.\n\nBelieving in the unseen is the foundational pillar of faith. If a person restricts their acceptance only to what they can physically touch or see, they reduce reality to mere material objects and remain blind to the spiritual truths of existence. As the Prophet Muhammad explained in the famous narration concerning faith, belief means having unwavering conviction in God, His angels, His revealed scriptures, His messengers, the Last Day, and divine destiny, both its good and its trials (recorded in Sahih Muslim).\n\n\"establish prayer\"\nThe second quality is establishing regular prayer. Establishing prayer means performing it consistently at its proper times, fulfilling its outward conditions and postures, while maintaining inward presence of heart, humility, and sincere devotion. \n\nPrayer is the daily spiritual lifeline connecting the servant directly to God, cleansing the soul from moral corruption and providing strength against worldly distractions. The Prophet Muhammad taught that the prayer is a pillar of the religion, serving as the constant source of spiritual light and tranquility in a believer's life.\n\n\"and donate from what We have provided for them\"\nThe third quality is generous giving, which includes both mandatory charity and voluntary acts of benevolence. The reminder that they spend from what God has provided contains a profound truth: wealth, health, talents, and resources are not purely the result of human cleverness; they are trusts generously bestowed by God.\n\nSharing a portion of that provision with those in need is an act of gratitude and obedience to the true Owner of all wealth. The Prophet Muhammad reassured believers by teaching that giving charity never decreases wealth, but rather purifies it, increases blessings, and softens the human heart (recorded in Sahih Muslim).",
  "4": "\"and who believe in what has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺\"\nThis describes the mindful believers as those who wholeheartedly embrace the final revelation given to the Prophet Muhammad—the Quran and the authentic prophetic guidance. They accept its teachings as the ultimate standard for truth, morality, and justice.\n\n\"and what was revealed before you\"\nA true believer does not embrace the Quran in isolation while rejecting previous divine messages; rather, they believe in the complete lineage of divine revelation sent to earlier messengers, including the Torah revealed to Moses, the Gospel revealed to Jesus, and the Psalms revealed to David, in their original forms.\n\nThis broad, inclusive belief fosters deep respect for the sacred brotherhood of all true prophets sent by God to humanity. The Quran affirms: \"The Messenger has believed in what was revealed to him from his Lord, and so have the believers. All of them have believed in Allah and His angels and His books and His messengers, saying, 'We make no distinction between any of His messengers'\" (Surah Al-Baqarah, 2:285).\n\n\"and have sure faith in the Hereafter\"\nCertainty regarding the life to come means an unwavering, rock-solid conviction that leaves no room for doubt or hesitation. Believing with absolute certainty in the Hereafter fundamentally reshapes a person's priorities, character, and daily decisions.\n\nWhen someone is fully convinced that every word, action, and hidden intention will be weighed before a perfectly just Creator, they become self-motivated to live righteously, treat people with fairness, resist temptation, and endure temporary worldly hardships with patient dignity, knowing that eternal reward awaits the sincere.",
  "5": "\"It is they who are ˹truly˺ guided by their Lord\"\nThis concluding verdict confirms that those who cultivate genuine faith in the unseen, establish sincere prayer, spend generously in good causes, honor all divine revelations, and live with certain conviction in the Hereafter have attained authentic guidance. \n\nGuidance is not simply a personal intellectual triumph or a product of human cleverness; it is a divine light and blessing granted by the Lord to hearts that sincerely seek truth. The phrasing indicates that they are firmly situated upon guidance, as if riding upon a secure, elevated path that carries them safely through the hazards, confusions, and moral pitfalls of this worldly life.\n\n\"and it is they who will be successful\"\nTrue success in the Quranic worldview is not measured by accumulating temporary worldly riches, political dominance, or transient social status. Real success means spiritual safety in this life, salvation from divine punishment, and attaining eternal joy, peace, and honor in Paradise. \n\nThe image of success here is like a farmer who diligently tills the soil, plants good seeds, waters them with patience, protects them from weeds, and ultimately reaps an abundant and joyful harvest. Sincere faith and good deeds yield the greatest harvest in both this life and the life to come.",
  "6": "\"As for those who persist in disbelief\"\nThis passage turns to address those who deliberately and persistently reject divine truth. This does not refer to people who simply have not heard the message or are genuinely searching for truth, but to those who have witnessed clear signs and proofs yet stubbornly refuse to accept them out of arrogance, tribal pride, or corrupt worldly interests.\n\n\"it is the same whether you warn them or not—they will never believe\"\nBecause their rejection is rooted in a hardened, willful decision rather than a lack of information, warnings and logical proofs no longer affect their hearts. \n\nThis brings comfort to the Prophet Muhammad and all who convey the truth, reminding them that their duty is solely to convey the message clearly and compassionately, while the outcome of guidance rests with God alone, as the Quran states: \"Indeed, you do not guide whom you like, but Allah guides whom He wills\" (Surah Al-Qasas, 28:56).",
  "7": "\"Allah has sealed their hearts and their hearing\"\nThis sealing is not an arbitrary act of injustice from God; rather, it is the natural spiritual consequence of a person's repeated, persistent choice to reject truth and embrace falsehood. When someone continuously turns away from light and suppresses their moral conscience, their spiritual capacity to perceive truth gradually becomes numb and unresponsive.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad explained this reality by teaching that when a servant commits a sin, a dark spot appears on their heart. If they abandon the sin, seek forgiveness, and repent, their heart is polished clean; but if they persist and repeat the wrong, the stain spreads until it completely covers the heart (recorded in Sunan al-Tirmidhi).\n\n\"and their sight is covered\"\nTheir spiritual sight becomes obscured, preventing them from recognizing the moral lessons, divine signs, and beauty of truth around them. Though their physical eyes function perfectly, their inner vision is blind to spiritual reality.\n\n\"They will suffer a tremendous punishment\"\nBecause they chose to live in deliberate defiance of truth, misled others, and opposed righteousness, they will face full divine justice and a severe punishment in the Hereafter proportional to their persistent rejection.",
  "8": "\"And there are some who say, “We believe in Allah and the Last Day,”\"\nThis verse introduces the third category of people: the hypocrites. Unlike open believers or open deniers, hypocrites profess faith outwardly with their tongues, claiming belief in God and the Day of Judgment, while concealing skepticism, malice, and rejection in their hearts.\n\n\"yet they are not ˹true˺ believers\"\nGod exposes the emptiness of their claims. True faith is not merely a verbal slogan or a social costume worn for personal convenience; it requires genuine conviction in the heart and righteous actions that prove one's sincerity. Merely saying the right words without internal belief holds no value in the sight of God.",
  "9": "\"They seek to deceive Allah and the believers\"\nHypocrites attempt to play a dangerous double game. By outwardly aligning with the believers, they seek to enjoy social acceptance, safety, and worldly advantages, while privately maintaining their ties with deniers and opposing the truth.\n\n\"yet they only deceive themselves, but they fail to perceive it\"\nIt is impossible to deceive God, who knows every hidden thought, secret intention, and whisper of the heart. In reality, their deception harms only themselves. They deprive their own souls of authentic faith, peace, and eternal salvation, while digging a spiritual pit of guilt and hypocrisy from which they cannot easily escape.",
  "10": "\"There is sickness in their hearts\"\nThe sickness mentioned here is the spiritual disease of doubt, malice, arrogance, jealousy, and insincerity. When the human heart is infected with these moral illnesses, its ability to love truth and embrace goodness is severely weakened.\n\n\"and Allah ˹only˺ lets their sickness increase\"\nThis is a universal spiritual law: when a person chooses deception and nurtures ill intentions, every new sign of truth and every righteous victory of the believers only increases their bitterness, envy, and confusion. Their continued wrongdoing deepens their moral blindness.\n\n\"They will suffer a painful punishment for their lies\"\nLiving a life of continuous lying, betrayal, and false pretenses leads to severe consequences. In the Hereafter, they will face a painful punishment specifically due to their persistent dishonesty and betrayal of trust.",
  "11": "\"When they are told, “Do not spread corruption in the land,”\"\nCorruption includes promoting moral decay, creating discord among people, undermining social trust, and spreading doubt against divine guidance. When sincere advisors caution the hypocrites against their destructive behavior, they refuse to listen.\n\n\"they reply, “We are only peace-makers!”\"\nThis reveals a hallmark of hypocrisy: self-delusion. People who cause immense social and spiritual damage often justify their harmful actions under the banner of reform, diplomacy, modernization, or peace-making. They reframe wrongdoing as wisdom and compromise as progress.",
  "12": "\"Indeed, it is they who are the corruptors\"\nGod delivers an absolute verdict, cutting through their deceptive rhetoric and false claims. The true measure of reform is alignment with divine truth and justice, not self-serving political maneuvers.\n\n\"but they fail to perceive it\"\nPersistent hypocrisy blinds the moral compass. When a person spends their life deceiving others, they eventually deceive themselves, losing the ability to distinguish between genuine benefit and real harm.",
  "13": "\"And when they are told, “Believe as others believe,”\"\nThe hypocrites are invited to embrace sincere, wholehearted faith, just like the devoted companions who embraced truth with pure conviction, sacrifice, and loyalty.\n\n\"they reply, “Will we believe as the fools believe?”\"\nDriven by intellectual pride and arrogance, the hypocrites look down on sincere believers, viewing their devotion and willingness to sacrifice worldly comforts for God's sake as foolishness, naivety, or simple-mindedness.\n\n\"Indeed, it is they who are fools, but they do not know\"\nThe Quran reverses their judgment: true foolishness is trading eternal salvation and peace of mind for temporary worldly prestige and a life of deception. Sincere believers who secure eternal joy are the truly wise, while the arrogant hypocrites remain blind to their own foolish trade.",
  "14": "\"When they meet the believers they say, “We believe.”\"\nThis exposes their opportunistic, two-faced character. In the presence of believers, they smile, profess faith, and claim solidarity to secure worldly benefits, social standing, and protection.\n\n\"But when alone with their evil associates they say, “We are definitely with you; we were only mocking.”\"\nBehind closed doors and away from public scrutiny, they reveal their true allegiance to the enemies of truth. They mock the sincere believers, assuring their wicked companions that their outward profession of faith was merely a tactical charade and a joke.",
  "15": "\"Allah will throw their mockery back at them\"\nMockery directed against divine truth and sincere believers does not go unanswered. God repays their cynical ridicule with complete justice, exposing their deceit in this world and holding them fully accountable on the Day of Judgment.\n\n\"leaving them to continue wandering blindly in their defiance\"\nAs a consequence of their arrogance and persistent mockery, God leaves them to wander aimlessly in their confusion and spiritual blindness, unable to find peace, clarity, or true purpose in life.",
  "16": "\"They are the ones who trade guidance for misguidance\"\nThe Quran uses the vivid analogy of commerce to describe their tragic choice. They held the opportunity to embrace guidance, light, and salvation, yet they deliberately traded it away for doubt, hypocrisy, and spiritual darkness.\n\n\"But this trade is profitless, and they are not ˹rightly˺ guided\"\nIn any business transaction, a wise person trades something of lesser value for something greater. The hypocrites made the worst possible trade: they sacrificed eternal Paradise and true honor for temporary worldly comfort and deceit, ending in total spiritual bankruptcy.",
  "17": "\"Their example is that of someone who kindles a fire\"\nTo illustrate the spiritual condition of the hypocrites, God presents a vivid parable: a person wandering in pitch darkness who lights a fire to illuminate their surroundings and find warmth and security.\n\n\"but when it lights up all around them, Allah takes away their light, leaving them in complete darkness—unable to see\"\nAs soon as the fire illuminates the environment, the light is suddenly extinguished, plunging them back into terrifying darkness. This represents the hypocrite who outwardly associates with the light of revelation and community of faith, gaining brief worldly safety and insight, but because they never internalized faith in their hearts, the light is stripped away, leaving their souls in utter spiritual darkness and despair.",
  "18": "\"They are ˹wilfully˺ deaf, dumb, and blind\"\nAlthough they possess physical hearing, speech, and eyesight, they refuse to use these faculties for their true purpose. They turn a deaf ear to divine truth, refuse to speak words of sincere faith and justice, and close their eyes to the clear moral signs around them.\n\n\"so they will never return ˹to the Right Path˺\"\nBecause they have deliberately shut down all channels of guidance, they become trapped in their own rebellion, cutting themselves off from the path of repentance and spiritual return unless they sincerely change their ways.",
  "19": "\"Or ˹those caught in˺ a rainstorm from the sky with darkness, thunder, and lightning\"\nThis presents a second parable illustrating another aspect of hypocrisy. It depicts travelers caught in a fierce nocturnal thunderstorm, where the sky is filled with thick darkness, terrifying crashes of thunder, and blinding flashes of lightning. The rain represents the Quran with its life-giving guidance, while the thunder and lightning represent its solemn warnings, obligations, and trials.\n\n\"They press their fingers into their ears at the sound of every thunder-clap for fear of death\"\nThe hypocrites are terrified by the strict moral demands, commands of sacrifice, and warnings of punishment in revelation. Instead of embracing the life-giving rain, they desperately try to block out the warnings, hoping to escape accountability.\n\n\"And Allah encompasses the disbelievers ˹by His might˺\"\nNo matter how hard they try to avoid the truth or shield themselves from divine reality, God's limitless power, knowledge, and justice surround them completely. No soul can ever escape His dominion.",
  "20": "\"It is as if the lightning were about to snatch away their sight\"\nThe dazzling clarity of the Quran's proofs and moral truths is so overwhelming that it almost blinds the hypocrites, exposing the weakness and instability of their false convictions.\n\n\"Whenever lightning strikes, they walk in its light, but when darkness covers them, they stand still\"\nThis depicts their conditional, opportunistic relationship with faith. When practicing faith brings immediate worldly ease, victories, or personal benefit, they move forward cheerfully. But when faith demands patience, sacrifice, or perseverance through hardship, they freeze in hesitation and doubt.\n\n\"Had Allah willed, He could have taken away their hearing and sight. Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything\"\nThis reminds them that their faculties exist solely by God's grace. If He willed, He could instantly strip away their physical hearing and sight just as they abandoned their spiritual senses. It serves as a merciful warning of God's absolute power, urging them to rectify their hearts before it is too late.",
  "21": "\"O humanity! Worship your Lord, Who created you and those before you\"\nThis is the very first direct command in the Quran addressed to all of humankind. God calls upon every human being, without exception, to direct their devotion, gratitude, and worship exclusively to their Creator. The logical proof presented is simple and undeniable: God alone brought you into existence from nothingness, and He created all previous generations of humanity. Since no idol, human leader, or created force had any share in giving you life, none of them deserve a share in your worship.\n\n\"so that you may become mindful ˹of Him˺\"\nThis defines the ultimate purpose and benefit of worship. God does not need our prayers or devotion; rather, worship transforms human character. Sincere worship instills inner consciousness, moral discipline, and mindfulness of God, protecting a person from wrongdoing and guiding them to live with justice and compassion.",
  "22": "\"˹He is the One˺ Who has made the earth a place of settlement for you and the sky a canopy\"\nGod directs our attention to the magnificent harmony of the physical world. He designed the earth as a stable, comfortable, and hospitable habitat, equipped with all the conditions necessary for human life to thrive. Above it, He constructed the sky as a protective canopy and atmosphere, shielding the earth from cosmic hazards and maintaining the climate needed for living creatures.\n\n\"and sends down rain from the sky, causing fruits to grow as a provision for you\"\nGod highlights the cycle of life: pure water descending from clouds to revive dry soil and bring forth an astonishing variety of fruits, grains, and nourishment for human sustenance. As the Quran reminds us elsewhere: \"Have you seen the water that you drink? Is it you who brought it down from the clouds, or is it We who bring it down?\" (Surah Al-Waqi'ah, 56:68-69).\n\n\"So do not knowingly set up equals to Allah ˹in worship˺\"\nSince God alone created the universe and continuously provides every blessing, it is deeply unjust and irrational to direct worship, ultimate reliance, or divine attributes to created beings, idols, or worldly powers while knowing in your heart that God is the sole Creator.",
  "23": "\"And if you are in doubt about what We have revealed to Our servant\"\nThis issues a profound and eternal challenge to anyone who questions the divine origin of the Quran revealed to the Prophet Muhammad. If skeptics claim that the Quran is merely the product of human imagination or poetic skill, they are presented with an open, objective test.\n\n\"then produce a sûrah like it and call your helpers other than Allah, if what you say is true\"\nGod challenges all deniers across all times and places to produce even a single short chapter comparable to the Quran in its eloquence, wisdom, moral guidance, and linguistic perfection. They are invited to summon all their greatest scholars, poets, philosophers, and allies to assist them. This challenge stands unfulfilled throughout history, serving as an enduring proof that the Quran is the miraculous word of the Creator.",
  "24": "\"But if you are unable to do so—and you will never be able to do so\"\nThe Quran makes a bold and confident prophecy: humanity will never possess the ability to produce anything comparable to divine speech. Despite centuries of intense literary competition and hostility from critics, this challenge remains completely unmet.\n\n\"then fear the Fire fuelled with people and stones, which is prepared for the disbelievers\"\nHaving established the miraculous truth of revelation, God warns against stubborn arrogance. Persistently denying clear truth leads to severe consequences in the Hereafter. The Fire is described as being fueled by human wrongdoers and stones, highlighting its terrifying intensity and serving as an urgent call for people to humble their hearts and embrace truth.",
  "25": "\"Give good news ˹O Prophet˺ to those who believe and do good that they will have Gardens under which rivers flow\"\nIn contrast to the warning given to persistent deniers, God commands the Prophet to deliver joyful news to those who unite sincere faith with righteous action. True faith always expresses itself through moral integrity, compassion, and good deeds. Their reward is eternal Paradise, depicted as lush, serene gardens with flowing rivers.\n\n\"Whenever provided with fruit, they will say, “This is what we were given before,” for they will be served fruit that looks similar ˹but tastes different˺\"\nThe fruits of Paradise are familiar in appearance to what people enjoyed on earth, creating a welcoming sense of recognition, yet each bite delivers an entirely new, unimaginably exquisite flavor and delight, fulfilling the prophetic teaching that Paradise contains what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has ever conceived (recorded in Sahih Bukhari and Sahih Muslim).\n\n\"They will have pure spouses, and they will be there forever\"\nBelievers will enjoy companionship entirely purified from all physical flaws, moral blemishes, jealousy, and anger. Above all, their joy is made complete by the assurance of eternity: they will never face death, illness, loss, or expulsion from this eternal bliss.",
  "26": "\"Surely Allah does not shy away from using the parable of a mosquito or what is even smaller\"\nWhen critics mocked the Quran for mentioning small creatures like spiders, flies, and ants in its parables, God clarified that truth is not measured by physical size. Even the tiniest creature, such as a mosquito, is a masterpiece of complex biological design, intricate anatomy, and divine wisdom.\n\n\"As for the believers, they know that it is the truth from their Lord\"\nSincere believers reflect on the deeper lesson behind the parable, recognizing that God uses simple, accessible examples from nature to illuminate profound spiritual truths about human weakness, pride, and dependence on God.\n\n\"And as for the disbelievers, they argue, “What does Allah mean by such a parable?”\"\nArrogant deniers focus superficially on the smallness of the creature, using it as an excuse to argue, mock, and dismiss the divine message.\n\n\"Through this ˹test˺, He leaves many to stray, and guides many. And He leaves none to stray except the rebellious\"\nParables act as a moral mirror: those who approach them with humility and open minds gain deeper guidance, while those filled with arrogance and rebellion use them to justify their skepticism. God never leads anyone astray unjustly; rather, spiritual deviation is the direct result of a person's own stubborn rebellion.",
  "27": "\"those who violate Allah’s covenant after it has been affirmed\"\nThis describes the core traits of the rebellious. The first is breaking their sacred covenants with God—both the universal natural covenant inscribed in the human conscience to recognize the Creator, and the specific commitments made to uphold divine guidance and moral truth.\n\n\"break whatever ˹ties˺ Allah has ordered to be maintained\"\nThe second trait is severing vital bonds, which includes cutting ties of kinship, breaking social trust, abandoning compassion toward family and neighbors, and tearing apart community unity. The Prophet Muhammad warned that one who severs family ties will not enter Paradise peacefully (recorded in Sahih Bukhari and Sahih Muslim).\n\n\"and spread corruption in the land. It is they who are the ˹true˺ losers\"\nThe third trait is promoting injustice, moral decay, and discord. Despite any temporary worldly gains they might achieve, they are the ultimate losers because they have destroyed their own integrity, forfeited inner peace, and traded eternal salvation for fleeting worldly pleasures.",
  "28": "\"How can you deny Allah? You were lifeless and He gave you life\"\nGod addresses humanity with a deeply moving question that appeals to fundamental human reason and personal history. Before being conceived and born, every human being was non-existent and lifeless in the earth. God alone gave you the gift of life, shaping your body, breathing the soul into you, and granting you consciousness and senses.\n\n\"then He will cause you to die and again bring you to life\"\nJust as you could not prevent your own creation, you cannot prevent your ultimate physical death. And the One who created you from nothing the first time is fully capable of resurrecting you a second time for final accountability, as the Quran states: \"As We began the first creation, We will repeat it\" (Surah Al-Anbiya, 21:104).\n\n\"and then to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned\"\nEvery soul will ultimately stand before its Creator to review its intentions and deeds. Remembering this four-stage journey—non-existence, life, death, and resurrection—shatters arrogance and instills a sense of moral purpose.",
  "29": "\"He is the One Who created everything in the earth for you\"\nGod created all the resources, minerals, oceans, plants, and animals on this planet for human benefit, sustenance, and responsible stewardship. This establishes that the natural world is a divine blessing and trust to be treated with gratitude, wisdom, and care rather than reckless exploitation.\n\n\"Then He turned towards the heaven, forming it into seven heavens. And He has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things\"\nGod's supreme power organized the cosmos above into seven distinct realms with absolute precision and harmony. His infinite knowledge encompasses every atom, movement, and secret across all realms of existence, assuring humanity that the Lord who oversees this vast universe is fully aware of every human need and prayer.",
  "30": "\"˹Remember˺ when your Lord said to the angels, “I am going to place a successive ˹human˺ authority on earth.”\"\nGod announced to the angels His divine plan to create humanity and appoint them as stewards on earth, entrusted with moral agency, intellect, free will, and the responsibility to establish justice and worship.\n\n\"They asked ˹Allah˺, “Will You place in it someone who will spread corruption there and shed blood while we glorify Your praises and proclaim Your holiness?”\"\nThe angels' question was not voiced in protest or jealousy, but out of an earnest desire to understand divine wisdom. Having observed that beings with free will could abuse their choices to commit violence and injustice, the angels highlighted their own constant, pure worship of God.\n\n\"Allah responded, “I know what you do not know.”\"\nGod reassured the angels that His infinite wisdom envisioned righteous prophets, steadfast truth-tellers, compassionate leaders, and sincere worshippers among humanity who would freely choose righteousness and bring light, justice, and goodness to the world.",
  "31": "\"He taught Adam the names of all things\"\nTo demonstrate the unique honor and capacity granted to humanity, God endowed Adam with intellect, language, and the ability to conceptualize, name, and understand the nature and functions of things around him. Knowledge and language are the foundational gifts that enable human stewardship.\n\n\"then He presented them to the angels and said, “Tell Me the names of these, if what you say is true?”\"\nGod presented these created things before the angels and asked them to name them, demonstrating that the angels, despite their purity and constant worship, lacked the specialized intellectual capacity and learning ability granted to human beings.",
  "32": "\"They replied, “Glory be to You! We have no knowledge except what You have taught us\"\nThe angels responded with immediate humility, reverence, and complete submission. They acknowledged their own limitations, glorifying God and admitting that created beings possess only the knowledge that God chooses to grant them.\n\n\"You are truly the All-Knowing, All-Wise\"\nThey affirmed God's absolute perfection in knowledge and wisdom, demonstrating that true spiritual excellence begins with intellectual humility and recognizing that all wisdom belongs to the Creator.",
  "33": "\"Allah said, “O Adam! Inform them of their names.” Then when Adam did\"\nBy divine instruction, Adam named the objects and explained their purposes, demonstrating the unique intellectual and communicative abilities bestowed upon him by God.\n\n\"Allah said, “Did I not tell you that I know the secrets of the heavens and the earth, and I know what you reveal and what you conceal?”\"\nGod reminded the angels of His all-encompassing knowledge, which penetrates every unseen realm of the cosmos as well as every open thought and hidden feeling of all creatures. This established the profound divine wisdom behind creating humanity.",
  "34": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We said to the angels, “Prostrate before Adam,” so they all did\"\nGod commanded the angels to prostrate before Adam as an act of honor and respect to human knowledge and divine creation, and as an act of worship to God who commanded it. The angels obeyed immediately without hesitation.\n\n\"but not Iblîs, who refused and acted arrogantly, becoming unfaithful\"\nIblis (Satan), who was among their ranks, refused to obey out of pride and jealousy, claiming superiority because he was made of fire while Adam was made of clay. This reveals that arrogance is the root of spiritual rebellion, blinding a person from recognizing truth and submitting to divine commands.",
  "35": "\"We cautioned, “O Adam! Live with your wife in Paradise and eat as freely as you please\"\nGod welcomed Adam and his wife, Eve, into Paradise, granting them complete freedom to enjoy its abundant delights, peace, and blessings without restriction.\n\n\"but do not approach this tree, or else you will be wrongdoers\"\nTo test their obedience and self-discipline, God placed a single boundary: they were forbidden from approaching one specific tree. This teaches that true moral freedom exists within boundaries, and that self-restraint in honoring divine limits is the foundation of spiritual safety.",
  "36": "\"But Satan deceived them—leading to their fall from the ˹blissful˺ state they were in\"\nSatan whispered deceitful promises of eternal life and angelic status, exploiting their human desires until they slipped and ate from the forbidden tree, causing them to lose their state of pure bliss.\n\n\"and We said, “Descend from the heavens ˹to the earth˺ as enemies to each other. You will find in the earth a residence and provision for your appointed stay.”\"\nGod decreed that humanity and Satan would descend to earth, where human history would unfold as a continuous spiritual test between righteousness and demonic deception, with the earth serving as a temporary home and dwelling place until the Day of Judgment.",
  "37": "\"Then Adam was inspired with words ˹of prayer˺ by his Lord, so He accepted his repentance\"\nUnlike Satan, who justified his rebellion with stubborn pride, Adam and Eve immediately felt deep remorse. In His immense mercy, God taught Adam specific words of prayer to seek forgiveness, recorded in the Quran: \"Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us, we will surely be among the losers\" (Surah Al-A'raf, 7:23).\n\n\"Surely He is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful\"\nGod turned to Adam with boundless compassion and accepted his sincere repentance. This establishes that human beings are prone to mistakes, but the door of sincere repentance is always wide open to those who humble themselves and return to God.",
  "38": "\"We said, “Descend all of you! Then when guidance comes to you from Me\"\nAs humanity began its worldly journey on earth, God promised that He would not leave mankind to wander in moral confusion. He would continuously send divine guidance through prophets, messengers, and revealed scriptures.\n\n\"whoever follows it, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve\"\nThis provides the eternal guarantee for human happiness: those who embrace divine guidance will be free from paralyzing fear regarding the future and free from devastating grief over the past. Faith grants serenity in this world and eternal peace in the Hereafter.",
  "39": "\"But those who disbelieve and deny Our signs will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever\"\nIn contrast to those who follow guidance, those who deliberately reject truth, deny divine signs, and persist in rebellion choose their own spiritual ruin. Their eternal dwelling will be the Fire, receiving the just recompense for their persistent rejection of truth.",
  "40": "\"O children of Israel! Remember My favours upon you\"\nThe address turns to the Children of Israel (the descendants of Prophet Jacob), reminding them of the countless historical blessings, miracles, and protections God granted their ancestors, including liberation from tyranny, heavenly provisions, and prophetic leadership.\n\n\"Fulfil your covenant and I will fulfil Mine, and stand in awe of Me ˹alone˺\"\nGod calls upon them to honor their solemn covenant: to believe in the truth, uphold moral integrity, and embrace the final Prophet sent as a mercy to mankind. In return, God promises to fulfill His covenant of divine favor and eternal reward, urging them to revere God alone rather than fearing worldly pressures or social disapproval.",
  "41": "\"Believe in My revelations which confirm your Scriptures\"\nGod calls upon the scholars and leaders among the Children of Israel to accept the Quran, which confirms the core divine truths, prophecies, and monotheistic principles already present in the original Torah revealed to Moses.\n\n\"Do not be the first to deny them or trade them for a fleeting gain\"\nThey are cautioned against leading the opposition out of fear of losing social status, religious authority, or material donations. Trading away eternal truth for temporary worldly advantages is a tragic bargain that brings only spiritual bankruptcy.\n\n\"And be mindful of Me\"\nGod urges them to cultivate true reverence and awe of their Creator, prioritizing divine approval over human approval and worldly wealth.",
  "42": "\"Do not mix truth with falsehood\"\nThis forbids the deceitful practice of blending authentic divine revelation with human fabrications, personal opinions, or distorted interpretations to confuse sincere seekers of truth.\n\n\"or hide the truth knowingly\"\nThis directly rebukes the act of concealing clear divine prophecies, moral laws, and descriptions of the final Prophet that were clearly preserved in their scriptures, committing a grave injustice against both themselves and society.",
  "43": "\"Establish prayer, pay alms-tax\"\nThis command establishes the two primary pillars of righteous life: maintaining sincere, punctual prayer to strengthen one's personal connection with God, and paying the purifying charity to support the poor, vulnerable, and needy.\n\n\"and bow down with those who bow down\"\nThis encourages collective worship, unity, and humble fellowship with the community of believers, breaking down personal arrogance and fostering social solidarity before the Creator.",
  "44": "\"Do you preach righteousness and fail to practice it yourselves, although you read the Scripture?\"\nThis verse delivers a powerful rebuke against religious hypocrisy: instructing others to do good, act with integrity, and obey God, while neglecting those very duties in one's own private and public life.\n\n\"Do you not understand?\"\nGod appeals to their basic intelligence and conscience. True knowledge must lead to moral transformation. The Prophet Muhammad warned that a person who commands good while failing to practice it, and forbids evil while committing it, will face severe humiliation and accounting on the Day of Judgment (recorded in Sahih Bukhari and Sahih Muslim).",
  "45": "\"And seek help through patience and prayer\"\nGod provides humanity with two essential spiritual anchors for facing life's trials: patient perseverance and regular prayer. Patience empowers a person to endure hardship, restrain impulses, and stay steadfast, while prayer opens the door to divine assistance, peace, and inner strength.\n\n\"Indeed, it is a burden except for the humble\"\nMaintaining regular prayer and steadfast patience feels heavy, difficult, and burdensome to the arrogant and heedless, but it is sweet, comforting, and light for those whose hearts are filled with reverence, awe, and genuine submission to God.",
  "46": "\"those who are certain that they will meet their Lord\"\nThis defines the secret behind true humility and devotion: an unwavering conviction that every individual will eventually stand before their Creator. When a person lives with this realization, worship becomes deeply meaningful and worldly hardships become temporary and easy to bear.\n\n\"and to Him they will return\"\nKnowing that all deeds, intentions, and life outcomes ultimately return to God provides an unshakable moral compass, inspiring honesty, kindness, and continuous preparation for the Hereafter.",
  "47": "\"O Children of Israel! Remember ˹all˺ the favours I granted you\"\nGod calls upon them once again to reflect on the immense historical blessings, divine miracles, and heavenly gifts bestowed upon their ancestors throughout history as a foundation for gratitude and obedience.\n\n\"and how I honoured you above the others\"\nThis historical distinction was granted to their ancestors during their era because they were entrusted with divine scripture and prophetic leadership. It was a sacred moral trust requiring continuous righteousness and humility, not an unconditional genetic entitlement.",
  "48": "\"Guard yourselves against the Day on which no soul will be of help to another\"\nGod warns against false complacency on the Day of Judgment. On that Day, all worldly lineage, family influence, and tribal status will vanish; every person will be held individually accountable for their own beliefs and actions.\n\n\"No intercession will be accepted, no ransom taken, and no help will be given\"\nThis systematically dismantles four false hopes: unauthorized intercession will not save a persistent denier, no amount of earthly wealth could ever be offered as ransom, and no external army or power can protect against divine justice. Salvation is attained solely through sincere faith and righteous deeds.",
  "49": "\"˹Remember˺ how We delivered you from the people of Pharaoh, who afflicted you with dreadful torment\"\nGod reminds them of their miraculous rescue from brutal slavery in Egypt, where Pharaoh subjected their ancestors to exhausting labor, humiliation, and severe oppression.\n\n\"slaughtering your sons and keeping your women. That was a severe test from your Lord\"\nPharaoh's state policy involved murdering newborn male infants while keeping the females for servitude. Surpassing this horrific ordeal through divine deliverance was a profound historical trial and monumental blessing that demanded perpetual gratitude.",
  "50": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We parted the sea, rescued you\"\nGod highlights the miraculous event at the Red Sea, where the waters split to provide a safe, dry passage for Moses and the Israelites, escaping the pursuing Egyptian army.\n\n\"and drowned Pharaoh’s people before your very eyes\"\nThe sea collapsed upon Pharaoh and his soldiers while the Israelites watched safely from the shore, proving that tyrannical power is completely powerless when confronted with divine justice and deliverance.",
  "51": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We appointed forty nights for Moses\"\nMoses went to Mount Sinai for forty nights of spiritual seclusion and communion to receive the divine tablets of the Torah.\n\n\"then you worshipped the calf in his absence, acting wrongfully\"\nIn his brief absence, they succumbed to the temptation of crafting and worshipping a golden calf, committing a grave act of idolatry immediately after witnessing monumental miracles with their own eyes.",
  "52": "\"Even then We ˹still˺ forgave you so perhaps you would be grateful\"\nDespite their shocking betrayal and idolatry, God showed immense forbearance and accepted their repentance when they turned back, demonstrating His boundless mercy and calling them to live with deep thankfulness.",
  "53": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We gave Moses the Scripture—the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺\"\nGod bestowed the Torah upon Moses as both a sacred scripture and an objective criterion to clearly distinguish truth from falsehood, justice from tyranny, and lawful from forbidden.\n\n\"that perhaps you would be ˹rightly˺ guided\"\nThe primary purpose of divine revelation is to illuminate the human mind and guide the community toward moral uprightness, peace, and spiritual salvation.",
  "54": "\"And ˹remember˺ when Moses said to his people, “O my people! Surely you have wronged yourselves by worshipping the calf\"\nMoses confronted his community with moral clarity, reminding them of the severe spiritual harm they brought upon their own souls through idolatry.\n\n\"so turn in repentance to your Creator and execute ˹the calf-worshippers among˺ yourselves. That is best for you in the sight of your Creator.”\"\nTo cleanse the community of treason against the covenant, strict judicial repentance was mandated for those who led the worship of the idol.\n\n\"Then He accepted your repentance. Surely He is the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful\"\nWhen they submitted to divine command with sincere remorse, God accepted their repentance, confirming that His mercy welcomes all who truly turn back to Him.",
  "55": "\"And ˹remember˺ when you said, “O Moses! We will never believe you until we see Allah with our own eyes,”\"\nWhen seventy chosen leaders accompanied Moses to the mountain, their arrogance led them to demand a direct physical vision of God before they would submit to faith.\n\n\"so a thunderbolt struck you while you were looking on\"\nBecause of their obstinate defiance and disrespectful demand, a terrifying thunderbolt seized them, rendering them lifeless on the spot as a dramatic demonstration of human frailty before the majesty of God.",
  "56": "\"Then We brought you back to life after your death\"\nWhen Moses earnestly pleaded and supplicated for divine mercy on behalf of his people, God revived them from physical death, granting them a second chance at life.\n\n\"so that perhaps you would be grateful\"\nExperiencing resurrection in this world was an extraordinary sign of divine power, meant to instill enduring gratitude, humility, and steadfast faith in their hearts.",
  "57": "\"And ˹remember when˺ We shaded you with clouds and sent down to you manna and quails\"\nDuring their forty years of wandering in the scorching desert of Sinai, God provided protective cloud cover from the blazing sun, and miraculously provided sweet manna and succulent quails for their daily sustenance.\n\n\"˹saying˺, “Eat from the good things We have provided for you.” The evildoers ˹certainly˺ did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves\"\nGod permitted them to enjoy pure, delicious food with only the simplest conditions. When they grew ungrateful and greedy, their rebellion caused zero harm to God; rather, it harmed only their own spiritual and physical well-being.",
  "58": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We said, “Enter this city and eat freely from wherever you please\"\nGod commanded them to enter the holy city, promising abundant provision, peace, and security.\n\n\"enter the gate with humility, saying, ‘Absolve us.’ We will forgive your sins and multiply the reward for the good-doers\"\nThey were instructed to enter with outward physical humility (bowing) and inward spiritual repentance (asking for forgiveness), with God promising to forgive their past shortcomings and abundantly reward those who excel in goodness.",
  "59": "\"But the wrongdoers changed the words they were commanded to say\"\nInstead of entering with reverence and asking for forgiveness, the corrupt among them mocked the divine command, entering on their backs and twisting the sacred words into crude mockery and demands for wheat.\n\n\"So We sent down a punishment from the heavens upon them for their rebelliousness\"\nBecause of their deliberate mockery, disrespect, and persistent rebellion against divine guidance, a severe plague descended upon them as just retribution.",
  "60": "\"And ˹remember˺ when Moses prayed for water for his people, We said, “Strike the rock with your staff.”\"\nWhen his people were desperate for water in the arid desert, Moses turned to God in sincere prayer. God commanded him to strike a rock with his staff.\n\n\"Then twelve springs gushed out, ˹and˺ each tribe knew its drinking place\"\nInstantly, twelve distinct freshwater springs gushed forth from the rock, providing an organized, designated water supply for each of the twelve tribes of Israel, preventing conflict and chaos.\n\n\"˹We then said,˺ “Eat and drink of Allah’s provisions, and do not go about spreading corruption in the land.”\"\nGod invited them to enjoy His miraculous provisions with gratitude, while strictly warning them against ingratitude, lawlessness, and spreading corruption in society.",
  "61": "\"And ˹remember˺ when you said, “O Moses! We cannot endure the same meal ˹every day˺. So ˹just˺ call upon your Lord on our behalf, He will bring forth for us some of what the earth produces of herbs, cucumbers, garlic, lentils, and onions.”\"\nDespite being provided with heavenly, pure manna and quails without physical toil, their ingratitude and restlessness led them to complain and demand ordinary agricultural crops like cucumbers, lentils, and onions.\n\n\"Moses scolded ˹them˺, “Do you exchange what is better for what is worse? ˹You can˺ go down to any village and you will find what you have asked for.”\"\nMoses rebuked their shallow discontent, pointing out the absurdity of trading pure, miraculous divine gifts for common produce that could be acquired in any ordinary town.\n\n\"They were stricken with disgrace and misery, and they invited the displeasure of Allah for rejecting Allah’s signs and unjustly killing the prophets. This is ˹a fair reward˺ for their disobedience and violations\"\nTheir spiritual decline culminated in rejecting divine signs, disobeying God's commandments, and even persecuting and murdering noble prophets who called them to justice. This invited profound moral humiliation and divine displeasure as just recompense for their persistent violations.",
  "62": "\"Indeed, the believers, Jews, Christians, and Sabians—whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good will have their reward with their Lord\"\nThis establishes the universal divine rule regarding salvation: throughout all historical eras, anyone among these communities who sincerely believed in the One True God, had certain faith in the Day of Judgment, and followed the authentic prophetic guidance of their era with righteous action will receive their complete reward with their Lord.\n\n\"And there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve\"\nSincere faith coupled with righteous deeds removes all dread of the future and regret over the past, guaranteeing eternal peace, serenity, and joy in Paradise.",
  "63": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We took a covenant from you and raised the mountain above you\"\nGod highlights the solemn covenant taken at Mount Sinai, where the mountain was miraculously suspended above them as an awe-inspiring sign of divine majesty and the seriousness of their pledge.\n\n\"˹saying˺, “Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful ˹of Allah˺.”\"\nThey were commanded to embrace the Torah with determination, conviction, and diligence, constantly remembering its moral teachings to cultivate deep mindfulness and protection from wrongdoing.",
  "64": "\"Yet you turned away afterwards\"\nDespite witnessing extraordinary signs and making solemn oaths before God, they broke their commitments and turned away from divine commands.\n\n\"Had it not been for Allah’s grace and mercy upon you, you would have certainly been of the losers\"\nGod's continuous forbearance, delayed punishment, and acceptance of repentance preserved them from total destruction, giving them repeated opportunities to return to righteousness.",
  "65": "\"You are already aware of those of you who broke the Sabbath\"\nGod refers to a well-known historical event involving a coastal community commanded to dedicate Saturday (the Sabbath) exclusively to worship and rest from fishing.\n\n\"We said to them, “Be disgraced apes!”\"\nTo circumvent the divine prohibition, they devised deceitful tricks, setting nets on Friday and collecting fish on Sunday. Because of their arrogant mockery of divine law and deceitful manipulation, God transformed their physical form and nature into humiliated apes as a dramatic punishment.",
  "66": "\"So We made their fate an example to present and future generations\"\nGod made their dramatic downfall an enduring historical warning and reminder for their contemporaries and all coming generations against the dangers of playing deceitful games with divine commandments.\n\n\"and a lesson to the God-fearing\"\nWhile the arrogant ignore historical warnings, those who possess genuine reverence and fear of God absorb the profound lesson, ensuring that they respect sacred boundaries with sincerity and humility.",
  "67": "\"And ˹remember˺ when Moses said to his people, “Allah commands you to sacrifice a cow.”\"\nWhen a mysterious murder occurred among the Israelites and the perpetrator was hidden, God commanded them through Moses to slaughter a cow to resolve the dispute and reveal the killer.\n\n\"They replied, “Are you mocking us?” Moses responded, “I seek refuge in Allah from acting foolishly!”\"\nInstead of obeying with simple submission, they treated the command with cynicism, suspecting ridicule. Moses immediately sought God's protection from behaving foolishly or attributing mockery to divine revelation.",
  "68": "\"They said, “Call upon your Lord to clarify for us what type ˹of cow˺ it should be!”\"\nRather than slaughtering any ordinary cow to fulfill the basic command, their stubbornness led them to ask unnecessary, pedantic questions.\n\n\"He replied, “Allah says, ‘The cow should neither be old nor young but in between. So do as you are commanded!’”\"\nBecause of their probing, God narrowed the criteria, specifying an adult cow of prime age, neither worn out by old age nor too young, urging them once again to fulfill the command without further delay.",
  "69": "\"They said, “Call upon your Lord to specify for us its colour.”\"\nInstead of proceeding, they continued to overcomplicate the matter by demanding the exact color of the animal.\n\n\"He replied, “Allah says, ‘It should be a bright yellow cow—pleasant to see.’”\"\nGod further restricted the description to a rare, vibrant, pure golden-yellow cow whose striking beauty delighted all who looked upon it, making their search considerably more difficult.",
  "70": "\"Again they said, “Call upon your Lord so that He may make clear to us which cow, for all cows look the same to us\"\nStill hesitating, they asked yet again for more distinguishing marks, claiming that cows appeared too similar to identify the intended one.\n\n\"Then, Allah willing, we will be guided ˹to the right one˺.”\"\nThe Prophet Muhammad remarked that had they not said 'Allah willing' (if God wills), they would never have been guided to find the specific animal, showing the blessing of mentioning God's will.",
  "71": "\"He replied, “Allah says, ‘It should have been used neither to till the soil nor water the fields; wholesome and without blemish.’”\"\nGod gave the final, extremely rare criteria: a cow entirely free from physical defects, completely unaccustomed to agricultural labor or pulling water wheels, and pure in color without a single spot.\n\n\"They said, “Now you have come with the truth.” Yet they still slaughtered it hesitantly!\"\nHaving exhausted all excuses, they finally located the extremely rare cow and purchased it at an exorbitant price, slaughtering it with deep reluctance and hesitation because of their initial resistance to simple obedience.",
  "72": "\"˹This is˺ when a man was killed and you disputed who the killer was\"\nThis reveals the background context: an innocent, wealthy man among the Israelites was secretly murdered, and the various factions and relatives began blaming and accusing one another, almost sparking civil strife.\n\n\"but Allah revealed what you concealed\"\nHuman beings may hide their crimes from human eyes, but God brings hidden secrets into the open to establish truth and vindicate the innocent.",
  "73": "\"So We instructed, “Strike the dead body with a piece of the cow.”\"\nGod commanded them to strike the corpse of the murdered man with a part of the slaughtered cow. Miraculously, the dead man momentarily came back to life, named his murderer, and then died again.\n\n\"This is how ˹easily˺ Allah brings the dead to life, showing you His signs so that you may understand\"\nThis miraculous event served as an undeniable, visible demonstration of God's power to resurrect the dead on the Day of Judgment, proving that bringing life from death is effortless for the Creator.",
  "74": "\"Even then your hearts became hardened like a rock or even harder\"\nDespite witnessing the miraculous resurrection of a murdered man before their eyes, many among them remained spiritually unmoved, their hearts turning cold, stubborn, and impenetrable to divine guidance.\n\n\"for some rocks gush rivers; others split, spilling water; while others are humbled in awe of Allah\"\nGod draws a striking contrast between their stubborn hearts and inanimate stones. Even solid boulders soften to allow freshwater rivers to gush forth, crack open to release springs, or tumble down mountainsides out of reverence for their Creator, showing that nature submits to God while arrogant human hearts resist.\n\n\"And Allah is never unaware of what you do\"\nNothing escapes God's watchful knowledge; every hardened intention, secret doubt, and open deed is recorded with complete accuracy for final accounting.",
  "75": "\"Do you ˹believers still˺ expect them to be true to you\"\nGod addresses the early Muslim community, tempering their optimistic expectation that all the religious scholars of Medina would immediately embrace the Quran out of historical affinity with scripture.\n\n\"though a group of them would hear the word of Allah then knowingly corrupt it after understanding it?\"\nGod explains that throughout history, certain corrupt leaders among them heard the authentic word of God, understood its clear meaning and prophecies, yet willfully altered, concealed, or distorted its texts for worldly gain and pride.",
  "76": "\"When they meet the believers they say, “We believe.”\"\nCertain hypocritical leaders among them would outwardly profess faith when encountering the Muslim community to gain social peace and goodwill.\n\n\"But in private they say ˹to each other˺, “Will you disclose to the believers the knowledge Allah has revealed to you, so that they may use it against you before your Lord? Do you not understand?”\"\nWhen meeting privately among themselves, their elders would angrily rebuke those who had admitted the truth of the Prophet's description found in the Torah, fearing that Muslims would use these admissions as divine proof against them.",
  "77": "\"Do they not know that Allah is aware of what they conceal and what they reveal?\"\nGod exposes their foolish reasoning: they feared being held accountable by human debate, while completely forgetting that God knows every secret whispered behind closed doors just as clearly as open speech.",
  "78": "\"And among them are the illiterate who know nothing about the Scripture except lies\"\nIn contrast to the deceitful scholars, the common people among them were uneducated in scripture, relying solely on false oral myths, fabricated legends, and wishful thinking fed to them by their corrupt leaders.\n\n\"and ˹so˺ they ˹wishfully˺ speculate\"\nLacking authentic knowledge, they built their religious hopes on baseless assumptions, superstition, and unfounded claims of unconditional salvation.",
  "79": "\"So woe to those who distort the Scripture with their own hands then say, “This is from Allah”—seeking a fleeting gain!\"\nGod delivers a severe condemnation against corrupt religious scribes who altered the text of divine scriptures with their own hands, falsely attributing their human additions and deletions to God in order to secure money, prestige, and influence.\n\n\"So woe to them for what their hands have written, and woe to them for what they have earned\"\nThey face double destruction: first for the grave sin of falsifying sacred scripture and misleading thousands of seekers, and second for the ill-gotten wealth and worldly status they accumulated through their deceit.",
  "80": "\"˹Some of˺ the Jews claim, “The Fire will not touch us except for a number of days.”\"\nThey promoted a false doctrine of ethnic immunity, claiming that because of their noble prophetic ancestors, they would only be punished in Hell for a few brief days (corresponding to the forty days of calf worship) before entering Paradise forever.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Have you taken a pledge from Allah— for Allah never breaks His word—or are you ˹just˺ saying about Allah what you do not know?”\"\nGod commands the Prophet to challenge this unfounded presumption: have they received an explicit divine guarantee from God, or are they fabricating lies about God's justice without any proof? Divine reward and punishment are based on faith and deeds, not ancestral lineage.",
  "81": "\"But no! Those who commit evil and are engrossed in sin\"\nGod refutes the myth of unconditional ethnic salvation. Divine justice is universal: whoever engages in persistent wrongdoing and allows sins, idolatry, and arrogance to surround them completely from every side until their conscience is engulfed will face the consequences of their deeds.\n\n\"will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever\"\nA person whose life was totally defined by rebellion against the Creator and oppression of others without sincere repentance will face eternal accountability in the Fire, regardless of their family background or ancestral claims.",
  "82": "\"And those who believe and do good will be the residents of Paradise\"\nThe universal criterion for salvation is simple and fair: genuine faith in the One God, combined with active righteous deeds, justice, compassion, and moral integrity.\n\n\"They will be there forever\"\nBelievers who live righteously will be honored with an eternal home in Paradise, enjoying absolute peace, happiness, and closeness to God without fear of death, suffering, or loss.",
  "83": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We took a covenant from the children of Israel ˹stating˺, “Worship none but Allah\"\nGod outlines the comprehensive moral covenant given to them. The first and highest commandment is absolute monotheism: dedicating all worship, devotion, and reverence exclusively to God.\n\n\"be kind to parents, relatives, orphans and the needy\"\nThe second set of obligations governs social duty and compassion: honoring parents with devotion, maintaining family bonds, and actively protecting and caring for vulnerable orphans and the impoverished.\n\n\"speak kindly to people; establish prayer; and pay alms-tax.”\"\nThey were commanded to interact with all humanity using gentle, polite, and respectful speech, to establish regular prayer to nourish the soul, and to pay the purifying charity for social justice.\n\n\"But you ˹Israelites˺ turned away—except for a few of you—and were indifferent\"\nTragically, the majority neglected these noble universal commands through indifference and pride, while a faithful minority remained steadfast upon the truth.",
  "84": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We took your covenant that you would neither shed each other’s blood nor expel each other from their homes\"\nGod took a solemn pledge from them to protect the sacredness of human life and property, strictly forbidding internal warfare, murder, and the forced expulsion of their fellow believers from their homes.\n\n\"you gave your pledge and bore witness\"\nTheir ancestors explicitly agreed to these terms and bore witness before God to the sanctity of the covenant.",
  "85": "\"But here you are, killing each other and expelling some of your people from their homes, aiding one another in sin and aggression\"\nDuring the Prophet's time in Medina, the various Jewish tribes allied with opposing pagan factions (Aws and Khazraj). When war erupted, they fought against their own brethren, shed their blood, and expelled them from their homes in direct violation of the Torah.\n\n\"and when those ˹expelled˺ come to you as captives, you still ransom them—though expelling them was unlawful for you\"\nYet, after driving them out, they would pay ransom to free their captured brethren, claiming that the Torah commanded them to redeem Jewish captives, while ignoring that the same Torah strictly forbade killing or expelling them in the first place.\n\n\"Do you believe in some of the Scripture and reject the rest?\"\nGod exposes their selective, hypocritical adherence to religion: following convenient commandments that suit their social image while brazenly violating commandments that require moral sacrifice.\n\n\"Is there any reward for those who do so among you other than disgrace in this worldly life and being subjected to the harshest punishment on the Day of Judgment? For Allah is never unaware of what you do\"\nSelective faith leads to public humiliation and loss of credibility in this world, and severe retribution on the Day of Judgment. God records every compromise and hypocrisy with absolute accuracy.",
  "86": "\"These are the ones who trade the Hereafter for the life of this world\"\nThey made a destructive trade: sacrificing their eternal standing and peace in the Hereafter for temporary political alliances, social pride, and material wealth in this temporary life.\n\n\"So their punishment will not be reduced, nor will they be helped\"\nBecause they knowingly distorted divine commandments for worldly advantage, their retribution in the Hereafter will not be lightened, and no power will be able to rescue them from divine justice.",
  "87": "\"Indeed, We gave Moses the Book and sent after him successive messengers\"\nGod granted the Torah to Moses and followed him with an unbroken lineage of noble prophets and messengers to guide, teach, and remind the Children of Israel.\n\n\"And We gave Jesus, son of Mary, clear proofs and supported him with the holy spirit\"\nGod sent Jesus, the son of Mary, empowering him with undeniable miracles—healing the blind, curing the leper, and raising the dead by God's permission—and strengthening him through the Angel Gabriel.\n\n\"Why is it that every time a messenger comes to you ˹Israelites˺ with something you do not like, you become arrogant, rejecting some and killing others?\"\nWhenever a prophet came with commands that conflicted with their desires, greed, or pride, they responded with arrogance, denying prophets like Jesus and Muhammad, and violently murdering earlier prophets like Zechariah and John the Baptist.",
  "88": "\"They say, “Our hearts are unreceptive!”\"\nIn cynical defiance, they claimed that their hearts were completely sealed and content with their own traditions, needing no new guidance or prophetic teaching.\n\n\"In fact, Allah has condemned them for their disbelief. They have but little faith\"\nGod exposes that their hearts are not naturally closed; rather, they brought divine condemnation upon themselves through deliberate arrogance and rejection of truth, leaving them with practically no authentic faith.",
  "89": "\"Although they used to pray for victory ˹by means of the Prophet˺ over the polytheists\"\nBefore the Prophet Muhammad was sent, the Jewish tribes in Medina would frequently tell the pagan Arabs that an upcoming final prophet was about to emerge, and they prayed for victory under his leadership.\n\n\"when there came to them a Book from Allah which they recognized, confirming the Scripture they had ˹in their hands˺, they rejected it\"\nYet when the Quran was revealed through the Prophet Muhammad—matching the exact prophecies in their scriptures—they rejected him simply because he arose from the Arabs rather than their own lineage.\n\n\"So may Allah’s condemnation be upon the disbelievers\"\nTheir rejection was driven by racial prejudice and jealousy rather than a lack of proof, inviting divine disapproval upon all who obstinately reject the truth.",
  "90": "\"Miserable is the price they have sold their souls for—denying Allah’s revelation\"\nThey traded away their spiritual integrity and eternal salvation for the lowest price imaginable: denying divine truth out of pure pride and envy.\n\n\"and resenting Allah for granting His grace to whoever He wills of His servants!\"\nTheir resentment stemmed from ethnic arrogance, feeling bitter that God chose to bestow prophetic revelation upon an Arab prophet rather than an Israelite, forgetting that divine grace is granted according to God's supreme wisdom.\n\n\"They have earned wrath upon wrath. And such disbelievers will suffer a humiliating punishment\"\nThey accumulated multiple layers of divine displeasure: first for rejecting earlier prophets and violating covenants, and second for rejecting the final Messenger. Their retribution will be deeply humiliating, perfectly matching their arrogance.",
  "91": "\"When it is said to them: “Believe in what Allah has revealed,” they reply, “We only believe in what was sent down to us,”\"\nWhen invited to embrace the Quran, they claim that they are restricted to believing only in scriptures revealed to their own specific lineage.\n\n\"and they deny what came afterwards, though it is the truth confirming their own Scriptures!\"\nThey reject subsequent divine revelation even though it confirms the core monotheistic truth and prophecies contained in their own scriptures.\n\n\"Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, “Why then did you kill Allah’s prophets before, if you are ˹truly˺ believers?”\"\nGod dismantles their claim of loyalty to their own scripture: if they were truly faithful to the Torah, why did their leaders violently persecute and kill the righteous prophets sent directly to them from their own lineage?",
  "92": "\"Indeed, Moses came to you with clear proofs\"\nMoses came with undeniable, visible miracles—the parting of the sea, the glowing hand, the staff turning into a serpent, and the plagues upon Egypt—all demonstrating God's supreme power.\n\n\"then you worshipped the calf in his absence, acting wrongfully\"\nYet despite these clear proofs, they fell into the worship of a golden calf the moment Moses was away on Mount Sinai, proving that their disobedience was a deep-seated historical habit.",
  "93": "\"And when We took your covenant and raised the mountain above you ˹saying˺, “Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and obey,”\"\nGod reminds them of the awe-inspiring covenant at Mount Sinai, where they were commanded to uphold the Torah with unwavering loyalty and commitment.\n\n\"they answered, “We hear and disobey.” The love of the calf was rooted in their hearts because of their disbelief\"\nWhile some profess obedience with words, their actions shouted defiance. Their fascination with idolatry had deeply penetrated their hearts due to their underlying skepticism.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “How evil is what your ˹so-called˺ belief prompts you to do, if you ˹actually˺ believe ˹in the Torah˺!”\"\nThe Prophet is commanded to challenge them: if your claimed faith in the Torah leads you to worship idols, murder prophets, and reject truth, then how corrupt is such a claimed belief!",
  "94": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “If the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter with Allah is exclusively for you ˹Israelites˺ out of all humanity\"\nGod instructs the Prophet to propose a definitive test to those who claimed that Paradise is guaranteed solely for their ethnic group and that no one else can enter it.\n\n\"then wish for death if what you say is true!”\"\nIf someone is genuinely certain that an eternal palace in Paradise is guaranteed specifically for them without accountability, they should naturally look forward to passing from this world of hardship into eternal bliss.",
  "95": "\"But they will never wish for that because of what their hands have done\"\nThe Quran delivers an accurate psychological verdict: they will never wish for death because in their hearts they are fully aware of their past sins, violations of covenants, and rejection of truth, fearing the inevitable judgment that awaits them.\n\n\"And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the wrongdoers\"\nGod knows every hidden misdeed, concealed sin, and secret guilt of all wrongdoers, ensuring that divine justice will be executed with complete fairness.",
  "96": "\"You will surely find them clinging to life more eagerly than any other people, even more than polytheists\"\nBecause of their fear of the Hereafter, they display an intense, desperate attachment to physical worldly life, even exceeding the attachment of pagans who have no belief in resurrection at all.\n\n\"Each one of them wishes to live a thousand years. But even if they were to live that long, it would not save them from the punishment\"\nMany among them wish for an impossibly long lifespan. Yet prolonging physical life does not grant immunity from divine justice; every additional year of unrepentant rebellion merely adds to their moral record.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do\"\nGod observes every action, intention, and attachment, reminding humanity that the quality and righteousness of a life matters far more than its mere length.",
  "97": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Whoever is an enemy of Gabriel should know that he revealed this ˹Quran˺ to your heart by Allah’s Will\"\nWhen some opponents claimed they refused to accept the Quran because the Angel Gabriel—whom they viewed as an angel of punishment—brought the revelation, God clarified that Gabriel does not act independently; he descends solely by God's command to convey revelation to the Prophet's heart.\n\n\"confirming what came before it—a guide and good news for the believers\"\nThe Quran brought by Gabriel confirms all previous divine scriptures, serving as an illuminating guide for daily life and delivering joyful news of eternal salvation to sincere believers.",
  "98": "\"Whoever is an enemy of Allah, His angels, His messengers, Gabriel, and Michael\"\nAll angels and prophets form a single, unified, harmonious family of truth serving the One Creator. Being an enemy of any noble angel or messenger—such as Gabriel or Michael—is an act of war against the Creator Himself.\n\n\"then ˹let them know that˺ Allah is certainly the enemy of the disbelievers\"\nGod stands in absolute opposition to those who show hostility toward His noble angels, messengers, and divine guidance, warning all who harbor enmity against truth of their inevitable defeat.",
  "99": "\"Indeed, We have sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺ clear revelations\"\nThe Quran was revealed with crystal-clear verses, compelling logical proofs, and profound moral wisdom that leave no genuine excuse for doubt.\n\n\"˹But˺ none will deny them except the rebellious\"\nThose who reject these clear signs do so not because of intellectual ambiguity, but due to moral rebellion, arrogance, and a refusal to submit to divine authority.",
  "100": "\"Why is it that every time they make a covenant, a group of them casts it aside?\"\nGod highlights a recurring historical pattern of broken promises: whenever a solemn covenant was established, a faction among them brazenly broke their word and discarded their commitments.\n\n\"In fact, most of them do not believe\"\nThe root cause of this continuous betrayal of trusts is a fundamental lack of authentic, heartfelt conviction in God and the Hereafter.",
  "101": "\"Now, when a messenger from Allah has come to them— confirming their own Scriptures\"\nWhen the Prophet Muhammad arrived with the Quran, confirming the core monotheistic truth and prophecies found in their own scriptures, it should have been welcomed with joy and relief.\n\n\"some of the People of the Book cast the Book of Allah behind their backs as if they did not know\"\nInstead, some of their leaders deliberately discarded the revealed Book of God, throwing it behind their backs and acting completely ignorant of the very prophecies they previously taught, choosing worldly pride over divine truth.",
  "102": "\"They ˹instead˺ followed the magic promoted by the devils during the reign of Solomon\"\nHaving abandoned divine scripture, they turned to dark occult practices and sorcery that rebellious devils fabricated and falsely attributed to Prophet Solomon, claiming that his kingdom was built on magic rather than prophetic authority.\n\n\"Never did Solomon disbelieve, rather the devils disbelieved. They taught magic to the people\"\nGod completely exonerates Prophet Solomon from all accusations of sorcery and disbelief. Solomon was a noble, righteous prophet of God; it was the devils who disbelieved by teaching corrupt magic to humanity to destroy their faith.\n\n\"along with what had been revealed to the two angels, Hârût and Mârût, in Babylon. The two angels never taught anyone without saying, “We are only a test ˹for you˺, so do not abandon ˹your˺ faith.”\"\nIn ancient Babylon, two angels were sent as a moral test for humanity. Before teaching any occult knowledge, they explicitly warned every seeker that practicing it was a test of faith and would destroy their spiritual standing with God.\n\n\"Yet people learned ˹magic˺ that caused a rift ˹even˺ between husband and wife; although their magic could not harm anyone except by Allah’s Will\"\nPeople foolishly pursued harmful practices that broke sacred family bonds and sowed discord between spouses. Yet God makes it clear that no sorcerer, devil, or occult practice has independent power; nothing can harm any soul except by the ultimate permission and decree of God.\n\n\"They learned what harmed them and did not benefit them—although they already knew that whoever buys into magic would have no share in the Hereafter. Miserable indeed was the price for which they sold their souls, if only they knew!\"\nEngaging in magic brings only spiritual ruin, anxiety, and moral decay in this world, and total forfeiture of Paradise in the life to come. Selling one's eternal soul for dark illusions is the most tragic trade imaginable.",
  "103": "\"If only they were faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nGod contrasts the destructive pursuit of occult illusions with the peace and honor of genuine faith and mindfulness.\n\n\"there would have been a better reward from Allah, if only they knew!\"\nThe spiritual reward, protection, inner peace, and eternal blessing granted by God to sincere believers far surpasses anything that worldly trickery or occult power could ever offer.",
  "104": "\"O believers! Do not say, “Râ’ina.” [Herd us!] But say, “Unẓurna,” [Tend to us!] and listen ˹attentively˺\"\nGod instructs the early believers to be mindful of their language. While believers used the word respectfully to mean 'listen to us,' opponents used a subtle Hebrew wordplay to twist it into an insult meaning 'foolishness' or 'herd us.' God commanded believers to use the unambiguous, respectful term 'look upon us and tend to us,' while listening with deep respect.\n\n\"And the disbelievers will suffer a painful punishment\"\nThose who use subtle insults, wordplay, and disrespect against divine prophets and sacred matters will face a severe and painful accountability.",
  "105": "\"The disbelievers from the People of the Book and the polytheists would not want you to receive any blessing from your Lord\"\nHostile opponents harbored deep envy, resenting any blessing, revelation, or spiritual elevation bestowed upon the believers by God.\n\n\"but Allah selects whoever He wills for His mercy. And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty\"\nDivine grace is not subject to human jealousy or ethnic prejudice. God bestows His prophetic guidance, blessings, and wisdom upon whomever He chooses according to His perfect knowledge and infinite generosity.",
  "106": "\"If We ever abrogate a verse or cause it to be forgotten, We replace it with a better or similar one\"\nGod explains the divine wisdom behind legislation: throughout the stages of revelation, God sometimes replaced an earlier ruling with one that was easier, more comprehensive, or better suited to the community's developing spiritual maturity.\n\n\"Do you not know that Allah is Most Capable of everything?\"\nGod possesses supreme authority and absolute power over all things. As the Sovereign Lord, He legislates what is best for His servants at each stage of their spiritual development.",
  "107": "\"Do you not know that the kingdom of the heavens and the earth belongs ˹only˺ to Allah\"\nAbsolute sovereignty, dominion, and ownership over the entire cosmos belong exclusively to God. He alone has the right to command, guide, and legislate for creation.\n\n\"and you have no guardian or helper besides Allah?\"\nHuman beings have no true protector, guardian, or ultimate source of help outside of God, reminding believers to place their complete trust solely in the Almighty.",
  "108": "\"Or do you ˹believers˺ intend to ask of your Messenger as Moses was asked before?\"\nBelievers are cautioned against falling into the trap of demanding unnecessary, obstinate, or disrespectful signs and questions, similar to the stubborn demands the Israelites made to Moses (such as demanding to see God physically).\n\n\"But whoever trades belief for disbelief has truly strayed from the Right Way\"\nDemanding excessive signs out of skepticism rather than seeking genuine understanding leads to spiritual deviation and loss of faith.",
  "109": "\"Many among the People of the Book wish they could turn you ˹believers˺ back to disbelief because of their envy, after the truth has been made clear to them\"\nMany opponents actively desired to pull believers away from their faith, motivated not by honest intellectual disagreement, but by bitter envy after the truth of the Prophet had been made undeniable.\n\n\"Pardon and bear with them until Allah delivers His decision. Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything\"\nGod commanded the early Muslim community to respond with patience, dignified forbearance, and moral restraint, trusting that God's justice and victory would unfold at the appointed time.",
  "110": "\"Establish prayer, and pay alms-tax\"\nBelievers are reminded to focus on their own spiritual foundation and social duties: maintaining sincere, regular prayer to nourish the soul, and paying charity to support the poor.\n\n\"Whatever good you send forth for yourselves, you will ˹certainly˺ find ˹its reward˺ with Allah. Surely Allah is All-Seeing of what you do\"\nNo good deed, hidden prayer, act of patience, or charitable gift is ever lost. Every act of goodness is preserved by God, who sees all things and rewards the sincere with boundless generosity.",
  "111": "\"The Jews and Christians each claim that none will enter Paradise except those of their own faith. These are their desires\"\nEach sectarian group claimed exclusive monopoly over salvation, asserting that Paradise was reserved strictly for their specific label, without regard for moral character or genuine faith. God exposes these claims as empty wishful thinking.\n\n\"Reply, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Show ˹me˺ your proof if what you say is true.”\"\nGod establishes the universal standard of truth: bold religious assertions must be supported by clear, authentic divine proof rather than subjective cultural assumptions.",
  "112": "\"But no! Whoever submits themselves to Allah and does good will have their reward with their Lord\"\nGod presents the true, universal criterion for salvation: genuine submission of one's entire heart and will to God (sincere monotheism) combined with moral excellence and righteous deeds.\n\n\"And there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve\"\nWhoever lives by this sincere submission and moral goodness is promised complete security: free from fear regarding the future and free from sorrow over the past, dwelling in eternal joy.",
  "113": "\"The Jews say, “The Christians have nothing to stand on” and the Christians say, “The Jews have nothing to stand on,” although both recite the Scriptures\"\nEach group completely invalidated the other, despite both possessing revealed scriptures that commanded mutual respect, monotheism, and moral uprightness.\n\n\"And those ˹pagans˺ who have no knowledge say the same ˹about people of faith˺. Surely Allah will judge between them on the Day of Judgment regarding their dispute\"\nIgnorant idolaters copied this sectarian rhetoric, dismissing all people of faith. God will settle all disputes, claims, and mutual condemnations with absolute justice on the Day of Judgment.",
  "114": "\"Who does more wrong than those who prevent Allah’s Name from being mentioned in His places of worship and strive to destroy them?\"\nThere is no greater injustice than barring people from entering houses of worship to remember God, or attempting to destroy, desecrate, or close sacred sanctuaries (such as the pagans barring Muslims from the Sacred Mosque in Mecca).\n\n\"Such people have no right to enter these places except with fear. For them there is disgrace in this world, and they will suffer a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter\"\nDesecrating sacred spaces invites profound humiliation in this life and an immense, severe punishment in the Hereafter.",
  "115": "\"To Allah belong the east and the west, so wherever you turn you are facing ˹towards˺ Allah\"\nGod is not restricted by physical location or geographic boundaries; the entire universe belongs to Him. Wherever a believer turns in prayer—whether in travel, confusion of direction, or obedience to divine commands—they are turning toward their omnipresent Lord.\n\n\"Surely Allah is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing\"\nGod's presence, grace, and knowledge encompass every corner of creation, and He knows the sincere intention of every heart in worship.",
  "116": "\"They say, “Allah has offspring.” Glory be to Him!\"\nGod rejects and glorifies Himself above the false human claims that He has taken a son or daughters (such as the pagan claims about angels or the erroneous deification of Ezra and Jesus).\n\n\"In fact, to Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth—all are subject to His Will\"\nGod does not require offspring, partners, or lineage. Everything in the cosmos is His creation and servant, entirely obedient and subject to His absolute sovereignty.",
  "117": "\"˹He is˺ the Originator of the heavens and the earth!\"\nGod brought the entire universe into existence out of absolute nothingness, without any preexisting model, material, or effort.\n\n\"When He decrees a matter, He simply tells it, “Be!” And it is!\"\nCreating and executing divine will requires no physical labor, fatigue, or time; God simply commands 'Be,' and the entire universe, life, or event immediately comes into existence.",
  "118": "\"Those who have no knowledge say, “If only Allah would speak to us or a sign would come to us!”\"\nIgnorant skeptics demanded that God speak to each of them directly or produce spectacular customized miracles before they would believe.\n\n\"The same was said by those who came before. Their hearts are all alike. Indeed, We have made the signs clear for people of sure faith\"\nArrogant minds across all centuries repeat the same demands, blinded by pride. In reality, God has filled the universe and scripture with crystal-clear signs for anyone whose heart is sincere and seeks certainty.",
  "119": "\"We have surely sent you with the truth ˹O Prophet˺ as a deliverer of good news and a warner\"\nThe Prophet Muhammad was sent with the authentic truth to convey joyful news of Paradise to those who believe and do good, and to warn the arrogant of the consequences of their rebellion.\n\n\"And you will not be accountable for the residents of the Hellfire\"\nA prophet's responsibility is solely to deliver the message with clarity, kindness, and patience; he is not held responsible for those who obstinately choose to reject guidance and doom themselves.",
  "120": "\"Never will the Jews or Christians be pleased with you, until you follow their faith\"\nPartisan religious factions will never be fully satisfied with compromises or diplomatic overtures; their ultimate goal is to have you abandon your authentic guidance and adopt their specific sectarian beliefs.\n\n\"Say, “Allah’s guidance is the only ˹true˺ guidance.”\"\nThe Prophet is instructed to declare with firm clarity that authentic guidance comes solely from God, not from following human sects, traditions, or subjective desires.\n\n\"And if you were to follow their desires after ˹all˺ the knowledge that has come to you, there would be none to protect or help you against Allah\"\nThis serves as a stern warning: compromising clear divine truth to appease human factions forfeits divine protection, leaving a person without any helper against the justice of God.",
  "121": "\"Those We have given the Book follow it as it should be followed\"\nThis praises the sincere believers among the People of the Book who recite and study their scriptures with integrity, reflecting deeply upon their true meaning, implementing their moral commandments, and embracing the prophecies of the final Messenger.\n\n\"It is they who are ˹truly˺ believers in it. As for those who reject it, it is they who are the losers\"\nThose who genuinely follow their scripture will naturally recognize and accept the Quran. Those who distort or reject it out of arrogance forfeit both their original faith and their eternal salvation, ending in complete loss.",
  "122": "\"O Children of Israel! Remember My favours upon you\"\nGod repeats this appeal to the Children of Israel, urging them to reflect on the immense historical blessings, divine rescues, and prophetic honors bestowed upon their forefathers.\n\n\"and how I honoured you above the others\"\nThis historical elevation was given because they were entrusted with divine revelation and leadership during their era, reminding them that divine honors require continuous humility and obedience, not complacency.",
  "123": "\"And guard yourselves against the Day when no soul will be of any help to another\"\nA solemn reminder of the Day of Judgment, where all earthly family ties and tribal privileges will vanish, leaving each soul to stand solely on the merits of its own faith and deeds.\n\n\"No ransom will be taken, no intercession accepted, and no help will be given\"\nNo amount of worldly wealth could ever be accepted as a ransom from divine punishment, no unauthorized intercession will benefit a stubborn denier, and no human army or ally can protect against divine justice.",
  "124": "\"˹Remember˺ when Abraham was tested by his Lord with ˹certain˺ commandments, which he fulfilled\"\nProphet Abraham was subjected to severe, monumental trials—including being thrown into fire for calling to monotheism, leaving his wife and infant son in the barren desert of Mecca, and being commanded to sacrifice his beloved son—and he fulfilled every single command with unwavering faith and submission.\n\n\"Allah said, “I will certainly make you into a role model for the people.”\"\nBecause of his total obedience, God rewarded Abraham by appointing him as a universal spiritual leader and exemplary role model for all humanity.\n\n\"Abraham asked, “What about my offspring?” Allah replied, “My covenant is not extended to the wrongdoers.”\"\nWhen Abraham lovingly asked if this leadership would continue in his descendants, God established a fundamental principle: spiritual leadership and divine favor are not inherited by blood or race; wrongdoers and oppressors among his descendants have no share in this sacred covenant.",
  "125": "\"And ˹remember˺ when We made the Sacred House a centre and a sanctuary for the people\"\nGod designated the Kaaba in Mecca as a universal center of pilgrimage, spiritual return, and a safe sanctuary where violence, fear, and bloodshed are strictly forbidden.\n\n\"˹saying˺, “˹You may˺ take the standing-place of Abraham as a site of prayer.”\"\nBelievers are instructed to perform prayer near the Station of Abraham (the stone on which he stood while building the Kaaba), honoring his dedication and legacy of monotheism.\n\n\"And We entrusted Abraham and Ishmael to purify My House for those who circle it, who meditate in it, and who bow and prostrate themselves ˹in prayer˺\"\nAbraham and his eldest son Ishmael were commissioned to physically and spiritually cleanse the Sacred Mosque from all idols, filth, and false practices, preparing it as a pure space for worshippers performing circumambulation, spiritual retreat, and devout prayer.",
  "126": "\"And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said, “My Lord, make this city ˹of Mecca˺ secure and provide fruits to its people—those among them who believe in Allah and the Last Day.”\"\nAbraham prayed for the barren valley of Mecca, asking God to grant it permanent safety and peace, and to bless its believing inhabitants with abundant agricultural fruits and provisions.\n\n\"He answered, “As for those who disbelieve, I will let them enjoy themselves for a little while, then I will condemn them to the torment of the Fire. What an evil destination!”\"\nGod clarified that worldly provisions (food, water, wealth) are granted to both believers and deniers in this temporary life, but spiritual favor and eternal salvation in the Hereafter belong exclusively to the righteous, while unrepentant deniers will face the torment of the Fire.",
  "127": "\"And ˹remember˺ when Abraham raised the foundation of the House with Ishmael\"\nAbraham and Ishmael worked together with their own hands, lifting the stones and building the walls of the Sacred House (the Kaaba) upon its original foundations in Mecca.\n\n\"˹both praying,˺ “Our Lord! Accept ˹this˺ from us. You are indeed the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\"\nDespite performing one of the greatest acts of devotion in human history, they demonstrated supreme humility by earnestly pleading with God to accept their humble deed, teaching that true believers never boast about their good works but always pray for divine acceptance.",
  "128": "\"Our Lord! Make us both ˹fully˺ submit to You and from our descendants a nation that will submit to you\"\nThey prayed for themselves and their future progeny, asking God to keep them steadfast in pure submission and monotheism, and to raise a faithful, devoted community from their lineage.\n\n\"Show us our rituals, and turn to us in grace. You are truly the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful\"\nThey asked God to teach them the correct rites of pilgrimage and worship, and to continually forgive their shortcomings, acknowledging God's infinite mercy and acceptance of repentance.",
  "129": "\"Our Lord! Raise from among them a messenger who will recite to them Your revelations, teach them the Book and wisdom, and purify them\"\nAbraham and Ishmael made a profound historical supplication: asking God to send a final Messenger from among the Arabian descendants of Ishmael who would recite divine revelation, teach scripture and deep wisdom, and morally purify human hearts.\n\n\"Indeed, You ˹alone˺ are the Almighty, All-Wise\"\nThe Prophet Muhammad was the direct fulfillment of this ancient prayer of Abraham, sent to illuminate the world with divine power and wisdom.",
  "130": "\"And who would reject the faith of Abraham except a fool!\"\nOnly someone completely devoid of wisdom and moral clarity would turn away from the pure monotheistic faith of Abraham—a faith built on reason, sincerity, and direct devotion to the One Creator.\n\n\"We certainly chose him in this life, and in the Hereafter he will surely be among the righteous\"\nGod selected Abraham with high honor and distinction in this worldly life, and in the Hereafter he will reside in the highest ranks of the righteous in Paradise.",
  "131": "\"When his Lord ordered him, “Submit ˹to My Will˺,” he responded, “I submit to the Lord of all worlds.”\"\nThe defining characteristic of Abraham was his immediate, unhesitating, and wholehearted submission to the command of his Creator, placing divine will above personal desires, cultural traditions, and worldly attachments.",
  "132": "\"This was the advice of Abraham—as well as Jacob—to his children, ˹saying˺, “Indeed, Allah has chosen for you this faith\"\nAbraham and his grandson Jacob passed down the greatest possible inheritance to their children: not worldly wealth or land, but the legacy of pure monotheism and moral uprightness.\n\n\"so do not die except in ˹a state of full˺ submission.”\"\nThey urgently counseled their offspring to live every single day in devotion and submission to God, so that when death inevitably arrives, they depart this world in a state of authentic faith.",
  "133": "\"Or did you witness when death came to Jacob? He asked his children, “Who will you worship after my passing?”\"\nOn his deathbed, Jacob gathered his twelve sons (the ancestors of the tribes of Israel) to ensure the continuity of their spiritual foundation, asking them who they would worship after he departed this world.\n\n\"They replied, “We will ˹continue to˺ worship your God, the God of your forefathers— Abraham, Ishmael, and Isaac—the One God. And to Him we ˹all˺ submit.”\"\nHis sons united in declaring their unwavering commitment to pure monotheism: worshipping the One True God of Abraham, Ishmael, and Isaac, and submitting entirely to His Will, refuting any subsequent sectarian distortions.",
  "134": "\"That was a community that had already gone before. For them is what they earned and for you is what you have earned\"\nGod dismantles the concept of relying on the righteousness of ancestors. Those noble prophets lived their lives and earned their rewards through their own faith and sacrifices; contemporary generations will be judged strictly on their own personal deeds and choices.\n\n\"And you will not be accountable for what they have done\"\nNo person can enter Paradise on the coat-tails of holy forefathers, nor will they be blamed for their ancestors' errors; individual accountability before God is absolute.",
  "135": "\"The Jews and Christians each say, “Follow our faith to be ˹rightly˺ guided.”\"\nEach group claimed that salvation and guidance were confined strictly to their specific sectarian identity and denominational label.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “No! We follow the faith of Abraham, the upright—who was not a polytheist.”\"\nThe Prophet is commanded to proclaim that authentic guidance is the pure, upright monotheism of Abraham, who preceded both Judaism and Christianity and was entirely free from all forms of idolatry and compromise.",
  "136": "\"Say, ˹O believers,˺ “We believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us; and what was revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants\"\nBelievers are instructed to declare their broad, inclusive faith in the One True God and in the continuous chain of divine revelations given to the patriarchal prophets.\n\n\"and what was given to Moses, Jesus, and other prophets from their Lord. We make no distinction between any of them. And to Allah we all submit.”\"\nA true believer honors and embraces all genuine prophets sent by God without rejecting any of them, submitting wholeheartedly to the Creator who guided them all with the same core message.",
  "137": "\"So if they believe in what you believe, then they will indeed be ˹rightly˺ guided\"\nIf the People of the Book embrace this complete, uncorrupted monotheism and accept all of God's revealed scriptures and messengers, they will attain authentic guidance.\n\n\"But if they turn away, they are simply opposed ˹to the truth˺. But Allah will spare you their evil. For He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\"\nIf they refuse out of pride, their rejection is based on stubborn sectarian hostility rather than lack of evidence. God promises to protect the believers from their plots, for He hears all words and knows all intentions.",
  "138": "\"This is the ˹natural˺ Way of Allah. And who is better than Allah in ordaining a way?\"\nTrue faith is the natural spiritual imprint and purification bestowed by God upon the human soul, far surpassing any superficial ritual baptism or human religious coloring.\n\n\"And we worship ˹none but˺ Him\"\nThe essence of this divine way is dedicating all worship, devotion, and reverence exclusively to the Creator.",
  "139": "\"Say, “Would you dispute with us about Allah, while He is our Lord and your Lord?\"\nThe Prophet is commanded to challenge sectarian critics: why argue about God's universal mercy when He is the Lord, Creator, and Sustainer of all humanity, not the private property of any single tribe or nation?\n\n\"We are accountable for our deeds and you for yours. And we are devoted to Him ˹alone˺.”\"\nEvery human being will be judged solely on the basis of their own sincerity and actions, and true believers are those who dedicate their worship and lives with pure devotion to God alone.",
  "140": "\"Or do you claim that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants were all Jews or Christians?”\"\nGod exposes a historical impossibility: the terms and formal structures of Judaism and Christianity arose long after these early prophets lived. Abraham lived centuries before Moses and the Torah, and over a millennium before Jesus and the Gospel.\n\n\"Say, “Who is more knowledgeable: you or Allah?” Who does more wrong than those who hide the testimony they received from Allah? And Allah is never unaware of what you do\"\nGod knows history with absolute truth. Those who conceal the clear evidence in their scriptures that Abraham was a pure monotheist commit a grave injustice, but God is fully aware of everything they hide and do.",
  "141": "\"That was a community that had already gone before. For them is what they earned and for you is what you have earned\"\nThis principle of individual moral responsibility is repeated to emphasize that spiritual status cannot be inherited. Ancient prophets and noble predecessors achieved their high status through their own personal faith, righteousness, and sacrifices. Every modern generation and individual is judged solely by their own conduct.\n\n\"And you will not be accountable for what they have done\"\nGod ensures that no soul carries the burden or sins of another, nor does anyone gain salvation through the merits of others without personal faith and good deeds.",
  "142": "\"The foolish among the people will ask, “Why did they turn away from the direction of prayer they used to face?”\"\nWhen the command came to change the direction of prayer from Jerusalem to the Kaaba in Mecca, short-sighted critics and hypocrites began raising objections, viewing the physical direction as an absolute theological end rather than an act of obedience to God's living command.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “The east and west belong ˹only˺ to Allah. He guides whoever He wills to the Straight Path.”\"\nThe Prophet is commanded to clarify that all directions and realms belong exclusively to God. Physical directions possess no sacredness in themselves; true holiness lies in obeying God's commands wherever He directs.",
  "143": "\"And so We have made you ˹believers˺ an upright community so that you may be witnesses over humanity and that the Messenger may be a witness over you\"\nGod describes the community of believers as a balanced, just, and moderate nation, avoiding both ascetic negligence and fanatical extremism. They are appointed as moral witnesses for truth, justice, and righteousness before all humanity, while the Prophet serves as their exemplary witness and guide.\n\n\"We assigned your former direction of prayer only to distinguish those who would remain faithful to the Messenger from those who would lose faith. It was certainly a difficult test except for those ˹rightly˺ guided by Allah\"\nThe change of prayer direction was a vital spiritual test to distinguish those whose faith was anchored in total obedience to the Messenger from those whose attachment was merely cultural or tied to physical places.\n\n\"And Allah would never discount your ˹previous acts of˺ faith. Surely Allah is Ever Gracious and Most Merciful to humanity\"\nGod reassures the believers that all prayers previously performed facing Jerusalem were fully accepted and rewarded, for God never allows sincere acts of faith to go unrewarded, reflecting His boundless grace and mercy.",
  "144": "\"Indeed, We see you ˹O Prophet˺ turning your face towards heaven. Now We will make you turn towards a direction ˹of prayer˺ that will please you\"\nThe Prophet Muhammad naturally longed to face the Kaaba—the ancient House built by his forefather Abraham—frequently looking up to the heavens in silent anticipation of divine instruction. God lovingly answered his heartfelt wish.\n\n\"So turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque ˹in Mecca˺—wherever you are, turn your faces towards it\"\nGod established the Kaaba in Mecca as the permanent, universal focal point of prayer for all believers across the globe, creating a powerful symbol of unity for the entire global community.\n\n\"Those who were given the Scripture certainly know this to be the truth from their Lord. And Allah is never unaware of what they do\"\nScholars among the People of the Book knew from their own ancient prophecies that the final Prophet would face the sanctuary of Abraham, but some concealed this truth. God watches all actions and intentions with perfect awareness.",
  "145": "\"Even if you were to bring every proof to the People of the Book, they would not accept your direction ˹of prayer˺, nor would you accept theirs; nor would any of them accept the direction ˹of prayer˺ of another\"\nStubborn sectarian opponents are driven by partisan pride rather than evidence; no intellectual argument or proof will make them change their sectarian positions, nor do they agree among themselves (as Jews face Jerusalem and Christians face the east).\n\n\"And if you were to follow their desires after ˹all˺ the knowledge that has come to you, then you would certainly be one of the wrongdoers\"\nThis stern warning emphasizes that divine truth must never be compromised or diluted to appease sectarian expectations or popular desires.",
  "146": "\"Those We have given the Scripture recognize this ˹Prophet˺ as they recognize their own children\"\nThe scholarly leaders among the People of the Book possessed detailed prophetic descriptions of the final Messenger in their scriptures, recognizing his truthfulness and mission as clearly and instinctively as a parent recognizes their own child.\n\n\"Yet a group of them hides the truth knowingly\"\nDespite this undeniable internal certainty, some among them willfully suppressed and concealed the truth out of pride, jealousy, and fear of losing worldly influence.",
  "147": "\"˹This is˺ the truth from your Lord, so do not ever be one of those who doubt\"\nDivine revelation is absolute, unadulterated truth originating from the Lord of the universe. Sincere believers are urged to remain firmly anchored in certainty, rejecting all whispered doubts, sectarian propaganda, and confusion.",
  "148": "\"Everyone turns to their own direction ˹of prayer˺. So compete with one another in doing good\"\nRather than wasting precious time in endless sectarian disputes over physical directions and cultural formalities, believers are commanded to focus on what truly matters: racing with one another in performing righteous deeds, establishing justice, and serving humanity.\n\n\"Wherever you are, Allah will bring you all together ˹for judgment˺. Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything\"\nNo matter where human beings live or die across the earth, God will gather every soul on the Day of Resurrection for complete accounting, for His power over all creation is absolute.",
  "149": "\"Wherever you are ˹O Prophet˺, turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque\"\nThis instruction is reaffirmed to establish the universal, binding nature of facing the Kaaba during prayer from any location across the globe.\n\n\"This is certainly the truth from your Lord. And Allah is never unaware of what you ˹all˺ do\"\nFacing the Kaaba is a direct divine command that unifies the hearts and bodies of worshippers, and God observes every act of devotion with complete awareness.",
  "150": "\"Wherever you are ˹O Prophet˺, turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque. And wherever you ˹believers˺ are, face towards it, so that people will have no argument against you, except the wrongdoers among them\"\nUnifying the prayer direction toward the Kaaba dismantled all valid historical arguments: the Arabs could no longer claim the Prophet abandoned Abraham's sanctuary, and honest scholars of previous scriptures saw the fulfillment of ancient prophecies. Only stubborn, unjust opponents continued their baseless objections.\n\n\"Do not fear them; fear Me, so that I may ˹continue to˺ perfect My favour upon you and so you may be ˹rightly˺ guided\"\nBelievers are commanded to disregard human criticism and intimidation, placing their reverent awe solely in God. In doing so, God promises to complete His blessings of spiritual guidance, dignity, and unity upon them.",
  "151": "\"Since We have sent you a messenger from among yourselves—reciting to you Our revelations, purifying you, teaching you the Book and wisdom, and teaching you what you never knew\"\nGod highlights the monumental favor of sending the Prophet Muhammad from among humanity. His mission encompasses four vital pillars: reciting divine revelation with clarity, morally and spiritually purifying human hearts from corruption, teaching the deep wisdom and laws of scripture, and imparting knowledge of unseen truths that human intellect could never discover on its own.",
  "152": "\"remember Me; I will remember you\"\nThis is one of the most comforting and profound promises in all of divine revelation. When a human being remembers God through prayer, supplication, gratitude, and moral consciousness, God remembers them with divine assistance, forgiveness, protection, and elevation in the highest celestial company.\n\n\"And thank Me, and never be ungrateful\"\nBelievers are commanded to live in constant thankfulness for God's blessings and to guard against the spiritual disease of ingratitude, which blinds the soul to divine goodness.",
  "153": "\"O believers! Seek comfort in patience and prayer\"\nWhen facing the hardships, uncertainties, and emotional trials of life, believers are instructed to rely upon two powerful spiritual pillars: patience, which builds internal resilience and emotional restraint, and prayer, which connects the soul directly to the fountain of divine peace and strength.\n\n\"Allah is truly with those who are patient\"\nGod grants His special support, comfort, protection, and success to those who maintain patience during adversity, ensuring that they never walk through life's trials alone.",
  "154": "\"Never say that those martyred in the cause of Allah are dead—in fact, they are alive! But you do not perceive it\"\nThose who sacrifice their lives standing for truth, justice, and the defense of the innocent are not merely lifeless corpses. In the unseen spiritual realm, their souls are alive, conscious, honored, and provided with boundless joy and provision near their Lord, experiencing a noble reality that physical human senses cannot perceive.",
  "155": "\"We will certainly test you with a touch of fear and famine and loss of property, life, and crops\"\nWorldly life is an intentional realm of spiritual testing and character building, not permanent comfort. Every human being will experience various forms of adversity: moments of fear, economic hardship, loss of material wealth, the passing of loved ones, and disruptions in livelihood.\n\n\"Give good news to those who patiently endure\"\nJoyful news of divine reward, spiritual elevation, and eternal peace is promised to those who face these inevitable life challenges with dignified patience, steadfast faith, and moral integrity.",
  "156": "\"who, when faced with a disaster, say, “Surely to Allah we belong and to Him we will ˹all˺ return.”\"\nThis profound statement represents the ultimate spiritual anchor during grief and loss. It acknowledges that our lives, families, wealth, and health are not our absolute possessions, but temporary gifts on loan from God. When a trial occurs, returning to the realization that we belong entirely to God and will soon return to Him dissolves despair and infuses the heart with peace.",
  "157": "\"They are the ones who will receive Allah’s blessings and mercy\"\nThose who respond to grief and adversity with patient faith and trust in God receive the highest honors: divine praises in the celestial assembly, special coverings of divine mercy, and emotional solace in their hearts.\n\n\"And it is they who are ˹rightly˺ guided\"\nThey are the truly guided individuals who navigate life's storms with wisdom, finding light in darkness and turning personal trials into stepping stones for spiritual growth.",
  "158": "\"Indeed, ˹the hills of˺ Ṣafa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah\"\nThe two historic hills of Safa and Marwah in Mecca are designated as sacred landmarks of devotion, commemorating the heroic faith, patience, and desperate search for water by Hagar (the mother of Ishmael) in the barren desert.\n\n\"So whoever performs the pilgrimage or minor pilgrimage, let them walk between ˹the two hills˺\"\nWalking and pacing between these two hills during pilgrimage is an established act of worship that connects believers to the profound legacy of trust in God demonstrated by Abraham's family.\n\n\"And whoever does good willingly, Allah is truly Appreciative, All-Knowing\"\nGod recognizes and generously rewards every voluntary act of goodness, devotion, and charity performed with a sincere heart, for He is fully aware of every hidden effort.",
  "159": "\"Those who hide the clear proofs and guidance that We have revealed— after We made it clear for humanity in the Book—will be condemned by Allah and ˹all˺ those who condemn\"\nA severe divine warning is issued against religious scholars and leaders who knowingly conceal divine truths, moral guidance, and prophetic proofs from the public to protect their worldly status, popularity, or financial gain. They invite the condemnation of God, the angels, and all righteous creation.",
  "160": "\"As for those who repent, mend their ways, and let the truth be known\"\nThe door of divine mercy remains open even for those who previously concealed the truth, provided they fulfill three conditions: feeling sincere remorse and turning back to God, actively rectifying the moral harm they caused, and openly declaring the truth they once hid.\n\n\"they are the ones to whom I will turn ˹in forgiveness˺, for I am the Accepter of Repentance, Most Merciful\"\nGod reassures every sincere soul that when they step forward in genuine repentance, He meets them with boundless forgiveness, compassion, and welcoming grace.",
  "161": "\"Surely those who disbelieve and die as disbelievers\"\nThis verse specifies the tragic condition of those who not only reject divine truth during life, but persist in their rebellion and die without ever seeking forgiveness or turning to God in repentance.\n\n\"are condemned by Allah, the angels, and all of humanity\"\nBy willfully closing their hearts to the Creator and spreading moral corruption, they cut themselves off from all sources of divine grace, inviting the universal condemnation of God, His noble angels, and all righteous human beings on the Day of Judgment.",
  "162": "\"They will be in Hell forever\"\nBecause their rebellion against truth was intentional, unrepentant, and persistent throughout their entire worldly existence, their retribution in Hell is permanent.\n\n\"Their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be delayed ˹from it˺\"\nDivine justice will be exact and uninterrupted; their suffering will not be mitigated, nor will they be granted any postponement or second chance once the Day of Judgment arrives.",
  "163": "\"Your God is ˹only˺ One God\"\nThis central declaration proclaims the core pillar of all authentic religion: the absolute oneness and uniqueness of God. There are no co-creators, no partners, no intermediaries, and no equals to the Lord of the universe.\n\n\"There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful\"\nHe alone is worthy of ultimate devotion, love, reverence, and obedience, and His entire relationship with creation is guided by infinite compassion and tender mercy.",
  "164": "\"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth; the alternation of the day and the night\"\nGod directs the human intellect to observe the cosmic evidence of His existence and oneness: the vast, balanced architecture of the universe and the precise, rhythmic cycle of day and night providing light for work and darkness for rest.\n\n\"the ships that sail the sea for the benefit of humanity; the rain sent down by Allah from the skies, reviving the earth after its death\"\nHe highlights the physical laws of buoyancy enabling global trade across oceans, and the miracle of rainfall transforming barren, lifeless deserts into lush, productive landscapes.\n\n\"the scattering of all kinds of creatures throughout; the shifting of the winds; and the clouds drifting between the heavens and the earth—˹in all of this˺ are surely signs for people of understanding\"\nThe incredible diversity of animal life, the atmospheric wind patterns regulating temperatures and pollinating crops, and the controlled suspension of rainclouds are all compelling, visible signs designed for thinking minds to recognize the supreme wisdom, power, and benevolence of the One Creator.",
  "165": "\"Still there are some who take others as Allah’s equal—they love them as they should love Allah\"\nDespite overwhelming cosmic proofs of God's oneness, some people set up idols, rulers, saints, or worldly desires as rivals to God, giving them the ultimate love, fear, and obedience that belong exclusively to the Creator.\n\n\"but the ˹true˺ believers love Allah even more\"\nTrue believers possess an unshakeable, pure love for God that surpasses all worldly attachments, wealth, family, or personal comfort, because they recognize God as the ultimate Source of all beauty and life.\n\n\"If only the wrongdoers could see the ˹horrible˺ punishment ˹awaiting them˺, they would certainly realize that all power belongs to Allah and that Allah is indeed severe in punishment\"\nIf those who associate partners with God could glimpse the reality of the Hereafter, they would instantly realize that all authority, power, and dominion belong solely to God, and that their false idols can offer them zero protection against divine justice.",
  "166": "\"˹Consider the Day˺ when those who misled others will disown their followers—when they face the torment\"\nOn the Day of Judgment, corrupt leaders, false spiritual guides, and tyrants who led others into disbelief will completely disown, abandon, and curse their former followers as the reality of divine punishment becomes visible.\n\n\"and the bonds that united them will be cut off\"\nAll worldly loyalties, criminal pacts, political alliances, and social ties of mutual flattery will instantly shatter, leaving every wrongdoer isolated before the court of divine justice.",
  "167": "\"The ˹misled˺ followers will cry, “If only we could have a second chance, we would disown them as they disowned us.”\"\nThe betrayed followers will express deep, desperate regret, wishing they could return to the world to reject their corrupt leaders just as their leaders have now abandoned them.\n\n\"And so Allah will make them remorseful of their misdeeds. And they will never ˹be able to˺ leave the Fire\"\nTheir past deeds will become an agonizing source of bitter sorrow and regret, and they will find no escape from the consequences of their deliberate defiance.",
  "168": "\"O humanity! Eat from what is lawful and good on the earth\"\nGod addresses all of humankind with a gracious invitation to enjoy the abundant provisions of the earth, setting two fundamental criteria: food must be lawful (free from divine prohibition and injustice) and good (pure, healthy, wholesome, and nutritious).\n\n\"and do not follow Satan’s footsteps. He is truly your sworn enemy\"\nPeople are warned against following the subtle, gradual deceptions of Satan, who begins with small compromises and leads human beings step-by-step into moral destruction and spiritual ruin.",
  "169": "\"He only incites you to commit evil and indecency\"\nSatan's persistent mission is to drag human beings down by tempting them toward shameful acts, moral filth, injustice, and cruelty that degrade human dignity.\n\n\"and to claim against Allah what you do not know\"\nThe most dangerous satanic trap is speaking about God without knowledge—inventing religious innovations, fabricating false prohibitions, or distorting divine guidance to mislead humanity.",
  "170": "\"When it is said to them, “Follow what Allah has revealed,” they reply, “No! We ˹only˺ follow what we found our forefathers practicing.”\"\nWhen called to embrace clear divine revelation and rational truth, stubborn polytheists fall back on blind conformism, stubbornly clinging to inherited customs, cultural traditions, and ancestral habits.\n\n\"˹Would they still do so,˺ even if their forefathers had ˹absolutely˺ no understanding or guidance?\"\nThe Quran exposes the irrationality of blind traditionalism: why follow ancestors in religious matters when those ancestors were completely devoid of sound understanding, wisdom, and divine guidance?",
  "171": "\"The example of the disbelievers ˹not responding to the Messenger’s warning˺ is like a flock not comprehending the calls and cries of the shepherd\"\nThe Quran presents a vivid parable comparing unthinking deniers to a herd of livestock that hears the vocal sounds, shouts, and cries of the caring shepherd but understands nothing of the actual words, meaning, or warnings being spoken.\n\n\"˹They are wilfully˺ deaf, dumb and blind so they have no understanding\"\nBecause they refuse to apply their reason and moral conscience, they become spiritually deaf to truth, unable to speak righteousness, and blind to divine signs, behaving like unthinking animals.",
  "172": "\"O believers! Eat from the good things We have provided for you\"\nBelievers are commanded to enjoy the wholesome, pure, and delicious provisions created by God without imposing unnecessary ascetic restrictions upon themselves.\n\n\"And give thanks to Allah if you ˹truly˺ worship Him ˹alone˺\"\nEnjoying pure sustenance must be matched with active, heartfelt gratitude and sincere worship dedicated exclusively to the Creator who provides every meal and breath.",
  "173": "\"He has only forbidden you ˹to eat˺ carrion, blood, swine, and what is slaughtered in the name of any other than Allah\"\nGod outlines four specific prohibitions based on spiritual purity and physical health: naturally dead animals (carrion), running blood, pork, and animals slaughtered as sacrifices dedicated to idols or false deities instead of God.\n\n\"But if someone is compelled by necessity —neither driven by desire nor exceeding immediate need—they will not be sinful\"\nIslam establishes the compassionate legal principle of preserving life: in extreme, life-threatening emergencies where no lawful food is available, consuming just enough forbidden food to survive carries no sin.\n\n\"Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\"\nGod does not burden human beings beyond their capacity, legislating with boundless mercy, understanding, and forgiveness.",
  "174": "\"Indeed, those who hide Allah’s revelations, trading them for a fleeting gain consume nothing but fire into their bellies\"\nThose who knowingly suppress divine truth, prophecies, and moral rulings to secure money, gifts, or social popularity are essentially swallowing molten fire, as their illicit wealth will become the very instrument of their punishment in the Hereafter.\n\n\"Allah will neither speak to them on the Day of Judgment, nor will He purify them. And they will suffer a painful punishment\"\nOn that terrifying Day, God will turn away from them in anger, refusing to speak words of mercy or purify them of their sins, leaving them to face a humiliating and agonizing retribution.",
  "175": "\"They are the ones who trade guidance for misguidance and forgiveness for punishment\"\nThey made the most foolish trade imaginable: exchanging the light of divine guidance for the darkness of misguidance, and trading God's welcoming forgiveness for His severe punishment.\n\n\"How persistent are they in pursuit of the Fire!\"\nThis expresses astonishment at their reckless behavior, behaving as if they had an endless capacity to endure the torment of Hellfire through their brazen defiance of divine warnings.",
  "176": "\"That is because Allah has revealed the Book in truth\"\nGod revealed the holy scripture with absolute truth, justice, and clarity to unite humanity under divine guidance.\n\n\"And surely those who differ regarding it are totally engrossed in opposition\"\nThose who splinter into hostile sects, inventing conflicting doctrines and distorting scripture, have plunged into deep defiance and stubborn alienation from the truth.",
  "177": "\"Righteousness is not in turning your faces towards the east or the west\"\nTrue piety is not merely a collection of external rituals, formal motions, or debates over physical directions of prayer, important though prayer is.\n\n\"Rather, the righteous are those who believe in Allah, the Last Day, the angels, the Books, and the prophets\"\nTrue righteousness begins with internal conviction: sincere faith in the One True God, the Day of Judgment, the unseen angels, all revealed scriptures, and the complete lineage of noble prophets.\n\n\"who give charity out of their cherished wealth to relatives, orphans, the poor, ˹needy˺ travellers, beggars, and for freeing captives\"\nRighteousness is proven through compassionate social action: generously giving from wealth that one loves to support family, care for vulnerable orphans, relieve poverty, assist stranded travelers, help those who ask, and liberate people from debt and bondage.\n\n\"who establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and keep the pledges they make\"\nIt includes fulfilling spiritual duties through punctual prayer, paying the purifying charity, and demonstrating uncompromising integrity by honoring all promises, contracts, and moral pledges.\n\n\"and who are patient in times of suffering, adversity, and in ˹the heat of˺ battle. It is they who are true ˹in faith˺, and it is they who are mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nIt requires profound resilience: maintaining patience through poverty, physical illness, and courageous defense of justice. Those who unite these inner convictions with social compassion and moral strength are the truly faithful and God-conscious individuals.",
  "178": "\"O believers! ˹The law of˺ retaliation is set for you in cases of murder— a free man for a free man, a slave for a slave, and a female for a female\"\nGod establishes exact, equitable justice in cases of murder, abolishing the tribal arrogance of pre-Islamic Arabia where powerful tribes demanded multiple innocent lives or entire families in retaliation for a single person.\n\n\"But if the offender is pardoned by the victim’s guardian, then blood-money should be decided fairly and payment should be made courteously\"\nIslam balances justice with compassion: if the victim's family chooses to forgive the killer in exchange for financial compensation, the blood-money must be determined fairly and paid promptly with dignity and goodwill.\n\n\"This is a concession and a mercy from your Lord. But whoever transgresses after that will suffer a painful punishment\"\nAllowing pardon and compensation is a divine mercy that heals social wounds and prevents endless blood feuds. Taking revenge after accepting blood-money is a grave transgression that will be severely punished.",
  "179": "\"There is ˹security of˺ life for you in ˹the law of˺ retaliation, O people of reason\"\nGod reveals the profound sociological wisdom behind strict, fair legal consequences: knowing that taking an innocent life results in the forfeiture of one's own life serves as the ultimate deterrent against murder, preserving the lives of both potential victims and would-be killers.\n\n\"so that you may become mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nA just legal framework protects society from violence and chaos, instilling a deep collective reverence for the sacred sanctity of human life.",
  "180": "\"It is prescribed that when death approaches any of you—if they leave something of value—a will should be made in favour of parents and immediate family with fairness\"\nBefore the detailed, comprehensive inheritance laws were later finalized in the Quran, believers were commanded to write a fair, just will allocating resources to their parents and close relatives.\n\n\"˹This is˺ an obligation on those who are mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nEnsuring financial fairness and protecting the economic security of family members is a sacred moral duty binding upon all who cultivate mindfulness and reverence before God.",
  "181": "\"But whoever changes the will after hearing it, the blame will only be on those who made the change\"\nA deceased person's just last will and testament is a sacred legal trust. If an executor, witness, or inheritor secretly alters, falsifies, or tampers with the will to unfairly reallocate wealth, the sin and divine guilt rest entirely upon the person who made the fraudulent change, while the deceased person is completely free from blame.\n\n\"Indeed, Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing\"\nGod hears every spoken word and knows every secret manipulation and hidden document, assuring the wronged that divine justice will rectify all deceits.",
  "182": "\"Whoever suspects an error or an injustice in the will and brings about a ˹fair˺ settlement among the parties will not be sinful\"\nIf a mediator, family member, or judge notices that the deceased unintentionally made an unfair mistake or deliberately committed an injustice (such as depriving rightful heirs or favoring one child over another), it is lawful and commendable to intervene diplomatically and negotiate a fair, voluntary settlement among all parties.\n\n\"Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\"\nReconciling family disputes, establishing equity, and preventing bitter feuds is an act of peacemaking that earns God's boundless forgiveness and mercy.",
  "183": "\"O believers! Fasting is prescribed for you—as it was for those before you\"\nGod addresses the community of faith with the obligation of fasting during the holy month. Fasting is not a novel innovation; it is a universal, time-honored spiritual practice that was ordained for all previous nations and prophets (including Moses and Jesus) to cultivate spiritual elevation.\n\n\"so perhaps you will become mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nThe ultimate purpose of fasting is not mere physical deprivation or starvation, but the development of mindfulness and God-consciousness. By voluntarily restraining lawful physical desires (food, drink, and intimacy) from dawn until sunset solely for God's sake, a person develops the moral discipline and self-control required to restrain themselves from unlawful deeds throughout the entire year.",
  "184": "\"˹Fast a˺ prescribed number of days. But whoever of you is ill or on a journey, then ˹let them fast˺ an equal number of days ˹after Ramaḍân˺\"\nGod demonstrates His gentle compassion by prescribing fasting for only a limited, manageable number of days in the year. Furthermore, anyone experiencing physical illness or the hardships of travel is granted a divine concession to break their fast and make up the missed days at a later time when they have recovered or completed their journey.\n\n\"For those who can only fast with extreme difficulty, compensation can be made by feeding a needy person ˹for every day not fasted˺\"\nThose who suffer from chronic, incurable illnesses or advanced old age that make fasting permanently unsafe or unbearable are excused from fasting altogether, compensating by providing a wholesome daily meal to an impoverished person for each missed day.\n\n\"But whoever volunteers to give more, it is better for them. And to fast is better for you, if only you knew\"\nGiving extra charity beyond the required amount brings greater spiritual reward. And whenever one has the physical capacity, enduring the fast is always spiritually superior and more beneficial for the soul and body than opting for compensation.",
  "185": "\"Ramaḍân is the month in which the Quran was revealed as a guide for humanity with clear proofs of guidance and the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺\"\nThe month of Ramadan holds supreme spiritual honor because it was chosen by God as the historical time when the Quran began to be revealed to the Prophet Muhammad. The Quran serves three universal functions: comprehensive guidance for all humanity, crystal-clear proofs illuminating the truth, and the ultimate criterion to distinguish right from wrong.\n\n\"So whoever is present this month, let them fast. But whoever is ill or on a journey, then ˹let them fast˺ an equal number of days ˹after Ramaḍân˺\"\nEvery able-bodied, resident adult believer who witnesses this sacred month is commanded to observe its fast, while the compassionate exemptions for the sick and traveler are reaffirmed to emphasize divine flexibility.\n\n\"Allah intends ease for you, not hardship, so that you may complete the prescribed period and proclaim the greatness of Allah for guiding you, and perhaps you will be grateful\"\nThis verse articulates a fundamental principle of Islamic law: divine religion is built on ease, facilitation, and compassion, never on unnecessary hardship or burden. Completing the days of fasting culminates in joyful celebration (Eid), glorifying God for the gift of guidance, and cultivating deep gratitude for His boundless mercy.",
  "186": "\"When My servants ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about Me: I am truly near\"\nThis is one of the most tender, intimate, and comforting verses in the entire Quran. Unlike other verses where God instructs the Prophet to 'Say,' here God responds directly and personally to humanity, assuring every seeking soul that He is intimately close, closer to them than their own jugular vein.\n\n\"I respond to one’s prayer when they call upon Me\"\nGod guarantees that He personally hears and answers every sincere supplication. No intermediary, priest, or ritual barrier is required; any person can call upon their Creator directly at any time, in any language, and from any place. The Prophet Muhammad explained that God answers prayer in three ways: by granting what was asked, by diverting an equivalent harm, or by saving an immense reward for the Hereafter (recorded in Musnad Ahmad).\n\n\"So let them respond ˹with obedience˺ to Me and believe in Me, perhaps they will be guided ˹to the Right Way˺\"\nHuman beings are invited to respond to God's loving call with sincere faith, prayer, and moral obedience, which illuminates the heart and guides the soul toward permanent spiritual success.",
  "187": "\"It has been made permissible for you to be intimate with your wives during the nights preceding the fast. Your spouses are a garment for you as you are for them\"\nIn the early days of Islam, believers assumed intimacy was forbidden throughout the entire month of Ramadan once they slept. God lifted this restriction, permitting marital relations throughout the nights of Ramadan. The Quran uses the exquisite metaphor of a 'garment' to describe marriage: spouses serve as clothing for one another—providing warmth, mutual protection, emotional comfort, dignity, and covering each other's vulnerabilities.\n\n\"Allah knows that you were deceiving yourselves. So He has accepted your repentance and pardoned you\"\nGod recognizes human emotional needs and pardons their past anxieties, demonstrating His gentleness toward human weakness.\n\n\"So now you may be intimate with them and seek what Allah has prescribed for you. ˹You may˺ eat and drink until you see the light of dawn breaking the darkness of night, then complete the fast until nightfall\"\nEating, drinking, and marital relations are fully permitted throughout the entire night until the distinct white thread of dawn appears on the horizon, at which point the fast begins and continues until the sun sets completely at nightfall.\n\n\"Do not be intimate with your spouses while you are meditating in the mosques. These are the limits set by Allah, so do not exceed them. This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to people, so they may become mindful ˹of Him˺\"\nDuring dedicated spiritual retreat in the mosques (the practice of spiritual devotion in the final days of Ramadan), marital intimacy is set aside so the heart can focus entirely on communion with God. Honoring these clear divine boundaries cultivates true mindfulness and piety.",
  "188": "\"Do not consume one another’s wealth unjustly\"\nGod establishes strict ethical principles governing economic life: it is unlawful to acquire money or property through theft, fraud, usury, extortion, deception, counterfeit goods, or gambling.\n\n\"nor deliberately bribe authorities in order to devour a portion of others’ property, knowing that it is a sin\"\nUsing bribery, political corruption, or legal loopholes to manipulate judges and usurp the rightful property of others is a severe moral crime. True ownership is judged by divine justice, not corrupt court verdicts.",
  "189": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about the phases of the moon. Say, “They are a means for people to determine time and pilgrimage.”\"\nWhen people asked about the astronomical wisdom behind the lunar phases (the crescent waxing into a full moon and waning again), God explained that the moon provides an accessible, universal natural calendar for all humanity to calculate months, agricultural seasons, commercial contracts, and the sacred timing of pilgrimage.\n\n\"Righteousness is not in entering your houses from the back doors. Rather, righteousness is to be mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nGod abolishes a superstitious pre-Islamic pagan ritual where pilgrims returning from Mecca refused to enter their homes through the front door, climbing through the back instead. God clarifies that piety does not consist of meaningless superstitions or manufactured hardships, but in genuine mindfulness of God.\n\n\"So enter your homes through their ˹proper˺ doors, and be mindful of Allah so you may be successful\"\nBelievers are instructed to conduct their lives with practical common sense, approaching things directly and lawfully through proper channels, while maintaining moral integrity to attain ultimate success.",
  "190": "\"Fight in the cause of Allah ˹only˺ against those who wage war against you\"\nThis verse establishes the foundational ethics of warfare in Islam: military defense is permitted solely to protect innocent lives, defend religious freedom, and repel armed aggression from hostile oppressors who wage war against the community.\n\n\"but do not exceed the limits. Surely Allah does not like transgressors\"\nIslam strictly forbids exceeding the bounds of justice even in warfare. The Prophet Muhammad and his successors explicitly prohibited attacking non-combatants, murdering women, children, elderly people, monks, or destroying trees, crops, livestock, and infrastructure. Aggression and cruelty are abhorred by God.",
  "191": "\"Kill them wherever you come upon them and drive them out of the places from which they have driven you out\"\nThis command specifically applied to the pagan oppressors of Mecca who had persecuted, tortured, and violently expelled the early Muslims from their homes and city. Muslims were authorized to fight back on the battlefield and reclaim their stolen homeland.\n\n\"For persecution is far worse than killing\"\nReligious tyranny, violent suppression of conscience, torturing people for their faith, and driving families from their homes is a crime far more severe and socially destructive than the battlefield killing necessary to stop such tyranny.\n\n\"And do not fight them at the Sacred Mosque unless they attack you there. If they do so, then fight them—that is the reward of the disbelievers\"\nThe sanctity of the Sacred Mosque in Mecca must be honored; armed conflict is strictly prohibited within its borders unless the enemy initiates an attack there, in which case self-defense is fully permitted.",
  "192": "\"But if they cease, then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\"\nIf the aggressors halt their military hostilities, cease their persecution, and embrace peace or faith, all retaliation must immediately stop. God welcomes repentance and commands believers to meet peace with forgiveness and mercy.",
  "193": "\"Fight against them ˹if they persecute you˺ until there is no more persecution, and ˹your˺ devotion will be to Allah ˹alone˺\"\nThe ultimate objective of defensive resistance is the complete elimination of religious tyranny and persecution, establishing a society where every human being has the freedom to worship God without fear of violence or oppression.\n\n\"If they stop ˹persecuting you˺, let there be no hostility except against the aggressors\"\nThe moment aggression ceases, all fighting must end immediately. Hostility is never directed against people based on their identity, but solely against active oppressors who commit injustice.",
  "194": "\"˹There will be retaliation in˺ a sacred month for ˹an offence in˺ a sacred month, and all violations will bring about retaliation\"\nThe four traditional sacred months were historically observed as periods of strict ceasefire. If hostile enemies violate the sanctity of these months by launching an unprovoked attack against believers, the defenders are permitted proportional self-defense to protect their community.\n\n\"So, if anyone attacks you, retaliate in the same manner. ˹But˺ be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah is with those mindful ˹of Him˺\"\nSelf-defense must strictly adhere to the principle of proportionality: never exceeding the harm inflicted, never committing atrocities, and remaining constantly mindful of divine moral limits.",
  "195": "\"Spend in the cause of Allah and do not let your own hands throw you into destruction ˹by withholding˺\"\nBelievers are urged to generously donate wealth to support community defense, relieve poverty, and fund public welfare. Withholding wealth out of greed or hoarding leads to collective ruin, leaving society vulnerable to economic decay and defeat.\n\n\"And do good, for Allah certainly loves the good-doers\"\nBelievers are encouraged to strive for moral and spiritual excellence in all actions, for God bestows His special love upon those who live with generosity, compassion, and upright character.",
  "196": "\"Complete the pilgrimage and minor pilgrimage for Allah\"\nOnce a believer enters the sacred state of pilgrimage (Hajj or Umrah), they are obligated to complete all its rites with sincere devotion solely for God, not for worldly prestige, tourism, or social praise.\n\n\"But if prevented ˹from proceeding˺, then ˹offer˺ whatever sacrificial animals you can afford. And do not shave your heads until the sacrificial animal reaches its destination\"\nIf a pilgrim is blocked from reaching Mecca due to illness, enemy blockade, or unavoidable obstacles, they may exit the sacred state by offering a sacrificial animal and shaving their head where they are detained.\n\n\"But if any of you is ill or has a scalp ailment ˹requiring shaving˺, then compensate either by fasting, charity, or a sacrificial offering\"\nA pilgrim who is forced to shave their head early due to head lice, painful injury, or illness is excused, provided they offer a compensatory act: fasting three days, feeding six poor people, or slaughtering an animal.\n\n\"In times of peace, you may combine the pilgrimage and minor pilgrimage then make the sacrificial offering you can afford. Whoever cannot afford that ˹offering˺, let them fast three days during pilgrimage and seven after returning ˹home˺—completing ten. These offerings are for those who do not live near the Sacred House. And be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah is severe in punishment\"\nGod outlines the flexible rules for combining the minor and major pilgrimage in a single journey, mandating a sacrificial gift for non-residents of Mecca, or ten days of fasting for those who cannot afford the animal, concluding with a reminder of divine accountability.",
  "197": "\"˹Commitment to˺ pilgrimage is made in appointed months\"\nThe season for pilgrimage takes place during well-known lunar months (Shawwal, Dhu al-Qadah, and the first ten days of Dhu al-Hijjah), giving pilgrims ample time to prepare and travel.\n\n\"Whoever commits to ˹performing˺ pilgrimage, let them stay away from intimate relations, foul language, and arguments during pilgrimage\"\nDuring pilgrimage, a believer enters a heightened state of spiritual purity: all marital intimacy, vulgar speech, swearing, arrogance, and heated arguments are strictly forbidden, fostering an atmosphere of universal peace and brotherhood.\n\n\"Whatever good you do, Allah ˹fully˺ knows of it\"\nEvery act of patience, kindness, sharing, and prayer performed during this sacred journey is fully recorded and cherished by God.\n\n\"Take ˹necessary˺ provisions ˹for the journey˺—surely the best provision is righteousness. And be mindful of Me, O people of reason!\"\nPilgrims are instructed to carry sufficient physical food, water, and funds for their travel so they do not become a burden on others. Above all, they are reminded that the greatest spiritual provision for life's ultimate journey is God-consciousness, moral character, and righteousness.",
  "198": "\"There is no blame on you for seeking the bounty of your Lord ˹during this journey˺\"\nIslam does not demand unnatural asceticism: pilgrims are fully permitted to engage in honest commerce, trade, and business alongside their pilgrimage, provided trade does not distract from their primary spiritual devotion.\n\n\"When you return from ’Arafât, praise Allah near the sacred place and praise Him for having guided you, for surely before this ˹guidance˺ you were astray\"\nAfter standing in prayer at the plain of Arafat (the spiritual peak of Hajj), pilgrims gather at Muzdalifah (the Sacred Landmark) to remember God, thanking Him for delivering them from ignorance, idolatry, and confusion into the light of divine guidance.",
  "199": "\"Then go forth with the rest of the pilgrims\"\nGod abolishes an elitist pre-Islamic tribal practice where the noble Quraysh tribe refused to stand with common pilgrims at Arafat, standing at a closer location instead. God commands all human beings—regardless of race, wealth, or tribal lineage—to stand together in absolute equality at Arafat, demonstrating the universal brotherhood of humanity before the Creator.\n\n\"And seek Allah’s forgiveness. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\"\nEven after fulfilling the greatest rites of worship, pilgrims are commanded to seek divine forgiveness with humility, recognizing that human efforts are always in need of God's pardoning grace and mercy.",
  "200": "\"When you have fulfilled your sacred rites, praise Allah as you used to praise your forefathers ˹before Islam˺, or even more passionately\"\nIn pre-Islamic times, Arabs would gather after pilgrimage at Mina to boast about their ancestral lineage, tribal poetry, and past battles. God redirects this passionate enthusiasm toward what truly matters: filling the atmosphere with the remembrance, praise, and gratitude of God.\n\n\"There are some who say, “Our Lord! Grant us ˹Your bounties˺ in this world,” but they will have no share in the Hereafter\"\nGod describes short-sighted, materialistic people whose supplications and ambitions are restricted entirely to worldly wealth, health, and status, with no concern for the soul, moral character, or eternal salvation, leaving them with zero reward in the life to come.",
  "201": "\"Yet there are others who say, “Our Lord! Grant us the good of this world and the Hereafter\"\nThis is the most comprehensive, balanced, and beloved supplication in Islam. It contrasts with purely materialistic desires by asking for both wholesome worldly flourishing—such as physical health, lawful provision, a loving family, beneficial knowledge, and peace of mind—and the ultimate blessings of the Hereafter, including divine forgiveness, an easy reckoning, and high ranks in Paradise.\n\n\"and protect us from the torment of the Fire.”\"\nThe prayer concludes with a plea for divine protection from the punishment of Hell, as well as protection from the sins, temptations, and heedlessness in this worldly life that lead toward it. In authentic narrations in Sahih Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, the Companion Anas ibn Malik reported that this was the most frequent supplication recited by the Prophet Muhammad.",
  "202": "\"It is they who will receive a ˹heavenly˺ reward for the good they have done\"\nThose who maintain this holistic balance between worldly righteousness and sincere devotion to the Hereafter are guaranteed an abundant share of divine reward and eternal salvation proportional to their strivings.\n\n\"Surely Allah is swift in reckoning\"\nGod accounts for all creation simultaneously without distraction, holding billions accountable in the blink of an eye, and the Day of Judgment is swiftly approaching, reminding believers to utilize their time in doing good.",
  "203": "\"And remember Allah during ˹these˺ appointed days\"\nPilgrims are commanded to maintain continuous remembrance, glorification, and praise of God during the Days of Tashriq (the 11th, 12th, and 13th of Dhul-Hijjah) in Mina following the major rites of Hajj.\n\n\"Whoever departs swiftly on the second day is not sinful, neither are those who stay behind ˹till the third—seeking additional reward˺, so long as they are mindful ˹of their Lord˺\"\nGod provides practical flexibility: pilgrims may conclude their stay in Mina after two days without blame, or stay for the third day for extra reward, emphasizing that true acceptance depends on the presence of mindfulness and sincerity in the heart.\n\n\"And be mindful of Allah, and know that to Him you will ˹all˺ be gathered\"\nJust as millions of diverse pilgrims assemble in Mina, all humanity will ultimately be gathered before the Creator for final judgment.",
  "204": "\"There are some ˹hypocrites˺ who impress you with their views regarding worldly affairs and openly call upon Allah to witness what is in their hearts\"\nGod warns against slick-talking hypocrites whose eloquent speeches, political charm, and dramatic oaths of loyalty sound impressive in public meetings.\n\n\"yet they are your worst adversaries\"\nBehind their smooth speech and pious pretenses lies intense, bitter hostility against truth, justice, and sincere believers.",
  "205": "\"And when they leave ˹you˺, they strive throughout the land to spread mischief in it and destroy crops and cattle\"\nThe moment they leave public scrutiny, their true nature emerges: they spread corruption, create civil discord, and destroy agricultural crops, livestock, and societal infrastructure.\n\n\"Allah does not like mischief\"\nGod abhors all forms of environmental destruction, tyranny, and social corruption, establishing that righteousness is proven by constructive action, not deceptive speech.",
  "206": "\"When it is said to them, “Fear Allah,” pride carries them off to sin\"\nWhen sincere advisors gently remind them to revere God and cease their wrongdoing, their toxic arrogance and wounded ego drive them deeper into transgression and rebellion.\n\n\"Hell will be their proper place. What an evil place to rest!\"\nTheir pride will be stripped away in Hell, which serves as the exact just dwelling for their persistent arrogance.",
  "207": "\"And there are those who would dedicate their lives to Allah’s pleasure\"\nIn radiant contrast to arrogant hypocrites, God praises selfless heroes of faith (exemplified by Suhayb al-Rumi, who surrendered his entire life savings to his persecutors in Mecca just to be allowed to emigrate for God's sake).\n\n\"And Allah is Ever Gracious to ˹His˺ servants\"\nGod receives their profound sacrifices with tender grace, boundless love, and eternal honoring in Paradise.",
  "208": "\"O believers! Enter into Islam wholeheartedly\"\nBelievers are commanded to embrace the guidance of Islam completely and comprehensively—in their beliefs, ethics, business dealings, family relations, and personal conduct—rather than practicing faith selectively.\n\n\"and do not follow Satan’s footsteps. Surely he is your sworn enemy\"\nThey are warned against Satan's gradual deceptions, which begin with small compromises and lead step-by-step into spiritual ruin.",
  "209": "\"If you falter after receiving the clear proofs, then know that Allah is indeed Almighty, All-Wise\"\nWhoever slips into deliberate deviation and sin after receiving clear divine guidance should remember that God possesses absolute power to hold wrongdoers accountable, enacting all decrees with perfect wisdom.",
  "210": "\"Are they waiting for Allah ˹Himself˺ to come to them in the shade of clouds, along with the angels? ˹If He did˺, then the matter would be settled ˹at once˺\"\nSkeptics who demand a physical, visible manifestation of God and descending angels are warned: if the unseen realm became physically visible, the period of earthly testing would instantly end, and immediate destruction would fall upon the deniers.\n\n\"And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺\"\nAll human affairs and disputes will return to God for definitive, inescapable judgment.",
  "211": "\"Ask the Children of Israel how many clear signs We have given them\"\nGod points to the history of the Israelites, who were blessed with monumental miracles, heavenly provisions, and prophetic leadership.\n\n\"And whoever trades Allah’s favour—after receiving it—˹for disbelief˺ should know that Allah is indeed severe in punishment\"\nExchanging divine guidance and blessings for ingratitude, corruption, and disbelief invites severe divine justice upon any nation that betrays its moral trust.",
  "212": "\"The life of this world has been made appealing to the disbelievers, and they mock the believers\"\nDeniers are consumed by the superficial glamour and material wealth of this fleeting world, leading them to look down on and mock sincere believers who lack material riches.\n\n\"Those who are mindful ˹of Allah˺ will rank above them on the Day of Judgment. And Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit\"\nOn the Day of Judgment, worldly status will be reversed: the God-fearing will occupy the highest celestial ranks above the arrogant, and God bestows limitless eternal provisions upon the sincere.",
  "213": "\"Humanity had once been one community ˹of believers before they lost faith˺\"\nHumanity originally began as a unified community upon pure monotheism under Prophet Adam, until deviations and disputes arose over time.\n\n\"Then Allah raised prophets as deliverers of good news and as warners, and revealed to them the Scriptures in truth to judge among people regarding their disputes\"\nGod sent noble prophets throughout history with authentic scriptures to guide humanity, resolve conflicts, and establish justice.\n\n\"And no one disputed the Scriptures except the very people who received them after clear proofs had come to them—out of jealousy. Then Allah, by His grace, has guided the believers to the truth regarding those disputes. And Allah guides whoever He wills to the Straight Path\"\nSectarian conflict was fueled by human pride, jealousy, and rivalry, but God graciously guided sincere believers to the unified, authentic truth of pure monotheism.",
  "214": "\"Do you think you will be admitted into Paradise without being tested like those before you?\"\nGod dismantles spiritual naivety: entering Paradise is not achieved without facing trials that purify character and prove authentic conviction.\n\n\"They were afflicted with suffering and adversity and were so ˹violently˺ shaken that ˹even˺ the Messenger and the believers with him cried out, “When will Allah’s help come?” Indeed, Allah’s help is ˹always˺ near\"\nPast righteous communities endured severe poverty, physical persecution, and overwhelming adversity until they reached the limit of human endurance, crying out for divine help. God comforts every struggling believer with the eternal promise that divine rescue and relief are always near for the patient.",
  "215": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet in˺ what ˹way˺ they should donate\"\nWhen believers inquired about how to spend their charitable donations and who holds the highest priority for assistance,\n\n\"Say, “Whatever donations you give are for parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, and ˹needy˺ travellers. Whatever good you do is certainly well known to Allah.”\"\nGod outlined the rightful hierarchy of charity: first caring for parents and immediate family, then supporting orphans, relieving poverty, and aiding stranded travelers, assuring believers that every charitable gift is fully known and rewarded by God.",
  "216": "\"Fighting has been made obligatory upon you ˹believers˺, though you dislike it\"\nMilitary defense was mandated to protect the innocent from persecution and tyranny, even though human nature naturally dislikes the hardship, danger, and trauma of war.\n\n\"Perhaps you dislike something which is good for you and like something which is bad for you. Allah knows and you do not know\"\nHuman understanding is short-sighted: people often dislike challenging duties (like courage and sacrifice) that yield long-term freedom and justice, and desire easy pleasures that lead to ruin. God's infinite wisdom sees what human intellect cannot perceive.",
  "217": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about fighting in the sacred months. Say, “Fighting during these months is a great sin\"\nWhen an accidental clash occurred during a sacred month, God affirmed that warfare in sacred months is a serious violation.\n\n\"But hindering ˹others˺ from the Path of Allah, rejecting Him, and expelling the worshippers from the Sacred Mosque is ˹a˺ greater ˹sin˺ in the sight of Allah. For persecution is far worse than killing\"\nHowever, the ongoing crimes of the pagan oppressors—torturing believers, suppressing religious freedom, and violently expelling families from Mecca—are far more monstrous sins in God's sight than the accidental clash.\n\n\"And they will not stop fighting you until they turn you away from your faith—if they can. And whoever among you renounces their own faith and dies a disbeliever, their deeds will become void in this life and in the Hereafter. It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.”\"\nOppressors will persist in hostility until they crush religious freedom. Believers are warned against apostasy and despair, for abandoning faith destroys all spiritual reward and leads to eternal loss.",
  "218": "\"Surely those who have believed, emigrated, and struggled in the Way of Allah—they can hope for Allah’s mercy\"\nThose who sacrificed their homes, wealth, and comfort to preserve their faith and defend justice have the highest right to expect God's boundless mercy, forgiveness, and victory.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\"\nGod's mercy welcomes and honors all sincere striving and sacrifice.",
  "219": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about intoxicants and gambling. Say, “There is great evil in both, as well as some benefit for people—but the evil outweighs the benefit.”\"\nIn the second stage of prohibiting alcohol and gambling, God explains the rational moral basis: while alcohol may provide temporary escapism and trade profits, and gambling offers quick financial thrills, their immense harm—addiction, domestic violence, liver destruction, social ruined families, and loss of reason—vastly outweighs any minor benefit.\n\n\"They ˹also˺ ask you what they should donate. Say, “Whatever you can spare.” This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you ˹believers˺, so perhaps you may reflect\"\nRegarding charity, believers are instructed to give generously from what is surplus beyond their necessary personal and family needs, reflecting on divine wisdom.",
  "220": "\"upon this world and the Hereafter\"\nBelievers are called to maintain a thoughtful, reflective outlook that weighs the temporary nature of worldly choices against their eternal consequences in the Hereafter.\n\n\"And they ask you concerning orphans. Say, “Improving their condition is best. And if you partner with them, they are bonded with you ˹in faith˺. And Allah knows who intends harm and who intends good\"\nWhen believers feared handling orphans' wealth after hearing stern warnings against eating it, God provided ease: managing and integrating their assets with fairness to improve their economic well-being is highly commendable. God knows who acts with sincere benevolence and who intends exploitation.\n\n\"Had Allah willed, He could have made it difficult for you. Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.”\"\nGod legislates with compassion and ease, never intending to burden His servants beyond their capacity.",
  "221": "\"Do not marry polytheistic women until they believe; for a believing slave-woman is better than a free polytheist, even though she may look pleasant to you\"\nIslam establishes that marriage is fundamentally a spiritual partnership and the foundation of family life. A believer of humble social status who shares sincere faith in the One True God is vastly superior as a life partner than an attractive, wealthy idolater who rejects the Creator.\n\n\"And do not marry your women to polytheistic men until they believe, for a believing slave-man is better than a free polytheist, even though he may look pleasant to you\"\nLikewise, believing women must not be married to polytheistic men, emphasizing that spiritual compatibility, moral character, and shared faith outweigh superficial wealth, lineage, and physical glamour.\n\n\"They invite ˹you˺ to the Fire while Allah invites ˹you˺ to Paradise and forgiveness by His grace. He makes His revelations clear to the people so perhaps they will be mindful\"\nPolytheistic ideologies drag the soul toward spiritual destruction and the Hellfire, whereas God calls humanity toward moral purity, eternal forgiveness, and Paradise through His clear guidance.",
  "222": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about menstruation. Say, “Beware of its harm! So keep away, and do not have intercourse with your wives during their monthly cycles until they are purified\"\nIslam provides balanced, healthy guidance: menstruation is a natural biological cycle involving physical discomfort and shedding. Marital intercourse is temporarily paused during this time for physical hygiene, medical health, and the wife's comfort.\n\n\"When they purify themselves, then you may approach them in the manner specified by Allah. Surely Allah loves those who always turn to Him in repentance and those who purify themselves.”\"\nUnlike ancient cultures that ostracized menstruating women and refused to sit or eat with them, Islam emphasizes continuous loving companionship, hugging, and sharing meals, pausing only intercourse until the cycle concludes and the wife bathes. God loves those who maintain inward spiritual repentance and outward physical cleanliness.",
  "223": "\"Your wives are like farmland for you, so approach them ˹consensually˺ as you please\"\nThe Quran uses the beautiful metaphor of fertile farmland to describe the loving, creative, and life-giving nature of marital intimacy, permitting couples complete consensual freedom in intimate positions within lawful intercourse that leads to life.\n\n\"And send forth something good for yourselves. Be mindful of Allah, and know that you will meet Him. And give good news to the believers\"\nCouples are encouraged to begin intimate relations with the remembrance of God and prayers for righteous offspring, keeping their ultimate meeting with God at the center of their lives.",
  "224": "\"Do not use Allah’s Name in your oaths as an excuse for not doing good, not guarding against evil, or not making peace between people\"\nGod forbids the wrongful practice of taking casual, angry oaths in God's name to justify refusing to do good—such as swearing never to visit a relative or never to reconcile an argument.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing\"\nThe Prophet Muhammad instructed that if someone takes an oath not to do something and then sees that doing it is better and more righteous, they should break the oath, pay the required expiation, and perform the good deed (recorded in Sahih Muslim).",
  "225": "\"Allah will not hold you accountable for unintentional oaths, but for what you intended in your hearts\"\nGod does not penalize unintended, casual speech habits (such as phrases uttered without serious intent), but holds human beings strictly accountable for deliberate, binding oaths made with conscious conviction and intention.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing\"\nGod treats human speech with forbearance and boundless forgiveness, looking at the true intention of the heart.",
  "226": "\"Those who swear not to have intercourse with their wives must wait for four months\"\nTo protect women from emotional abandonment, Islam established a strict four-month legal limit for husbands who swear in anger to withhold intimacy from their wives.\n\n\"If they change their mind, then Allah is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\"\nIf the husband re-evaluates his conduct, breaks the oath, and returns to loving marital relations within the four months, God accepts his repentance and restores peace to the marriage.",
  "227": "\"But if they settle on divorce, then Allah is indeed All-Hearing, All-Knowing\"\nIf the four months expire and the husband stubbornly refuses reconciliation, the marriage must be formally dissolved through legal divorce to prevent the woman from being trapped in emotional limbo. God hears all statements and knows all motives.",
  "228": "\"Divorced women must wait three monthly cycles ˹before they can re-marry˺\"\nA divorced woman observes a mandatory waiting period of three menstrual cycles to definitively ensure there is no pregnancy (protecting paternity rights) and to provide an emotional buffer period for potential reconciliation.\n\n\"It is not lawful for them to conceal what Allah has created in their wombs, if they ˹truly˺ believe in Allah and the Last Day\"\nWomen are entrusted with the moral duty to report pregnancy honestly without concealment.\n\n\"And their husbands reserve the right to take them back within that period if they desire reconciliation\"\nDuring this revocable waiting period, the husband may reconcile and resume the marriage if the intention is genuine restoration of peace, not manipulation.\n\n\"Women have rights similar to those of men equitably, although men have a degree ˹of responsibility˺ above them. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise\"\nThis foundational declaration establishes that women possess equal, corresponding moral and legal rights in marriage, while the husband carries an additional degree of financial provider responsibility and household leadership.",
  "229": "\"Divorce may be retracted twice, then the husband must retain ˹his wife˺ with honour or separate ˹from her˺ with grace\"\nIslam dismantled an ancient abusive practice where men repeatedly divorced and recalled their wives endlessly to torment them. Revocable divorce is limited strictly to twice; after the second divorce, the husband must either maintain the marriage with genuine honor and kindness or release her through a graceful, dignified final divorce.\n\n\"It is not lawful for husbands to take back anything of the dowry given to their wives, unless the couple fears not being able to keep within the limits of Allah\"\nA husband is strictly forbidden from demanding back any part of the dowry or gifts he previously gave his wife upon divorce.\n\n\"So if you fear they will not be able to keep within the limits of Allah, there is no blame if the wife compensates the husband to obtain divorce\"\nIf the wife deeply desires to exit an unhappy marriage (khul') and the husband has committed no fault, she may voluntarily return a portion of her dowry to secure an amicable release.\n\n\"These are the limits set by Allah, so do not transgress them. And whoever transgresses the limits of Allah, they are the ˹true˺ wrongdoers\"\nThese boundaries protect the dignity of both spouses, and violating them constitutes grave injustice.",
  "230": "\"So if a husband divorces his wife ˹three times˺, then it is not lawful for him to remarry her until after she has married another man and then is divorced\"\nIf a husband issues a third, irrevocable divorce, the marriage is permanently severed. They cannot remarry unless the woman naturally marries another man in a genuine, unmanipulated marriage and that subsequent marriage later ends naturally through death or valid divorce.\n\n\"Then it is permissible for them to reunite, as long as they feel they are able to maintain the limits of Allah. These are the limits set by Allah, which He makes clear for people of knowledge\"\nThis strict rule serves as a powerful deterrent against reckless, impulsive divorces, protecting marriage from being treated as a casual game.",
  "231": "\"When you divorce women and they have ˹almost˺ reached the end of their waiting period, either retain them honourably or let them go honourably\"\nAs the waiting period approaches its end, the husband must make a clear, honorable decision: either genuinely reconcile with love and mutual respect, or allow the period to expire so she can move on with complete freedom and dignity.\n\n\"But do not retain them ˹only˺ to harm them ˹or˺ to take advantage ˹of them˺. Whoever does that surely wrongs his own soul\"\nTaking a wife back on the final day solely to prolong her waiting period and torment her is a severe sin that harms the oppressor's own spiritual soul.\n\n\"Do not take Allah’s revelations lightly. Remember Allah’s favours upon you as well as the Book and wisdom He has sent down for your guidance. Be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things\"\nManipulating sacred marital laws is treating divine revelation as a joke. Believers are urged to remember God's grace and live with deep reverence before the All-Knowing Lord.",
  "232": "\"When you have divorced women and they have reached the end of their waiting period, do not prevent them from re-marrying their ex-husbands if they come to an honourable agreement\"\nGuardians and relatives are strictly forbidden from obstructing a divorced woman from remarrying her former husband if both partners have resolved their past differences and mutually agree on a new, honorable marriage contract.\n\n\"This is enjoined on whoever has faith in Allah and the Last Day. This is purer and more dignifying for you. Allah knows and you do not know\"\nFacilitating lawful reconciliation and respecting a woman's autonomous choice brings greater social purity, emotional dignity, and family peace.",
  "233": "\"˹Divorced˺ mothers will breastfeed their offspring for two whole years, for those who wish to complete the nursing ˹of their child˺\"\nIslam recognizes the supreme physiological and emotional value of maternal breastfeeding, recommending up to two full years for optimal infant development.\n\n\"The child’s father will provide reasonable maintenance and clothing for the mother ˹during that period˺\"\nEven if divorced, the father is legally obligated to provide full financial support, housing, and clothing for the nursing mother according to customary fair standards.\n\n\"No one will be charged with more than they can bear. No mother or father should be made to suffer for their child. The ˹father’s˺ heirs are under the same obligation\"\nNeither parent may use the infant as a weapon to emotionally or financially exploit the other. If the father passes away, his heirs inherit the duty to maintain the child.\n\n\"But if both sides decide—after mutual consultation and consent—to wean a child, then there is no blame on them. And if you decide to have your children nursed by a wet-nurse, it is permissible as long as you pay fairly. Be mindful of Allah, and know that Allah is All-Seeing of what you do\"\nParents may decide to wean earlier through mutual consultation and consent based on the child's best interests, or hire a wet-nurse with fair wages, always remaining mindful of God's watchful gaze.",
  "234": "\"As for those of you who die and leave widows behind, let them observe a waiting period of four months and ten days\"\nA widow observes a waiting period of four lunar months and ten days, providing sufficient time to ensure there is no pregnancy and allowing an honorable period of emotional mourning and healing.\n\n\"When they have reached the end of this period, then you are not accountable for what they decide for themselves in a reasonable manner. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do\"\nOnce the waiting period concludes, the widow has complete autonomous legal freedom to dress as she pleases, socialize, and remarry without interference or social stigma from her deceased husband's family.",
  "235": "\"There is no blame on you for subtly showing interest in ˹divorced or widowed˺ women or for hiding ˹the intention˺ in your hearts\"\nDuring a widow's waiting period, potential suitors may subtly hint at marriage intentions or hold silent hope in their hearts without committing any sin.\n\n\"Allah knows that you are considering them ˹for marriage˺. But do not make a secret commitment with them—you can only show interest in them appropriately\"\nGod understands human romantic inclinations, but strictly forbids secret promises, private romantic meetings, or inappropriate commitments during the mourning period.\n\n\"Do not commit to the bond of marriage until the waiting period expires. Know that Allah is aware of what is in your hearts, so beware of Him. And know that Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing\"\nNo formal marriage contract may be signed until the waiting period officially ends, reminding humanity that God knows every secret intention and treats human weakness with forbearance.",
  "236": "\"There is no blame if you divorce women before the marriage is consummated or the dowry is settled\"\nIf a marriage is dissolved before physical consummation and before a specific dowry was stipulated, the divorce is valid.\n\n\"But give them a ˹suitable˺ compensation—the rich according to his means and the poor according to his. A reasonable compensation is an obligation on the good-doers\"\nTo heal the emotional disappointment and honor the woman's dignity, the husband is obligated to provide a generous consolatory gift (mut'ah), with the wealthy giving according to their means and the less affluent giving within their capacity.",
  "237": "\"And if you divorce them before consummating the marriage but after deciding on a dowry, pay half of the dowry\"\nIf a specific dowry was agreed upon and divorce occurs before consummation, the husband must pay exactly half of the stipulated dowry.\n\n\"unless the wife graciously waives it or the husband graciously pays in full. Graciousness is closer to righteousness\"\nEither party is encouraged to practice higher moral grace: the wife may generously waive her half, or the husband may magnanimously pay the full dowry.\n\n\"And do not forget kindness among yourselves. Surely Allah is All-Seeing of what you do\"\nEven when relationships end, former partners are urged never to forget past kindness, mutual respect, and generosity, for God sees every noble deed.",
  "238": "\"Observe the ˹five obligatory˺ prayers—especially the middle prayer—and stand in true devotion to Allah\"\nBelievers are commanded to punctually maintain the daily prayers with heart presence, with special emphasis on the 'middle prayer' (the Asr late-afternoon prayer, which occurs during the busiest time of daily business), standing in deep spiritual reverence before God.",
  "239": "\"If you are in danger, pray on foot or while riding\"\nIslam provides remarkable adaptability: in situations of active danger, war, or fear, believers may pray while walking or riding, facing whatever direction is possible, through head gestures.\n\n\"But when you are safe, ˹take time to˺ remember Allah for teaching you what you did not know\"\nOnce security returns, prayers must be performed in their full, tranquil postures with gratitude to God for the blessing of divine guidance.",
  "240": "\"Those of you who die leaving widows should bequeath for them a year’s maintenance without forcing them out\"\nIn the early transitional period before the final inheritance shares of Surah An-Nisa were revealed, husbands were encouraged to provide one full year of housing and maintenance for their widows.\n\n\"But if they choose to leave, you are not accountable for what they reasonably decide for themselves. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise\"\nIf the widow chose to move out earlier, the family incurred no blame, as divine wisdom established autonomy, dignity, and care for widows.",
  "241": "\"Reasonable provisions must be made for divorced women—a duty on those mindful ˹of Allah˺\"\nGod establishes a general ethical rule: every divorced woman is entitled to a fair consolatory provision or financial assistance from her former husband, serving as a moral duty upon all who cultivate God-consciousness and compassion.",
  "242": "\"This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you, so perhaps you will understand\"\nGod clarifies family laws, social ethics, and moral boundaries in crystal-clear language so humanity can comprehend the profound divine wisdom behind justice and peace.",
  "243": "\"Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who fled their homes in the thousands for fear of death?\"\nGod refers to an ancient community who fled their hometown in the thousands to escape a plague or military duty, foolishly imagining that running away could evade their appointed lifespan.\n\n\"Allah said to them, “Die!” then He gave them life. Surely Allah is ever Bountiful to humanity, but most people are ungrateful\"\nGod commanded them to die instantly, and then miraculously revived them after a period of death, teaching that lifespans are governed solely by God, who is bountiful to humanity despite widespread ingratitude.",
  "244": "\"Fight in the cause of Allah, and know that Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing\"\nBelievers are commanded to defend justice and resist oppression with courage, knowing that avoiding duty cannot prolong life, and that God hears all prayers and knows all intentions.",
  "245": "\"Who will lend to Allah a good loan which Allah will multiply many times over?\"\nGod uses the tender, beautiful metaphor of a 'goodly loan' to describe voluntary charity given to the poor. Spending wealth for God's sake is rewarded with exponential multiplication in this world and the Hereafter.\n\n\"It is Allah ˹alone˺ who decreases and increases ˹wealth˺. And to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned\"\nEconomic provisions are expanded or restricted solely by divine decree; giving charity never decreases true wealth, and all souls will return to God for full reward.",
  "246": "\"Have you not seen those chiefs of the Children of Israel after Moses? They said to one of their prophets, “Appoint for us a king, ˹and˺ we will fight in the cause of Allah.”\"\nFollowing Moses, the leaders of Israel asked their prophet (Samuel) to appoint a commander to unite them against their oppressors (the Philistines).\n\n\"He said, “Are you not going to cower if ordered to fight?” They replied, “How could we refuse to fight in the cause of Allah, while we were driven out of our homes and ˹separated from˺ our children?” But when they were ordered to fight, they fled, except for a few of them. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the wrongdoers\"\nDespite their bold initial enthusiasm, when military duty was commanded, the majority retreated in fear, leaving only a faithful few, proving that true courage is shown in action, not words.",
  "247": "\"Their prophet told them, “Allah has appointed Saul to be your king.” They protested, “How can he be our king when some of us are more deserving of kingship than he, and he has not been blessed with vast riches?”\"\nWhen Samuel announced Saul (Talut) as king, the tribal chiefs objected based on elitism and wealth, claiming royal lineage and riches made them more entitled to rule.\n\n\"He replied, “Allah has chosen him over you and blessed him with knowledge and stature. Allah grants kingship to whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.”\"\nTrue leadership is defined by strategic intellect, deep wisdom, integrity, and physical capability, not inherited fortune, and God appoints authority with perfect wisdom.",
  "248": "\"Their prophet further told them, “The sign of Saul’s kingship is that the Ark will come to you—containing reassurance from your Lord and relics of the family of Moses and the family of Aaron, which will be carried by the angels\"\nAs visible divine confirmation of Saul's authority, angels returned the lost sacred Ark of the Covenant, containing spiritual relics and divine reassurance, proving his appointed leadership.\n\n\"Surely in this is a sign for you, if you ˹truly˺ believe.”\"\nThis miraculous return served as undeniable proof of God's support for all sincere believers.",
  "249": "\"When Saul marched forth with his army, he cautioned: “Allah will test you with a river. So whoever drinks ˹his fill˺ from it is not with me, and whoever does not taste it—except a sip from the hollow of his hands—is definitely with me.” They all drank ˹their fill˺ except for a few!\"\nSaul tested their discipline and self-control at a river: soldiers were forbidden from drinking excessively, permitted only a single handful of water. The majority failed the test of discipline, drinking their fill and losing their physical stamina.\n\n\"When he and the ˹remaining˺ faithful with him crossed the river, they said, “Now we are no match for Goliath and his warriors.” But those ˹believers˺ who were certain they would meet Allah reasoned, “How many times has a small force vanquished a mighty army by the Will of Allah! And Allah is ˹always˺ with the steadfast.”\"\nFaced with Goliath's massive, heavily armored army, the faint-hearted panicked, but the sincere believers anchored in certainty of the Hereafter proclaimed that victory depends on divine aid, not numerical superiority.",
  "250": "\"When they advanced to face Goliath and his warriors, they prayed, “Our Lord! Shower us with perseverance, make our steps firm, and give us victory over the disbelieving people.”\"\nAs the two armies closed in battle, the small faithful force turned to God in profound prayer, asking for overflowing patience, steadfast courage on the battlefield, and divine victory over tyranny.",
  "251": "\"So they defeated them by Allah’s Will, and David killed Goliath\"\nGod granted victory to the small, disciplined army. The young, courageous David stepped forward and slew the tyrannical giant Goliath with a simple sling, proving that tyrant power collapses before sincere faith.\n\n\"And Allah blessed David with kingship and wisdom and taught him what He willed\"\nGod elevated David to prophetic kingship, deep wisdom, and mastery of ironworking and psalmody.\n\n\"Had Allah not repelled a group of people by ˹the might of˺ another, corruption would have dominated the earth, but Allah is Gracious to all\"\nGod maintains cosmic and social balance by using righteous forces to check and repel tyranny, preserving the earth from total corruption through His universal grace.",
  "252": "\"These are Allah’s revelations which We recite to you ˹O Prophet˺ in truth. And you are truly one of the messengers\"\nThese detailed, authentic historical narratives are divine revelations recited with absolute truth, proving beyond doubt that the Prophet Muhammad is a true messenger of God.",
  "253": "\"We have chosen some of those messengers above others. Allah spoke directly to some, and raised some high in rank. To Jesus, son of Mary, We gave clear proofs and supported him with the holy spirit\"\nAll prophets share the same foundational mission of monotheism, yet God elevated specific messengers with unique distinctions: speaking directly to Moses, elevating Muhammad to the highest rank, and empowering Jesus with undeniable miracles through the Angel Gabriel.\n\n\"If Allah had willed, succeeding generations would not have fought ˹among themselves˺ after receiving the clear proofs. But they differed—some believed while others disbelieved. Yet if Allah had willed, they would not have fought one another. But Allah does what He wills\"\nGod endowed humanity with free will and moral choice; sectarian conflicts arose because people freely chose disbelief and rivalry, all within the overarching permission and wisdom of divine decree.",
  "254": "\"O believers! Donate from what We have provided for you before the arrival of a Day when there will be no bargaining, friendship, or intercession\"\nBelievers are urged to spend generously in charity in this life before the Day of Judgment arrives—a day when worldly wealth cannot purchase safety, friendship cannot avert justice, and unauthorized intercession cannot save a persistent wrongdoer.\n\n\"Those who disbelieve are ˹truly˺ the wrongdoers\"\nThose who reject truth and hoard wealth commit the ultimate injustice against their own souls.",
  "255": "\"Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining\"\nThis is the famous Ayat al-Kursi (the Verse of the Throne), the greatest single verse in the Quran. It begins with the supreme declaration of pure monotheism and God's eternal, self-subsisting life and continuous maintenance of all creation.\n\n\"Neither drowsiness nor sleep overtakes Him. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth\"\nGod is entirely free from all human limitations, fatigue, and slumber, possessing absolute, unquestioned ownership over the entire cosmos.\n\n\"Who could possibly intercede with Him without His permission? He ˹fully˺ knows what is ahead of them and what is behind them, but no one can grasp any of His knowledge—except what He wills ˹to reveal˺\"\nNo created being can speak or intercede before God without His explicit permission, and His infinite knowledge encompasses past, present, and future, while creatures comprehend only what He chooses to reveal.\n\n\"His Seat encompasses the heavens and the earth, and the preservation of both does not tire Him. For He is the Most High, the Greatest\"\nHis celestial Seat (Kursi) spans the entire universe, and preserving billions of galaxies requires zero effort or fatigue for the Almighty, who is supreme in majesty and perfection.",
  "256": "\"Let there be no compulsion in religion, for the truth stands out clearly from falsehood\"\nIslam establishes the foundational principle of absolute freedom of conscience: faith cannot be forced upon anyone by violence or coercion, because truth has been made distinct and compelling through reason and revelation.\n\n\"So whoever renounces false gods and believes in Allah has certainly grasped the firmest, unfailing hand-hold. And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing\"\nRejecting idols, tyranny, and superstition while placing sincere faith in God connects the soul to an unbreakable spiritual lifeline that will never fail.",
  "257": "\"Allah is the Guardian of the believers—He brings them out of darkness and into light\"\nGod is the loving Protector and Guardian of sincere believers, guiding them out of the spiritual darkness of doubt, anxiety, and confusion into the radiant light of certainty, peace, and moral purpose.\n\n\"As for the disbelievers, their guardians are false gods who lead them out of light and into darkness. It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever\"\nThose who reject God take false idols, tyrants, and demonic desires as their guardians, which drag them out of truth into deep moral darkness and eternal loss in the Fire.",
  "258": "\"Are you ˹O Prophet˺ not aware of the one who argued with Abraham about his Lord because Allah had granted him kingship?\"\nGod recalls the historical debate between Prophet Abraham and the tyrannical Babylonian king (Nimrod), who became intoxicated with arrogance due to his vast worldly power.\n\n\"˹Remember˺ when Abraham said, “My Lord is the One Who has power to give life and cause death.” He argued, “I too have the power to give life and cause death.”\"\nNimrod foolishly claimed he had power over life and death by arbitrarily executing one prisoner and pardoning another.\n\n\"Abraham challenged ˹him˺, “Allah causes the sun to rise from the east. So make it rise from the west.” And so the disbeliever was dumbstruck. And Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people\"\nAbraham presented a simple, undeniable cosmic proof: if you claim divinity, reverse the cosmic order and make the sun rise from the west! The tyrant was rendered completely speechless, proving human powerlessness before the Creator.",
  "259": "\"Or ˹are you not aware of˺ the one who passed by a city which was in ruins. He wondered, “How could Allah bring this back to life after its destruction?”\"\nGod presents another historical sign (often associated with Ezra): a righteous man passed by the destroyed ruins of Jerusalem and wondered how God would restore such devastation to life.\n\n\"So Allah caused him to die for a hundred years then brought him back to life. Allah asked, “How long have you remained ˹in this state˺?” He replied, “Perhaps a day or part of a day.”\"\nGod caused him to die for a full century and then revived him, making the hundred years feel like a brief afternoon nap.\n\n\"Allah said, “No! You have remained here for a hundred years! Just look at your food and drink— they have not spoiled. ˹But now˺ look at ˹the remains of˺ your donkey! And ˹so˺ We have made you into a sign for humanity. And look at the bones ˹of the donkey˺, how We bring them together then clothe them with flesh!” When this was made clear to him, he declared, “˹Now˺ I know that Allah is Most Capable of everything.”\"\nGod demonstrated His power through two simultaneous miracles: preserving perishable food and drink fresh for a century without rotting, while reassembling the decomposed skeleton of his donkey and clothing it with flesh before his eyes, proving God's absolute power to resurrect the dead.",
  "260": "\"And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said, “My Lord! Show me how you give life to the dead.” Allah responded, “Do you not believe?” Abraham replied, “Yes I do, but just so my heart can be reassured.”\"\nAbraham's request was not rooted in doubt, but in a desire to move from intellectual certainty to direct visual tranquility and eyewitness experience of divine resurrection.\n\n\"Allah said, “Then bring four birds, train them to come to you, ˹then cut them into pieces,˺ and scatter them on different hilltops. Then call them back, they will fly to you in haste. And ˹so you will˺ know that Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.”\"\nGod instructed Abraham to train four birds, sacrifice them, mix their parts, and place portions on distant hilltops. Upon calling them, the scattered remains instantly reassembled and the living birds flew swiftly to Abraham, demonstrating that resurrecting billions of scattered human remains on the Day of Judgment is effortless for the Almighty.",
  "261": "The magnificent parable of exponential divine multiplication for charitable spending is proclaimed in 'The example of those who spend their wealth in the cause of Allah is that of a grain that sprouts into seven ears, each bearing one hundred grains. And Allah multiplies ˹the reward even more˺ to whoever He wills. For Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.'\n\nThe Quran uses a breathtaking agricultural metaphor to illustrate the immense spiritual economics of charity: 'The example of those who spend their wealth in the cause of Allah is that of a grain that sprouts into seven ears, each bearing one hundred grains.' A single small grain planted in fertile soil produces seven ears of corn, with each ear containing one hundred grains, yielding a seven-hundred-fold return from a single seed!\n\nIn the exact same way, a single lawful dollar or meal given sincerely in God's cause yields a minimum of seven hundred times its value in divine reward and spiritual blessings in the Hereafter.\n\nThe verse reveals that divine generosity is not capped at seven hundred: 'And Allah multiplies ˹the reward even more˺ to whoever He wills' ('wa Allahu yuda'ifu li-man yasha''). Depending on the giver's sincerity, sacrifice, and the urgency of the recipient's need, God multiplies the reward thousands of times over.\n\nThe verse concludes with comforting attributes: 'For Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing' ('wa Allahu Wasi'un 'Alim'). God's treasury of wealth is infinite and inexhaustible, and He is fully aware of every pure intention.\n\nThis verse inspires boundless generosity, assuring believers that wealth given in God's cause is never lost, but eternally multiplied.\n\nThe exponential multiplication of charity demonstrates that giving is the ultimate form of spiritual investment. True wealth is not what we accumulate and keep, but what we generously give away for the sake of God. This extraordinary multiplication of reward inspires believers to make generosity a daily habit, knowing that every act of charity brings blessing into this life and eternal abundance in the next.",
  "262": "The ethical conditions for preserving the spiritual reward of charity are detailed in 'Those who spend their wealth in the cause of Allah and do not follow their charity with reminders of their generosity or hurtful words—they will get their reward from their Lord, and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.'\n\nThe verse establishes that the acceptance of charitable deeds depends not only on the physical act of giving, but also on the moral purity of how the recipient is treated afterward: 'do not follow their charity with reminders of their generosity or hurtful words.'\n\nThe Quran identifies two destructive behaviors that completely ruin charity:\nFirst, 'reminders of generosity' ('mannan'), which means constantly reminding the recipient of the favor, boasting publicly about helping them, or making them feel indebted and obligated;\nSecond, 'hurtful words' ('adha'), which means insulting, humiliating, or condescending to the poor person, treating them with disdain, or violating their privacy and dignity.\n\nThose who give purely for God, preserving the recipient's honor with gentleness and humility, 'will get their reward from their Lord.'\n\nThey are guaranteed the ultimate reward: 'and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve' ('wa la khawfun 'alayhim wa la hum yahzanun').\n\nClassical scholars emphasize that charity in Islam is a spiritual service, not a tool for social dominance. Giving with humility protects both the recipient's dignity and the giver's eternal reward, ensuring that philanthropy is an instrument of pure mercy.\n\nProtecting the feelings and honor of the poor is the highest mark of spiritual refinement. When charity is accompanied by gentleness and humility, it heals hearts and earns the everlasting pleasure of our Lord. Sincere giving without condescension or hurtful reminders purifies the soul of the giver, establishing an enduring atmosphere of brotherhood, mutual honor, and communal flourishing under divine mercy.",
  "263": "The moral superiority of dignified speech and forgiveness over tainted charity is proclaimed in 'Kind words and forgiveness are better than charity followed by injury. And Allah is Self-Sufficient, Most Forbearing.'\n\nThe verse articulates a profound ethical hierarchy in Islamic interpersonal relations: 'Kind words and forgiveness are better than charity followed by injury' ('Qawlun ma'rufun wa maghfiratun khayrun min sadaqatin yatba'uha adha').\n\nA 'kind word' ('qawlun ma'ruf')—such as a gentle, polite response, a sincere prayer, or an encouraging smile when one cannot afford to give financial aid—coupled with 'forgiveness' ('maghfirah')—overlooking the rudeness, persistence, or impatience of an impoverished petitioner—is vastly superior in the sight of God to giving bags of gold accompanied by arrogance, insults, or public humiliation. Material aid never justifies emotional cruelty.\n\nThe verse concludes by revealing two magnificent divine names: 'And Allah is Self-Sufficient, Most Forbearing' ('wa Allahu Ghaniyyun Halim'). God is 'Ghaniyy,' entirely independent of all human donations and in no need of charity that harms His servants, and 'Halim,' exercising magnificent patience with human ingratitude and shortcomings, continuing to nourish even those who sin.\n\nThis verse teaches that maintaining human dignity, emotional sensitivity, and courteous speech is the foundational obligation of every believer, proving that a gentle tongue is worth far more than tainted wealth.\n\nA gentle word, a warm smile, and patient forgiveness elevate human society far more than arrogant donations. True wealth of character is measured by the kindness of our speech and the purity of our intentions. Embodying patience and speaking gentle words during difficult interactions reflects true moral excellence, reminding believers that maintaining human dignity is far more valuable than material generosity tainted by arrogance, ensuring that our interactions remain anchored in divine compassion, humility, and profound respect for all human beings, earning the everlasting pleasure and reward of our Creator.",
  "264": "The devastating parable of ruined charity through showmanship and insult is detailed in 'O believers! Do not waste your charity with reminders ˹of your generosity˺ or hurtful words, like those who donate their wealth just to show off and do not believe in Allah or the Last Day. Their example is that of a hard barren rock covered with a thin layer of soil hit by a strong rain— leaving it just a bare stone. Such people are unable to preserve the reward of their charity. Allah does not guide ˹such˺ disbelieving people.'\n\nThe verse issues an urgent command to the faithful: 'Do not waste your charity with reminders ˹of your generosity˺ or hurtful words' ('la tubtilu sadaqatikum bi-al-manni wa-al-adha'). Reminding recipients of favors and insulting them literally invalidates and erases ('tubtilu') all spiritual reward, rendering the good deed completely worthless.\n\nThe Quran compares this destructive behavior to the vanity of hypocrites who donate 'just to show off' ('riya'an') to gain social praise and applause while lacking sincere faith in God and the Hereafter.\n\nThe verse presents a vivid geological parable: their hypocritical deed is like a smooth, hard rock ('safwan') covered with a thin layer of dust or soil ('turab'). An onlooker might assume it is fertile ground ready to sprout crops; but when a torrential rainstorm ('wabil') strikes, the loose dust is completely washed away, leaving behind only the cold, barren, lifeless stone ('saldan').\n\nIn the exact same way, the torrential reality of the Day of Judgment will wash away all superficial worldly deeds, leaving the show-off with zero reward. This passage warns against ostentation and arrogance, calling believers to pure, hidden sincerity in all giving.\n\nGuarding our good deeds against the destructive poisons of ostentation and pride is essential for spiritual survival, ensuring that our strivings are preserved as eternal treasures on the Day of Judgment.",
  "265": "The beautiful parable of sincere, multiplied charity is presented in 'And the example of those who donate their wealth, seeking Allah’s pleasure and believing the reward is certain, is that of a garden on a fertile hill: when heavy rain falls, it yields up twice its normal produce. If no heavy rain falls, a drizzle is sufficient. And Allah is All-Seeing of what you do.'\n\nIn radiant contrast to the barren rock of the hypocrite in the previous verse, the Quran portrays the sincere believer's charity: 'seeking Allah’s pleasure and believing the reward is certain' ('ibtigha'a mardati Allahi wa tathbitan min anfusihim'). They give out of pure love for God and firm, unwavering conviction in divine promise.\n\nTheir deed is compared to 'a garden on a fertile hill' ('jannatin bi-rabwah')—an elevated orchard that receives abundant sunlight, fresh breezes, and nutrient-rich soil.\n\nThe agricultural blessing is twofold: 'when heavy rain falls, it yields up twice its normal produce' ('atath ukulaha di'fayn'). If abundant wealth is spent with sincere intention, God multiplies the harvest of rewards exponentially.\n\nEven if abundant resources are unavailable: 'If no heavy rain falls, a drizzle is sufficient' ('fa-in lam yusibha wabilun fa-tall'). Even a small, humble donation—like a single coin or a piece of bread—flourishes and bears magnificent fruit because of the elevated sincerity of the giver's heart.\n\nThe verse closes: 'And Allah is All-Seeing of what you do' ('wa Allahu bi-ma ta'maluna Basir'). God sees the purity of every intention and guarantees an abundant harvest for sincere charity.\n\nSincere charity, whether abundant or modest, is nurtured by divine grace into a magnificent harvest of blessings, filling the life of the giver with peace, purpose, and unshakeable divine favor. Cultivating sincere conviction in divine promises ensures that our good deeds flourish, filling life with spiritual purpose and assuring the soul of an abundant harvest on the Day of Reckoning.",
  "266": "The poignant warning against losing one's life's work through sudden destruction is detailed in 'Would any of you wish to have a garden with palm trees, grapevines, and all kinds of fruits with rivers flowing underneath and as they grow very old with dependent children, a fiery whirlwind hits the garden, burning it all up? This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you, so perhaps you will reflect.'\n\nIn an authentic narration in Sahih al-Bukhari, Caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab asked the Companions about the meaning of this verse, and Ibn Abbas explained it with profound clarity: this is an analogy for a person who performed good deeds throughout their entire life, but at the end of their life, they ruined all their deeds through arrogance, reminders of generosity, and major sins, leaving themselves destitute on the Day of Judgment.\n\nThe parable depicts an elderly man who possesses a magnificent, fertile orchard filled with dates, grapes, and flowing rivers—representing a lifetime of accumulated good deeds. He reaches extreme old age ('kibar'), becomes physically helpless, and has young, vulnerable, dependent children ('dhurriyyatun du'afa'') who rely entirely on the garden for survival.\n\nSuddenly, a terrifying whirlwind containing blazing fire ('i'sarun fihi narun') strikes the garden, incinerating every tree and leaving behind only ash! In that moment of utter catastrophe, the old man has neither the strength to rebuild nor any other source of survival.\n\nThe Quran asks: would anyone among you wish for such tragic ruin?\n\nThe verse concludes: 'This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you, so perhaps you will reflect,' urging believers to protect their good deeds with lifelong humility and sincere devotion.\n\nProtecting our lifetime of righteous deeds through steadfast humility and continuous vigilance safeguards the soul from sudden spiritual bankruptcy, ensuring that we meet our Lord with full hands and peaceful hearts.",
  "267": "The divine standard of quality and sincerity in charitable donations is commanded in 'O believers! Donate from the best of what you have earned and of what We have produced for you from the earth. Do not pick out worthless things for donation, which you yourselves would only accept with closed eyes. And know that Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.'\n\nAddressing the community of faith ('O believers!'), God establishes the standard of what should be offered in charity: 'Donate from the best of what you have earned and of what We have produced for you from the earth' ('anfiqu min tayyibati ma kasabtum'). Charity must come from lawful, pure earnings ('tayyibat') and the wholesome fruits and crops provided by God.\n\nThe verse directly corrects a poor practice: 'Do not pick out worthless things for donation, which you yourselves would only accept with closed eyes' ('wa la tayammamu al-khabitha minhu tunfiqun'). In Medina, some people brought dry, rotten, unpalatable dates to hang in the mosque for the poor, keeping the fresh, sweet dates for themselves. God rebukes this disrespect: if someone offered you such rotten food as a payment or gift, you would only accept it with embarrassment, disgust, or closed eyes ('ighmad')! How then could you offer what is worthless to your Creator?\n\nThe verse concludes with a profound reminder: 'And know that Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy' ('wa-i'lamu anna Allaha Ghaniyyun Hamid'). God is in no need of our charity; giving our best is for our own spiritual purification and honor.\n\nThis passage teaches that true love of God is demonstrated by giving the best of what we possess to those in need.\n\nOffering the best of our wealth to the poor reflects the highest standard of love and gratitude to God, transforming charity into a profound act of spiritual elevation and heartfelt worship.",
  "268": "The psychological contrast between satanic fear and divine abundance is revealed in 'The Devil threatens you with ˹the prospect of˺ poverty and bids you to the shameful deed ˹of stinginess˺, while Allah promises you forgiveness and ˹great˺ bounties from Him. And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.'\n\nThe verse exposes the internal spiritual battle surrounding charitable giving: 'The Devil threatens you with ˹the prospect of˺ poverty' ('Al-Shaytanu ya'idukumu al-faqr'). Whenever a person considers donating generously to a noble cause, helping an orphan, or paying their zakah, Satan immediately whispers anxieties: 'If you give this money away, you will become poor, you will not afford future luxuries, or an emergency will ruin you!'\n\nDriven by this manufactured fear of scarcity, Satan 'bids you to the shameful deed ˹of stinginess˺' ('wa ya'murukum bi-al-fahsha''), compelling a person into miserliness, greed, and accumulating wealth through unlawful means.\n\nIn radiant contrast, God offers a magnificent dual promise: 'while Allah promises you forgiveness and ˹great˺ bounties from Him' ('wa Allahu ya'idukum maghfiratan minhu wa fadla'). God promises to erase past sins through the expiation of charity, and guarantees 'bounties' ('fadl')—expanding lawful wealth in this world, infusing life with barakah (divine blessing), and preparing eternal treasures in Paradise.\n\nThe verse closes: 'And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing' ('wa Allahu Wasi'un 'Alim'). God's resources are boundless, and He knows the sincere generosity of every heart.\n\nThis verse empowers the believer to dismiss satanic fear and give with joyful confidence in God's infinite promises.\n\nRejecting satanic whispers of scarcity and trusting in God's promises of abundance fills life with generosity, inner peace, and the radiant blessings of divine mercy in both worlds. Choosing divine abundance over satanic fear transforms our relationship with wealth, inspiring a life of joyful generosity, profound gratitude, and continuous reliance upon the All-Bountiful Creator across all seasons of life, experiencing the true peace of selfless giving.",
  "269": "The supreme divine gift of wisdom and intellectual discernment is celebrated in 'Allah grants wisdom to whoever He wills. And whoever is granted wisdom is certainly blessed with a great privilege. But none will be mindful ˹of this˺ except people of reason.'\n\nThe verse reveals the highest intellectual and spiritual blessing bestowed upon humanity: 'Allah grants wisdom to whoever He wills' ('Yu'ti al-hikmata man yasha''). The Arabic concept of 'al-hikmah' (wisdom) is far deeper than mere factual knowledge or clever intellect; classical scholars like Ibn Abbas, Mujahid, and al-Hasan al-Basri explain that wisdom is deep understanding of the Quran, sound moral discernment, putting knowledge into righteous action, and placing everything in its rightful, balanced place.\n\nThe Quran proclaims the immense value of this gift: 'And whoever is granted wisdom is certainly blessed with a great privilege' ('wa man yu'ta al-hikmata fa-qad utiya khayran kathira'). Possessing wisdom is greater than possessing kingdoms of gold and silver, because a person with wealth but without wisdom will destroy themselves, whereas a person with wisdom will navigate life's trials with peace, manage wealth with justice, and achieve eternal salvation.\n\nThe verse concludes: 'But none will be mindful ˹of this˺ except people of reason' ('wa ma yadh-dhakkaru illa ulu al-albab'). Only those who possess deep, reflective intellects appreciate the supreme value of spiritual discernment over material accumulation.\n\nThis verse inspires believers to pray continuously for divine wisdom, allowing their lives to be guided by sound understanding, moral maturity, and profound reflection.\n\nSincere wisdom is the greatest treasure a human being can possess, guiding our choices, speech, and interactions with justice, compassion, and deep grounding in divine truth. Praying for divine wisdom enables the believer to live with balance, justice, and deep insight, illuminating our daily choices and guiding society toward enduring righteousness, justice, and spiritual peace in both this world and the Hereafter.",
  "270": "The certainty of divine awareness and the warning against spiritual betrayal are proclaimed in 'Whatever charities you give or vows you make are surely known to Allah. And the wrongdoers will have no helpers.'\n\nThe verse establishes absolute divine surveillance over all philanthropic and spiritual commitments: 'Whatever charities you give or vows you make are surely known to Allah' ('wa ma anfaqtum min nafaqatin aw nadhartum min nadhrin fa-inna Allaha ya'lamuh').\n\nWhether a donation is small or large, given secretly in the middle of the night or publicly before the community, and whether a solemn religious vow ('nadhr') is fulfilled with integrity or broken with neglect, God is fully aware of the action and the precise motive of the heart. Every sincere gift is recorded for multiplication, and every broken vow is accounted for.\n\nThe verse issues a stern warning to the dishonest and the miserly: 'And the wrongdoers will have no helpers' ('wa ma li-al-zalimina min ansar'). Those who withhold mandatory charity, oppress the poor, exploit orphans, or break their vows to God are classified as 'wrongdoers' ('zalimin'). On the Day of Judgment, no worldly influence, wealth, or patron will have the power to protect or rescue them from divine justice.\n\nClassical commentators emphasize that this verse provides dual motivation: it comforts the sincere giver with the certainty of divine awareness, while restraining the greedy from injustice through the reality of inescapable accountability.\n\nLiving with the constant awareness of God's all-encompassing knowledge purifies our philanthropy, ensuring that our private and public strivings are dedicated purely to the Creator. Sincere fulfillment of our moral vows and charitable duties reflects true spiritual integrity, providing an unshakeable fortress of peace in the conscious awareness of our All-Knowing Lord, ensuring that our lives are governed by sincere devotion, honesty, and steadfast adherence to divine commands.",
  "271": "The balance between public and private charitable giving is detailed in 'To give charity publicly is good, but to give to the poor privately is better for you, and will absolve you of your sins. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.'\n\nThe Quran clarifies the spiritual etiquette of charitable giving: 'To give charity publicly is good' ('In tubdu al-sadaqati fa-ni'imma hiya'). Giving charity openly is praiseworthy when done with sincere intention, because it establishes a visible culture of generosity, inspires others to give, and removes false suspicions regarding mandatory obligations like zakah.\n\nHowever, the Quran reveals the superior spiritual path: 'but to give to the poor privately is better for you' ('wa in tukhfuha wa tu'tuha al-fuqara'a fa-huwa khayrun lakum'). Giving charity in total secrecy is vastly superior because it protects the giver's heart from vanity, pride, and showmanship ('riya''), while fiercely preserving the dignity, privacy, and self-respect of the impoverished recipient.\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, the Prophet Muhammad explained that among the seven categories of people shaded by God's Throne on the Day of Judgment is 'a person who gives charity so secretly that their left hand does not know what their right hand gave.'\n\nGod promises a magnificent reward: 'and will absolve you of your sins' ('wa yukaffiru 'ankum min sayyi'atikum'). Sincere secret charity literally extinguishes sins just as water extinguishes fire.\n\nThe verse closes: 'And Allah is All-Aware of what you do,' affirming that every hidden gift is known and cherished by God.\n\nPracticing secret charity protects the human heart from vanity while fiercely defending the dignity of the poor, creating a society anchored in genuine compassion and moral excellence. Protecting the privacy of the poor and purifying our hearts from the desire for human applause ensures that our charity remains purely for the sake of God, earning divine forgiveness and eternal honor.",
  "272": "The universal scope of humanitarian compassion and the guarantee of full repayment are proclaimed in 'You are not responsible for people’s guidance ˹O Prophet˺—it is Allah Who guides whoever He wills. Whatever you ˹believers˺ spend in charity, it is for your own good—as long as you do so seeking the pleasure of Allah. Whatever you donate will be paid back to you in full, and you will not be wronged.'\n\nIn Medina, when some Muslims hesitated to give voluntary charity to poor non-Muslim relatives or destitute polytheists, hoping that withholding aid might pressure them into accepting Islam, this verse was revealed to correct their perspective.\n\nGod reassures the Prophet and the community: 'You are not responsible for people’s guidance ˹O Prophet˺—it is Allah Who guides whoever He wills.' Humanitarian charity should not be weaponized as a tool of financial coercion. Sincere believers are encouraged to feed the hungry and help the destitute purely out of human compassion, leaving the guidance of hearts to God.\n\nThe verse reminds the giver of the true beneficiary: 'Whatever you ˹believers˺ spend in charity, it is for your own good—as long as you do so seeking the pleasure of Allah' ('wa ma tunfiqu min khayrin fa-li-anfusikum'). Giving charity benefits the giver far more than the recipient: it cleanses the soul from greed, expands provision, and builds eternal palaces in Paradise.\n\nGod delivers an absolute guarantee: 'Whatever you donate will be paid back to you in full, and you will not be wronged' ('yuwaffa ilaykum wa antum la tuzlamun').\n\nThis verse establishes universal humanitarian ethics in Islam, commanding believers to show mercy to all creation purely for the love of God.\n\nShowing universal humanitarian kindness to all people regardless of their background reflects the expansive mercy of Islam, assuring the giver of complete repayment and eternal honor with God.",
  "273": "The noble profile of the dignified poor is depicted in '˹Charity is˺ for the needy who are too engaged in the cause of Allah to move about in the land ˹for work˺. Those unfamiliar with their situation will think they are not in need ˹of charity˺ because they do not beg. You can recognize them by their appearance. They do not beg people persistently. Whatever you give in charity is certainly well known to Allah.'\n\nThe verse identifies the most deserving recipients of charity: '˹Charity is˺ for the needy who are too engaged in the cause of Allah to move about in the land ˹for work˺' ('li-al-fuqara'i alladhina uhsiru fi sabili Allah'). Classical commentators explain that this was revealed concerning the companions of Ahl al-Suffah—over four hundred dedicated students who resided on the bench of the Prophet's Mosque, devoting their entire lives to studying revelation and defending the community, having no time for trade.\n\nThe Quran describes their profound dignity and self-respect: 'Those unfamiliar with their situation will think they are not in need ˹of charity˺ because they do not beg' ('ya'sabuhumu al-jahilu aghniya'a mina al-ta'affuf'). The word 'ta'affuf' denotes noble restraint and modesty; despite their extreme hunger, their dignity prevents them from complaining, asking for handouts, or compromising their self-respect.\n\nThe verse urges community discernment: 'You can recognize them by their appearance. They do not beg people persistently' ('la yas'aluna al-nasa ilhafa'). A sensitive, observant believer notices the signs of quiet poverty—worn clothes, pale faces, and quiet humility—and provides for them discreetly.\n\nThe verse closes: 'Whatever you give in charity is certainly well known to Allah.'\n\nThis passage teaches that true charity seeks out the dignified, quiet poor who suffer in silence, honoring their self-respect with discreet, generous assistance.\n\nSeeking out and supporting the dignified, silent poor is the highest form of social responsibility, honoring human self-respect with discreet generosity and compassionate care.",
  "274": "The boundless spiritual reward for continuous and diverse charitable spending is proclaimed in 'Those who spend their wealth in charity day and night, secretly and openly—their reward is with their Lord, and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.'\n\nThe verse honors the dedicated believers who make philanthropy a continuous way of life: 'Those who spend their wealth in charity day and night, secretly and openly' ('alladhina yunfiquna amwalahum bi-al-layli wa-al-nahari sirran wa 'alaniyah'). Classical commentators like Ibn Abbas explain that this verse was revealed praising the noble Companion Ali ibn Abi Talib, who possessed four silver coins: he gave one by night, one by day, one in secret, and one openly, dedicating all his resources to God's pleasure.\n\nThe verse encompasses all dimensions of giving:\nGiving 'by night' in private preserves sincerity and protects the dignity of the poor;\nGiving 'by day' openly establishes a public culture of generosity and inspires others;\nGiving in both circumstances ensures that the poor are supported continuously without delay.\n\nGod delivers an absolute guarantee: 'their reward is with their Lord' ('fa-lahum ajruhum 'inda Rabbihim'). Because their giving was constant and pure, their reward is preserved and multiplied by their Lord.\n\nThe verse concludes with the ultimate promise of salvation: 'and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve' ('wa la khawfun 'alayhim wa la hum yahzanun').\n\nClassical scholars emphasize that this verse closes the extensive section on charity with an inspiring vision of lifelong generosity, assuring believers that wealth given for God transforms into eternal peace and divine intimacy.\n\nMaking charity an ongoing, holistic practice purifies wealth and transforms human character, ensuring that the vulnerable are continuously supported and the giver is eternally honored by God. This ongoing dedication transforms wealth into an unshakeable fortress of spiritual light, inspiring a life lived in boundless charity, compassion, and divine grace.",
  "275": "The severe spiritual and psychological diagnosis of financial exploitation is unveiled in 'Those who consume interest will stand ˹on Judgment Day˺ like those driven to madness by Satan’s touch. That is because they say, “Trade is no different than interest.” But Allah has permitted trading and forbidden interest. Whoever refrains—after having received warning from their Lord—may keep their previous gains, and their case is left to Allah. As for those who persist, it is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.'\n\nThe verse introduces the total, non-negotiable prohibition of usury and interest, known in Arabic as 'riba': 'Those who consume interest will stand ˹on Judgment Day˺ like those driven to madness by Satan’s touch' ('la yaqumuna illa kama yaqumu alladhi yatakhabbatuhu al-shaytanu mina al-mass'). On the Day of Resurrection, usurers will be resurrected staggering, convulsing, and stumbling in complete insanity and humiliation, making their spiritual sickness visible to all creation.\n\nThe Quran exposes their false economic rationale: 'That is because they say, “Trade is no different than interest.”' They attempted to justify exploitative lending by equating it with legitimate commerce.\n\nGod shatters this false equivalence through divine law: 'But Allah has permitted trading and forbidden interest' ('wa ahalla Allahu al-bay'a wa harrama al-riba'). Legitimate trade involves productive labor, risk-sharing, and mutual benefit, whereas interest guarantees risk-free profit to the wealthy lender while transferring all risk and crushing debt onto the vulnerable borrower.\n\nThe verse details the legal transition: those who repent upon hearing the prohibition may keep past gains, but 'As for those who persist, it is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.'\n\nThis passage establishes economic justice as a foundational pillar of Islam, fiercely condemning all systems of financial exploitation.\n\nFreeing economic systems from usurious exploitation establishes justice and protects the vulnerable, ensuring that wealth circulates productively and brings lasting prosperity to the entire community.",
  "276": "The contrasting spiritual and economic destinies of usury and charity are proclaimed in 'Allah has made interest fruitless and charity fruitful. And Allah does not like any ungrateful evildoer.'\n\nThe Quran articulates an immutable divine economic law: 'Allah has made interest fruitless and charity fruitful' ('yamhaqu Allahu al-riba wa yurbi al-sadaqat').\n\nThe Arabic verb 'yamhaq' means to eradicate, destroy, and strip of all blessing. Although interest may appear to increase wealth on paper, God destroys its real-world benefit ('barakah')—leading to societal collapse, economic recessions, financial ruin, family breakdown, and eternal punishment in the Hereafter.\n\nIn radiant contrast, God causes charity to become 'fruitful' ('yurbi al-sadaqat'), multiplying the giver's wealth, infusing life with peace and security, and multiplying its reward thousands of times over in Paradise.\n\nThe verse concludes with a stern moral condemnation: 'And Allah does not like any ungrateful evildoer' ('wa Allahu la yuhibbu kulla kaffarin athim'). The person who deals in usury is described as 'kaffar' (deeply ungrateful to God for divine blessings) and 'athim' (hardened in persistent sin), because they exploit the poverty and desperation of fellow human beings for personal enrichment.\n\nClassical commentators like al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir observe that this verse contrasts two opposing economic philosophies: the capitalist greed of riba that concentrates wealth among the elite, versus the compassionate sharing of sadaqah that circulates wealth and uplifts the entire society.\n\nThis passage teaches that true, enduring prosperity is achieved through ethical commerce and charitable sharing rather than financial exploitation.\n\nRecognizing that true prosperity is bestowed by God inspires ethical trade and charitable generosity, protecting society from the systemic collapse caused by unbridled greed and exploitation. Sincere charity purifies the human soul from greed, elevating society into a sanctuary of mutual support, ethical business, and continuous divine blessings. Aligning our financial strivings with divine values transforms wealth into a lasting source of peace, dignity, and divine light across all areas of life.",
  "277": "The comprehensive portrait of righteous economic and spiritual character is celebrated in 'Indeed, those who believe, do good, establish prayer, and pay alms-tax will receive their reward from their Lord, and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.'\n\nFollowing the condemnation of the usurer in the preceding verses, the Quran presents the luminous alternative of the righteous believer: 'Indeed, those who believe, do good, establish prayer, and pay alms-tax.'\n\nThe verse unites four foundational dimensions of Islamic life:\nFirst, sincere internal faith ('alladhina amanu'), anchoring the heart in monotheism;\nSecond, active righteous deeds ('wa 'amilu al-salihat'), practicing honesty, justice, and moral integrity;\nThird, devoted spiritual worship ('wa aqamu al-salah'), maintaining consistent communion with God;\nFourth, compassionate economic justice ('wa atawu al-zakah'), purifying wealth by sharing mandatory dues with the poor and needy.\n\nThe contrast with the usurer is profound: while the usurer hoards wealth and exploits the vulnerable, the righteous believer purifies wealth through zakah and uplifts the community.\n\nGod guarantees their ultimate success: 'will receive their reward from their Lord, and there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve' ('lahum ajruhum 'inda Rabbihim wa la khawfun 'alayhim wa la hum yahzanun').\n\nClassical scholars explain that combining spiritual prayer with social charity establishes an unshakeable fortress of inner peace, ensuring that life is lived with purpose, dignity, and everlasting divine favor.\n\nHarmonizing personal devotion through prayer with social solidarity through zakah builds a resilient, compassionate society where divine blessings flourish and human dignity is celebrated. Integrating spiritual prayer with economic justice protects the human soul from selfishness, ensuring that our strivings are rewarded with eternal joy, dignity, and divine peace in the Hereafter. Living in accordance with these four pillars elevates human existence, creating strong, harmonious communities rooted in mutual love, social equity, and unwavering faith.",
  "278": "The direct, urgent command to cancel all outstanding interest is proclaimed in 'O believers! Fear Allah, and give up outstanding interest if you are ˹true˺ believers.'\n\nAddressing the community of faith with supreme seriousness ('O believers!'), God commands: 'Fear Allah, and give up outstanding interest if you are ˹true˺ believers' ('ittaqu Allaha wa dharu ma baqiya mina al-riba in kuntum mu'minin').\n\nHistorical commentators like Ibn Abbas and al-Suddi recount that this verse was revealed concerning prominent tribes in Arabia (such as the Banu Thaqif and Banu Makhzum) who had extensive interest-bearing contracts before Islam. When they embraced Islam, they sought to collect the accumulated interest accrued prior to their conversion.\n\nGod intervened with absolute clarity, ordering the immediate and complete cancellation of all remaining, uncollected interest debts. Sincere faith in God demands total cessation of exploitative practices.\n\nThe conditional phrase 'if you are ˹true˺ believers' serves as a profound test of faith: true belief is proven by willing submission to divine commands, especially when it requires surrendering unlawful financial gains.\n\nClassical scholars emphasize that this verse marks a decisive break from the exploitative economic models of the pre-Islamic era, challenging believers in every generation to purge their financial dealings from all forms of usury and unjust enrichment in obedience to God.\n\nSubmitting our financial dealings to divine commands demonstrates the authenticity of faith, freeing the human heart from the destructive pursuit of unlawful wealth and exploitation. Abandoning all forms of interest is a vital test of moral integrity, proving that our faith is grounded in sincere obedience and dedication to universal economic justice across all dealings. Cleansing our wealth from exploitative gains brings true spiritual freedom, ensuring that our sustenance is blessed, pure, and pleasing to the Almighty in every way, establishing a foundation of genuine barakah and eternal peace.",
  "279": "The historic declaration of divine war against institutional exploitation and the mandate of principal preservation are ordained in 'If you do not, then beware of a war with Allah and His Messenger! But if you repent, you may retain your principal—neither inflicting nor suffering harm.'\n\nThis is the only sin in the entire Quran for which God and His Messenger declare an open state of war: 'If you do not, then beware of a war with Allah and His Messenger!' ('fa'dhanu bi-harbin mina Allahi wa rasulih'). The sheer gravity of this declaration reveals that usury is not merely an individual moral failing, but a catastrophic systemic crime that dismantles social peace, enslaves the vulnerable, and wages war against human welfare.\n\nThe verse outlines the path of financial repentance: 'But if you repent, you may retain your principal' ('wa in tubtum fa-lakum ru'usu amwalikum'). The lender is legally entitled to recover the original principal sum lent, without any interest added.\n\nThe Quran articulates the golden universal maxim of economic justice: 'neither inflicting nor suffering harm' ('la tazlimuna wa la tuzlamun'). The lender must not inflict injustice ('la tazlimun') by demanding interest from the borrower, nor should the lender suffer injustice ('la tuzlamun') by having their original capital diminished or withheld.\n\nIn his historic Farewell Sermon, the Prophet Muhammad announced the permanent abolition of all usury, declaring all tribal interest null and void, beginning with that of his own uncle, Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib.\n\nThis verse establishes the foundational Islamic standard of absolute fairness and mutual equity in all financial contracts.\n\nUpholding equity and mutual protection in economic transactions reflects the core justice of Islam, ensuring that financial relationships are built upon mutual fairness and respect. This divine declaration against usury establishes that economic justice is sacred, compelling believers to construct fair financial systems based on mutual risk, transparency, and human flourishing.",
  "280": "The sublime ethics of debt relief, mercy, and charitable cancellation are commanded in 'If it is difficult for someone to repay a debt, postpone it until a time of ease. And if you waive it as an act of charity, it will be better for you, if only you knew.'\n\nHaving established the right of the lender to recover the principal in the previous verse, the Quran now addresses the situation where the borrower is genuinely destitute and unable to pay: 'If it is difficult for someone to repay a debt' ('wa in kana dhu 'usratin').\n\nIn pre-Islamic times, when a debtor could not pay, the lender would say: 'Either pay up now or the debt doubles with interest!'—trapping the debtor in lifelong slavery. The Quran instituted a radically compassionate legal obligation: 'postpone it until a time of ease' ('fa-naziratun ila maysarah'). The creditor is legally and morally required to grant an extension of time without charging a single penny of penalty or interest.\n\nThe Quran then invites the creditor to the highest station of spiritual grace: 'And if you waive it as an act of charity, it will be better for you, if only you knew' ('wa an tasaddaqu khayrun lakum in kuntum ta'lamun'). Forgiving the debt entirely as an act of charity ('sadaqah') relieves a human being from crushing anxiety and earns the supreme reward of God.\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sahih Muslim, the Prophet Muhammad declared: 'Whoever grants respite to a poor debtor, or waives their debt, Allah will shade them beneath His Throne on the Day when there is no shade but His.'\n\nThis passage showcases the unmatched compassion of Islamic economic ethics, prioritizing human dignity above material wealth.\n\nGranting respite and forgiving debts transforms financial hardship into an occasion for divine mercy, earning the creditor the supreme honor of being shaded beneath the Throne of God.",
  "281": "The final revealed verse of the Quran and the ultimate reminder of accountability are proclaimed in 'Be mindful of the Day when you will ˹all˺ be returned to Allah, then every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged.'\n\nAccording to the most authentic narrations reported by Ibn Abbas, al-Suddi, and Ibn Kathir in Sahih al-Bukhari, this was the very last verse of the entire Quran revealed to the Prophet Muhammad, revealed just nine days before his passing. It serves as the ultimate, crowning farewell reminder for all humanity.\n\nThe verse commands urgent, conscious mindfulness: 'Be mindful of the Day when you will ˹all˺ be returned to Allah' ('Wa-ittaqu yawman turja'una fihi ila Allah'). It calls every human heart to live with continuous awareness of the impending Day of Judgment, when all earthly journeys will conclude and every soul will stand before the Creator.\n\nThe verse affirms absolute divine justice: 'then every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged' ('thumma tuwaffa kullu nafsin ma kasabat wa hum la yuzlamun'). Every secret intention, every charitable gift, every act of patience, and every hidden sin will be recompensed with flawless equity, without the slightest injustice.\n\nEnding the revelation with this verse is full of profound wisdom: it anchors every commandment, prohibition, financial transaction, and personal relationship in the reality of eternal accountability.\n\nThis verse calls every believer to live with an awake conscience, preparing each day for the ultimate, peaceful return to the Almighty.\n\nLiving with the constant awareness of our final return to God transforms our priorities, ensuring that our daily choices, speech, and dealings are governed by absolute moral integrity and justice. Preparing for the Day of Reckoning with sincere repentance and righteous action brings unshakeable peace of mind, ensuring that we meet our Lord with clear consciences and joyous hearts.",
  "282": "The longest verse in the Quran, known as Ayat al-Dayn (the Verse of Debt), establishes comprehensive commercial law and financial documentation in 'O believers! When you contract a loan for a fixed period of time, commit it to writing. Let the scribe maintain justice between the parties. The scribe should not refuse to write as Allah has taught them to write. They will write what the debtor dictates, bearing Allah in mind and not defrauding the debt. If the debtor is incompetent, weak, or unable to dictate, let their guardian dictate for them with justice. Call upon two of your men to witness. If two men cannot be found, then one man and two women of your choice will witness—so if one of the women forgets the other may remind her. The witnesses must not refuse when they are summoned. You must not be against writing ˹contracts˺ for a fixed period—whether the sum is small or great. This is more just ˹for you˺ in the sight of Allah, and more convenient to establish evidence and remove doubts. However, if you conduct an immediate transaction among yourselves, then there is no need for you to record it, but call upon witnesses when a deal is finalized. Let no harm come to the scribe or witnesses. If you do, then you have gravely exceeded ˹your limits˺. Be mindful of Allah, for Allah ˹is the One Who˺ teaches you. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.'\n\nThis monumental verse provides an extraordinary legal framework for financial transactions:\nFirst, mandatory documentation: all future loans must be recorded in writing by a neutral, just scribe, with the debtor dictating the exact terms to prevent exploitation;\nSecond, protecting the vulnerable: if the debtor is mentally frail or a minor, their legal guardian dictates with justice;\nThird, formal witnessing: requiring two reliable male witnesses, or one male and two females in commercial matters where financial experience was specialized;\nFourth, protecting professionals: strictly forbidding harassment of scribes and witnesses.\n\nThe Quran explains the wisdom: writing contracts removes doubts, preserves evidence, and prevents disputes.\n\nThe verse closes: 'Be mindful of Allah, for Allah ˹is the One Who˺ teaches you. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things,' establishing that sound commercial law is a divine gift that preserves social trust.",
  "283": "The regulations for financial pledges during travel and the sacred trust of testimony are ordained in 'If you are on a journey and a scribe cannot be found, then a security can be taken. If you trust one another, then ˹there is no need for a security, but˺ the debtor should honour this trust ˹by repaying the debt˺—and let them fear Allah, their Lord. And do not conceal the testimony, for whoever conceals it, their hearts are indeed sinful. And Allah ˹fully˺ knows what you do.'\n\nThe verse provides practical legal alternatives when standard written documentation is impossible during travel: 'If you are on a journey and a scribe cannot be found, then a security can be taken' ('rihanun maqbudah'). The creditor is permitted to hold a physical collateral or security ('rahn') to secure the loan until repayment.\n\nHowever, the Quran highlights the higher station of mutual trust: 'If you trust one another, then the debtor should honour this trust ˹by repaying the debt˺' ('fa-l-yu'addi alladhi i'tumina amanatahu'). A debtor entrusted without collateral must demonstrate fear of God by repaying the loan promptly and honestly, without delay or evasion.\n\nThe verse delivers an uncompromising command regarding legal integrity: 'And do not conceal the testimony, for whoever conceals it, their hearts are indeed sinful' ('wa la taktumu al-shahadata wa man yaktumha fa-innahu athimun qalbuh'). Concealing legal testimony when summoned destroys justice and harms innocent people. The Quran specifically attributes the sin to the 'heart' ('qalb') because concealing truth is a deliberate, internal decision of malice and cowardice.\n\nThe verse closes: 'And Allah ˹fully˺ knows what you do,' reminding all witnesses and debtors that God witnesses every hidden truth and ensures ultimate justice.\n\nFulfilling financial trusts and upholding truthful testimony preserves social stability, ensuring that our dealings are anchored in mutual integrity and conscious fear of the All-Knowing Creator.",
  "284": "The supreme sovereignty of God and the accountability of internal thoughts are declared in 'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Whether you reveal what is in your hearts or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it. He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills. And Allah is Most Capable of everything.'\n\nThe verse opens by affirming universal divine ownership: 'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.'\n\nThe Quran then announces absolute internal surveillance: 'Whether you reveal what is in your hearts or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it' ('wa in tubdu ma fi anfusikum aw tukhfuhu yuhasibkum bihi Allah'). In an authentic narration in Sahih Muslim, when this verse was revealed, the Companions fell to their knees in deep distress, fearing that passing whispers and involuntary thoughts would bring punishment. The Prophet Muhammad comforted them, instructing them to say: 'We hear and we obey.' When they submitted with complete humility, God revealed the subsequent verses, clarifying that accountability applies to conscious, settled intentions and willful malice, while involuntary passing thoughts are forgiven.\n\nThe verse affirms divine sovereignty: 'He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills' based on His infinite wisdom and justice.\n\nThe verse concludes: 'And Allah is Most Capable of everything' ('wa Allahu 'ala kulli shay'in Qadir').\n\nThis passage teaches that spiritual purification requires guarding the inner heart from settled malice, jealousy, and arrogance, anchoring the soul in sincere mindfulness of the All-Seeing Creator.\n\nGuarding the inner heart from secret malice and arrogance brings true spiritual peace, ensuring that our private thoughts and public deeds remain fully aligned with divine truth and justice. Sincere internal purification liberates the human heart from the toxic traps of hypocrisy, ensuring that our thoughts, intentions, and deeds remain completely aligned with truth and justice.",
  "285": "The sublime declaration of apostolic faith, universal monotheistic brotherhood, and humble submission is proclaimed in 'The Messenger ˹firmly˺ believes in what has been revealed to him from his Lord, and so do the believers. They ˹all˺ believe in Allah, His angels, His Books, and His messengers. ˹They proclaim,˺ “We make no distinction between any of His messengers.” And they say, “We hear and obey. ˹We seek˺ Your forgiveness, our Lord! And to You ˹alone˺ is the final return.”'\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari, the Prophet Muhammad declared: 'Whoever recites the last two verses of Surah al-Baqarah at night, they will be sufficient for him'—providing total spiritual protection and peace.\n\nThe verse honors the Prophet and the faithful: 'The Messenger ˹firmly˺ believes in what has been revealed to him from his Lord, and so do the believers.'\n\nTheir comprehensive creed is articulated across four pillars: believing in God, His angels, His Books, and His messengers. They declare universal prophetic unity: 'We make no distinction between any of His messengers' ('la nufarriqu bayna ahadin min rusulih'), honoring all legitimate prophets sent throughout human history.\n\nTheir core spiritual posture is absolute surrender: 'And they say, “We hear and obey”' ('Samina wa ata'na'). Unlike past rebellious nations who said 'we hear and disobey,' the Muslim community responds to divine commands with immediate, willing obedience.\n\nThey conclude with profound humility: '˹We seek˺ Your forgiveness, our Lord! And to You ˹alone˺ is the final return' ('Ghufranaka Rabbana wa ilayka al-masir').\n\nThis verse reflects the pinnacle of spiritual maturity: pairing active obedience with humble pleas for divine forgiveness.\n\nEmbodying apostolic faith and humble obedience elevates the human spirit, uniting the global community of believers in the joyful service and perpetual worship of the one true Lord. Responding to divine commands with immediate, wholehearted obedience is the hallmark of true faith, uniting the community of believers in continuous gratitude and sincere devotion.",
  "286": "The triumphant conclusion of Surah al-Baqarah, the divine guarantee of human capacity, and the ultimate supplications of the faithful are proclaimed in 'Allah does not require of any soul more than what it can afford. All good will be for its own benefit, and all evil will be to its own loss. ˹The believers pray,˺ “Our Lord! Do not punish us if we forget or make a mistake. Our Lord! Do not place a burden on us like the one you placed on those before us. Our Lord! Do not burden us with what we cannot bear. Pardon us, forgive us, and have mercy on us. You are our ˹only˺ Guardian. So grant us victory over the disbelieving people.”'\n\nThe verse articulates the foundational legal and theological maxim of Islam: 'Allah does not require of any soul more than what it can afford' ('La yukallifu Allahu nafsan illa wus'aha'). God's laws are never designed to crush human beings; every command and prohibition is tailored perfectly within human capacity.\n\nThe verse affirms personal moral responsibility: 'All good will be for its own benefit, and all evil will be to its own loss.'\n\nThe chapter culminates with the seven moving supplications of the believers:\n1. 'Our Lord! Do not punish us if we forget or make a mistake'—seeking pardon for involuntary errors.\n2. 'Our Lord! Do not place a burden on us like the one you placed on those before us'—praying for ease.\n3. 'Our Lord! Do not burden us with what we cannot bear.'\n4. 'Pardon us' ('wa'fu 'anna')—erasing past shortcomings.\n5. 'forgive us' ('waghfir lana')—concealing sins.\n6. 'and have mercy on us' ('warhamna')—enveloping us in grace.\n7. 'You are our ˹only˺ Guardian. So grant us victory over the disbelieving people.'\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sahih Muslim, God replied to each of these prayers: 'I have done so!' This brings Surah al-Baqarah to a triumphant, peaceful close."
};
